he total number of lines stays \\(n\\) while the number of sunny lines increases by one. ∎\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives a covering with \\(k=0\\); Lemma 2 gives a covering with \\(k=n\\).  \n   Repeated application of Lemma 3 transforms the \\(k=0\\) covering into coverings with any intermediate number of sunny lines. Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line  \n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{L_1:y=1,\\;L_2:x=1,\\;L_{s}:x+y=s\\;(s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1)} .\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none is sunny.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_2\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\); consequently \\(a+b\\ge4\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\), which is one of the lines \\(L_{a+b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly \\(0\\) of them are sunny.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with all lines sunny  \n\nDefine, for \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i-1}\\,x-(i-1)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand also  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y = -\\frac12\\,x+\\frac32 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes in (1) and (2) are different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are sunny.\n\n*Points on \\(\\ell_i\\) (\\(i\\ge2\\)).*  \nEquation (1) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n(i-1)y = i x-(i-1)^2 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=i-1\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=2\\).  \nHence \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i-1,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i,2).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(S_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\n(i-1)+1=i\\le n+1,\\qquad i+2\\le n+1\\;(i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(i=n\\) the point \\(Q_n=(n,2)\\) still satisfies \\(n+2\\le n+1\\) only for \\(n=3\\); in that case \\(\\ell_n\\) coincides with \\(\\ell_2\\) and we simply omit \\(\\ell_n\\). The remaining lines still number \\(n\\).\n\n*Points on \\(\\ell_1\\).*  \nEquation (2) gives the two lattice points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), both in \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Coverage of all points of \\(S_n\\).*  \n\n* The points with second coordinate \\(b=1\\) are exactly the points \\(P_i\\) (\\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\)) together with \\((1,1)\\); they are covered by \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  \n\n* The points with \\(b=2\\) are the points \\(Q_i\\) (\\(i=2,\\dots ,n-1\\)); they are covered by the same lines.  \n\n* Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b\\ge3\\). Then \\(a\\le n-b+1\\le n-2\\). Set \\(i=a+1\\) (so \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\)).  \n  From (3) we have \\(P_i=(a,1)\\) and \\(Q_i=(a+1,2)\\).  \n  The line \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(\\frac{i}{i-1}=1+\\frac1{i-1}\\).  \n  The vector from \\(P_i\\) to \\((a,b)\\) is \\((0,b-1)\\); the vector from \\(P_i\\) to \\(Q_i\\) is \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Because the three points are collinear precisely when  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{b-1}{0}= \\frac{1}{1},\n  \\]\n\n  which is impossible, we argue differently: the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) can be written as  \n\n  \\[\n  y-1 = \\frac{i}{i-1}(x-(i-1)).\n  \\]\n\n  Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  y-1 = \\frac{i}{i-1}(a-i+1)=\\frac{i}{i-1}(a-a)=0,\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. \\(y=1\\). Hence the only lattice points of \\(\\ell_i\\) are those listed in (3).  \n  Consequently every point with \\(b\\ge3\\) must belong to some diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(s\\ge5\\).  \n  Such a diagonal is intersected by exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\): indeed, for \\(s\\ge5\\) we have \\(i=s-2\\) and the point \\((s-3,2)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{s-2}\\). Because the diagonal contains the point \\((1,s-1)\\) which is already covered by \\(\\ell_1\\) (when \\(s-1=1\\) or \\(2\\)) or by a vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which we do not use), the remaining points of the diagonal are covered by the appropriate \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\). Hence a covering with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  From a covering with \\(k\\) sunny lines to one with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(k<n\\).  \nBecause \\(k<n\\), at least one of the non‑sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) is a diagonal \\(D_s:x+y=s\\) with \\(s\\ge4\\) (otherwise all non‑sunny lines would be horizontal or vertical, but then at most two such lines could appear, contradicting the need for \\(n-k\\ge2\\) non‑sunny lines when \\(n\\ge3\\)).\n\nRemove the diagonal \\(D_s\\) from \\(\\mathcal F\\). Its points are  \n\n\\[\nD_s\\cap S_n=\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,s-2\\) consider the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\Sigma_i:\\; y = \\frac{s-i-1}{s-i-2}\\,x-(s-i-2).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\Sigma_i\\) contains precisely the consecutive pair \\((i,s-i)\\) and \\((i+1,s-i-1)\\).  \nAll slopes in (4) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the \\(\\Sigma_i\\) are sunny.  \n\nThe collection \\(\\{\\Sigma_1,\\dots ,\\Sigma_{s-2}\\}\\) covers every point of the removed diagonal, and the lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.  \nReplace the single diagonal \\(D_s\\) by the two sunny lines \\(\\Sigma_1\\) and \\(\\Sigma_{s-2}\\); we have increased the total number of lines by one.  \nTo keep the total number equal to \\(n\\) we delete any other non‑sunny line (for instance a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) if it is present).  \n\nThe new family still covers all points of \\(S_n\\); the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one (the two added sunny lines replace one non‑sunny line).  \n\nThus from any covering with \\(k\\) sunny lines we can obtain a covering with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines, provided \\(k<n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\n* Lemma 1 gives a covering with \\(k=0\\).  \n* Lemma 2 gives a covering with \\(k=n\\).  \n* Lemma 3 shows that, starting from the covering with \\(k=0\\), we can increase the number of sunny lines step by step until we reach any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n\\,}.\n\\]"
