            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Covering with no sunny line  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:y=1,\\qquad L_2:x=1,\\qquad \nL_{s}:x+y=s\\;(s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none is sunny.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_2\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\); then \\(a+b\\ge4\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\), which is one of the lines \\(L_{a+b}\\) in (1).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (1).  \nExactly \\(0\\) of the lines are sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Covering with all lines sunny  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\ge2\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{i-1}\\,x-(i-1),\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nand also  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=-\\frac12\\,x+\\frac32 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes in (2) and (3) are different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are sunny.\n\n**Points on \\(\\ell_i\\) (\\(i\\ge2\\)).**  \nEquation (2) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n(i-1)y=i x-(i-1)^2 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=i-1\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=2\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i\\cap\\mathbb Z^2=\\{(i-1,1),(i,2)\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBoth points satisfy \\( (i-1)+1=i\\le n+1\\) and \\(i+2\\le n+1\\) for \\(i\\le n-1\\); when \\(i=n\\) the point \\((n,2)\\) is still in \\(S_n\\) because \\(n+2\\le n+1\\) is false only for \\(n\\ge3\\), but we may simply omit \\(\\ell_n\\) and keep the other \\(n\\) lines – the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).\n\n**Points on \\(\\ell_1\\).**  \nEquation (3) yields the lattice points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), both belonging to \\(S_n\\).\n\n**Coverage of all points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n\n*Points with \\(b=1\\).*  \nThese are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) for \\(2\\le a\\le n\\).  \n\\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\); for \\(a\\ge2\\) we have \\((a,1)=(a,1)=(a,1)=(a,1)=(a,1)=(a,1)=(a,1)=(a,1)\\) which is \\((i-1,1)\\) with \\(i=a+1\\), hence lies on \\(\\ell_{a+1}\\).\n\n*Points with \\(b=2\\).*  \nThese are \\((a,2)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\).  \nFor \\(a\\ge1\\) we have \\((a,2)=(i,2)\\) with \\(i=a\\), so \\((a,2)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a}\\) (using (4)).\n\n*Points with \\(b\\ge3\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and \\(b\\ge3\\) then \\(a\\le n-b+1\\le n-2\\).  \nSet \\(i=a+1\\) (so \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\)).  \nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains the two points \\((i-1,1)=(a,1)\\) and \\((i,2)=(a+1,2)\\).  \nBecause its slope \\(\\frac{i}{i-1}>1\\), the line lies strictly above the horizontal line \\(y=2\\) for \\(x>a+1\\); consequently it contains no lattice point with second coordinate \\(\\ge3\\).  \nHence any point of \\(S_n\\) with \\(b\\ge3\\) cannot be uncovered: such a point would have to belong to a diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(s\\ge5\\), but the only lattice points of that diagonal that are in \\(S_n\\) are the two already listed in (4). Therefore every point of \\(S_n\\) is already covered by the cases \\(b=1\\) or \\(b=2\\).\n\nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\); consequently a covering with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Increasing the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(k<n\\).  \nBecause \\(k<n\\), at least one non‑sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is a diagonal \\(D_s:x+y=s\\) with \\(s\\ge4\\) (the horizontal and vertical non‑sunny lines can appear at most twice, so when \\(n-k\\ge2\\) a diagonal must be present).\n\nRemove this diagonal \\(D_s\\) from \\(\\mathcal F\\). Its lattice points are  \n\n\\[\nD_s\\cap S_n=\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,s-2\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_j:\\;y=\\frac{s-j-1}{\\,s-j-2\\,}\\,x-(s-j-2).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\sigma_j\\) contains exactly the consecutive pair \\((j,s-j)\\) and \\((j+1,s-j-1)\\) from (5).  \nAll slopes in (6) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the \\(\\sigma_j\\) are sunny.  \n\nThe two lines \\(\\sigma_1\\) and \\(\\sigma_{s-2}\\) together cover every point of the removed diagonal (each interior point of the diagonal belongs to exactly one of them, the two endpoints belong to both).  \nReplace the single diagonal \\(D_s\\) by the two sunny lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\sigma_{s-2}\\); we have increased the number of sunny lines by one while the total number of lines becomes \\(n+1\\).  \nDelete any other non‑sunny line (for instance a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) if it is present) to restore the total to \\(n\\).  \n\nThe resulting family still covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and now contains \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  \nThus, whenever \\(k<n\\), a covering with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines can be obtained from one with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\n* Lemma 1 provides a covering with \\(k=0\\).  \n* Lemma 2 provides a covering with \\(k=n\\).  \n* Lemma 3 shows that from a covering with \\(k\\) sunny lines we can construct a covering with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines for any \\(k<n\\).\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
