nts such as \\((1,2)\\). Hence the construction for \\(k=n\\) fails.\n\nSince the existence of a covering with \\(k=n\\) is not established, the later inductive argument cannot start from the claimed extremal case.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Increasing the number of sunny lines (Lemma\u202f3)\n\nEven if we ignore the failure of Lemma\u202f2, we must examine Lemma\u202f3.\n\n**Step:** Assume a covering \\(\\mathcal F\\) with \\(k<n\\) sunny lines. The solution asserts that because \\(k<n\\), at least one non\u2011sunny line is a diagonal \\(D_s:x+y=s\\) with \\(s\\ge4\\).\n\n*Justification Gap:*  \nThe claim \u201chorizontal and vertical non\u2011sunny lines can appear at most twice\u201d is not proved. It is conceivable (though unlikely) that a covering uses many horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) with different \\(c\\) (all non\u2011sunny) while still covering all points. Without a proof, the existence of a diagonal to replace is not guaranteed.\n\n**Step:** For a chosen diagonal \\(D_s\\) the solution defines, for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,s-2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_j:\\; y=\\frac{s-j-1}{\\,s-j-2\\,}x-(s-j-2).\n\\]\n\n*Justification Gap:*  \nIt is asserted that \\(\\sigma_j\\) contains exactly the consecutive pair \\((j,s-j)\\) and \\((j+1,s-j-1)\\). A direct substitution shows:\n\n- Plug \\(x=j\\) into \\(\\sigma_j\\):  \n  \\(y=\\frac{s-j-1}{s-j-2}j-(s-j-2)=\\frac{j(s-j-1)-(s-j-2)^2}{s-j-2}\\).  \n  Simplifying does **not** obviously yield \\(s-j\\). The solution provides no algebraic verification, so the claim is unsubstantiated.\n\n- Moreover, the slope \\(\\frac{s-j-1}{s-j-2}\\) could equal \\(-1\\) when \\(s-j-1 = -(s-j-2)\\), i.e. when \\(2(s-j-1)= -1\\), which never occurs for integer \\(s,j\\). However, the possibility of the slope being \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) is also not ruled out (it would require \\(s-j-2=0\\) which is excluded by the range). Still, a rigorous check is missing.\n\nBecause the key property of \\(\\sigma_j\\) (covering exactly two consecutive points of the diagonal) is not demonstrated, the replacement step is not justified.\n\n**Step:** \u201cThe two lines \\(\\sigma_1\\) and \\(\\sigma_{s-2}\\) together cover every point of the removed diagonal (each interior point belongs to exactly one of them, the two endpoints belong to both).\u201d\n\n*Justification Gap:*  \nEven if the previous claim were true, the argument that *only* \\(\\sigma_1\\) and \\(\\sigma_{s-2}\\) are needed to cover the whole diagonal is unexplained. The diagonal contains \\(s-1\\) points; the construction provides \\(s-2\\) lines \\(\\sigma_j\\). Selecting only the first and last of them leaves many interior points uncovered unless each \\(\\sigma_j\\) overlaps with the next, which is not shown.\n\n**Step:** \u201cDelete any other non\u2011sunny line (for instance a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) if it is present) to restore the total to \\(n\\).\u201d\n\n*Justification Gap:*  \nRemoving an arbitrary non\u2011sunny line may eliminate coverage of points that are not on the replaced diagonal. No argument is given that the remaining lines still cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus Lemma\u202f3 contains multiple gaps; even if Lemma\u202f2 were correct, the inductive step is not rigorously established.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Completion of the proof\n\nThe final conclusion relies on Lemma\u202f1 (valid), Lemma\u202f2 (invalid due to critical error), and Lemma\u202f3 (contains justification gaps). Because Lemma\u202f2 fails, the claim that \\(k=n\\) is attainable is false, and the inductive argument cannot be initiated. Consequently the statement that **all** integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are possible is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\nThe solution correctly handles the case \\(k=0\\) but contains a **critical algebraic error** in the construction for \\(k=n\\) (incorrect lattice points on the proposed sunny lines). Moreover, the subsequent inductive lemma is insufficiently justified. Therefore the solution does **not** prove the claimed resu