a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge3\\) lies on the same diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) as \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a+1,2)\\), and that diagonal is intersected by \\(\\ell_i\\) at \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a+1,2)\\). Since the diagonal contains no other lattice points of \\(S_n\\) (its interior points have sum \\(>n+1\\)), the point \\((a,b)\\) must already be one of the two listed points, which is impossible. Hence such a point cannot exist; therefore all points of \\(S_n\\) are already covered by the cases \\(b=1,2\\).  \n   Thus a covering with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n4. **Lemma 3 (increasing the number of sunny lines).**  \n   Suppose a covering \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(k<n\\).  \n   Because \\(k<n\\), at least one non‑sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is a diagonal \\(D_s:x+y=s\\) with \\(s\\ge4\\).  \n   Remove this diagonal and replace it by the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_1:\\;y=\\frac{s-2}{s-3}\\,x-(s-3),\\qquad \n   \\sigma_2:\\;y=\\frac{s-1}{s-2}\\,x-(s-2).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\sigma_j\\) contains two consecutive points of the removed diagonal, so together they cover all points of \\(D_s\\). Their slopes are neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence they are sunny.  \n   The total number of lines is restored to \\(n\\) (we added two and removed one, then delete any other non‑sunny line if necessary), and the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one.  \n   Therefore a covering with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives a covering with \\(k=0\\); Lemma 2 gives a covering with \\(k=n\\).  \n   Repeated application of Lemma 3 transforms the \\(k=0\\) covering into coverings with any intermediate number of sunny lines. Consequently for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n