uently each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\nThe lattice points of \\(\\ell_i\\) that belong to \\(S_n\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i\\cap S_n=\\{(i+j,\\,1+j)\\mid j=0,1,\\dots ,\\,n-i\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_i\\) contains the whole “south‑west to north‑east’’ chain that\nstarts at \\((i,1)\\) and proceeds with step \\((1,1)\\) until the sum of the\ncoordinates reaches \\(n+1\\).\n\n*Additional non‑sunny lines.*  \nWe already have \\(1+k\\) lines.  To reach a total of \\(n\\) lines we add\n\\(n-1-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Verification of the covering  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, the point lies on the diagonal \\(D\\) (5).\n\n*Otherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\).*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\ni:=a-b+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge1\\) we have \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)*, then \\(b=a-i+1\\) and \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the\nsunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) (6).\n\n*If \\(i>k\\)*, then \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  Since \\(i\\le n-1\\) we have\n\\(a\\le n-1\\).  Consequently \\(a\\) is one of the integers\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k\\); the point \\((a,b)\\) therefore lies on the vertical\nline \\(V_a\\) (8).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by at least one of the lines\n\\[\n\\{D\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-1-k}\\},\n\\]\nwhich are exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  By construction exactly the\n\\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny.\n\nHence for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a required family of\n\\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Conclusion  \n\n* From Lemma 2 and Corollary 3 we proved that a covering of the set\n\\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines must contain at least one non‑sunny line; therefore\n\\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n* The explicit construction above shows that for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) such a covering can be built.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1 } .\n\\]"
