            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nThe counter‑example shows that the previous claim “all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable’’ is false.  \nThe correct set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,\\;1,\\;2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\, } .\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; for any larger \\(k\\) such a family cannot exist.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   N=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Lemma 2.* A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal \\(x+y=s\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   Using these lemmas, if a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines, then  \n\n   \\[\n   2k+(n-k)n\\ge N\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   k\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence no covering can have more than \\(\