on‑sunny lines.  \n   As shown above one of them must be the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\); the other must be the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (otherwise the column \\(x=1\\) could not be covered).  \n   After removing these two lines, the still uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=S_n\\setminus\\bigl(\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}\\bigr),\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The row \\(y=1\\) contains the points \\((a,1)\\;(2\\le a\\le n-1)\\); each of the remaining \\(n-2\\) sunny lines can meet this row in at most one point, so every such point must lie on a different sunny line.  \n   Consequently each sunny line is forced to pass through a distinct point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n-1\\).\n\n   Consider now the row \\(y=2\\).  \n   Apart from the points already covered by the two non‑sunny lines, the points that still have to be covered are \\((a,2)\\;(2\\le a\\le n-2)\\) – there are \\(n-3\\) of them.  \n   A line through \\((a,1)\\) meets the row \\(y=2\\) at the point \\((a+1,2)\\) **iff** its slope equals \\(1\\); for any other admissible slope the intersection with the line \\(y=2\\) has a non‑integral \\(x\\)-coordinate and therefore does not belong to \\(S_n\\).  \n   Hence the only way a sunny line can cover a point of the form \\((a,2)\\) is to have slope \\(1\\) and to be the line  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x-(a-1)\\qquad\\text{through }(a,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Such a line hits the point \\((a+1,2)\\).  \n   As \\(a\\) runs through \\(2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\) the covered points are \\((3,2),(4,2),\\dots ,(n,2)\\); the point \\((2,2)\\) is never obtained.  \n   Therefore the set \\(T_n\\) cannot be covered with only \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n   Consequently at least **three** non‑sunny lines are necessary, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-3\\qquad (n\\ge 4).\n   \\]\n\n5. **Realising every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).**  \n   *Induction basis* \\(n=4\\).  \n   The following four distinct lines cover \\(S_4\\) and contain exactly one sunny line:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell_1:\\;x+y=5 \\quad\\text{(non‑sunny)},\\\\\n   &\\ell_2:\\;x=1 \\quad\\text{(non‑sunny)},\\\\\n   &\\ell_3:\\;x=2 \\quad\\text{(non‑sunny)},\\\\\n   &\\ell_4:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac73 \\quad\\text{(sunny)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The three non‑sunny lines cover the diagonal, the whole first column and the points \\((2,1),(2,2)\\); the sunny line passes through \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\). Hence \\(k=1=n-3\\) is attainable for \\(n=4\\).  \n   The case \\(k=0\\) is trivial (use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\)).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge4\\) we have a family \\(\\mathcal F_{m}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{m}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k\\le m-3\\).  \n   For \\(n=m+1\\) construct  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n}= \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1,\\;x=1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\,\\ell+ (1,0)\\mid \\ell\\in\\mathcal F_{m}\\bigr\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. we translate every line of \\(\\mathcal F_{m}\\) one unit to the right and add the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) and the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   The translation sends the set \\(S_{m}\\) onto the subset \\(\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid a\\ge2\\}\\); together with the two newly added lines this covers the whole \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   The number of sunny lines does not change, so \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Because \\(k\\le m-3 = (n-1)-3 = n-4\\), we obtain configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-4\\).  \n   Adding the configuration for \\(k=n-3\\) given by the basis \\(n=4\\) (and its translates) yields all values up to \\(n-3\\).\n\n6. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) can be covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=2x,\\qquad y=\\tfrac12x ,\n   \\]\n\n   showing that \\(k=3\\) is possible.  \n   Using the constructions of step 5 with \\(k=0,1,2\\) we obtain the remaining values. Hence for \\(n=3\\) all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are attainable.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 4 shows that for \\(n\\ge4\\) at least three non‑sunny lines are necessary, i.e. \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \n   The inductive construction proves that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) can indeed be realised.  \n   Together with the special analysis for \\(n=3\\) we obtain the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\mathcal K_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
