r the interior points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence a second non‑sunny line is unavoidable; we may take it to be  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_V:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge4\\) at least two non‑sunny lines are required.\n\n#### Lemma 2 (three for \\(n=4\\))\n\nFor \\(n=4\\) the set still uncovered after \\(\\ell_D\\) and \\(\\ell_V\\) is  \n\n\\[\nT_4=S_4\\setminus(\\ell_D\\cup\\ell_V)=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can intersect the column \\(x=2\\) in at most one point, so two sunny lines can cover at most two of the three points \\((2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\\).  Consequently a third non‑sunny line is necessary.  Hence for \\(n=4\\) at least three non‑sunny lines are needed.\n\n#### Lemma 3 (two are enough for \\(n\\ge5\\))\n\nWe prove by induction that for every \\(n\\ge5\\) the set \\(S_n\\) can be covered by exactly two non‑sunny lines, namely \\(\\ell_D:x+y=n+1\\) and \\(\\ell_V:x=1\\), together with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base \\(n=5\\).*  The five lines displayed in the summary (two non‑sunny, three sunny) cover all fifteen points of \\(S_5\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume a family \\(\\mathcal F_m\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_m\\) and contains precisely the two non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_D^{(m)}:x+y=m+1\\) and \\(\\ell_V^{(m)}:x=1\\).  \nDefine for \\(n=m+1\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n}= \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1,\\;x=1\\,\\bigr\\}\n                \\cup\\bigl\\{\\,\\ell+(1,0)\\mid \\ell\\in\\mathcal F_m\\setminus\\{\\ell_D^{(m)},\\ell_V^{(m)}\\}\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe translation \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x+1,y)\\) maps \\(S_m\\) bijectively onto \\(\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge2\\}\\).  Hence the translated sunny lines together with the two new non‑sunny lines cover the whole \\(S_n\\).  Translation does not change slopes, so the number of sunny lines remains \\((m-2) = (n-1)-2 = n-2\\).  Distinctness is preserved, completing the induction.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge5\\) the minimum possible number of non‑sunny lines equals 2, i.e. the maximum possible number of sunny lines equals \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  From the maximum to all smaller values\n\nIf a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, we can obtain a configuration with \\(k-1\\) sunny lines by replacing one sunny line by a vertical (or horizontal) line that passes through a point already covered by the removed line.  This operation does not destroy the covering property and reduces the number of sunny lines by one.  Repeating the operation yields configurations for every integer between \\(0\\) and the maximal value.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Determination of the admissible set \\(\\mathcal K_n\\)\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).**  The three points of the diagonal are covered by the line \\(x+y=4\\) (non‑sunny).  The remaining three points are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32 .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence \\(k_{\\max}=3\\).  By the reduction argument all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) occur.\n\n* **\\(n=4\\).**  Lemma 2 forces at least three non‑sunny lines, so \\(k_{\\max}=1\\).  \n  Examples:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &k=0:\\; \\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3,\\;x=4\\},\\\\\n  &k=1:\\; \\{x+y=5,\\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac73\\}.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(\\mathcal K_4=\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(n\\ge5\\).**  Lemma 3 gives \\(k_{\\max}=n-2\\).  By Lemma 4 every integer \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\mathcal K_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\},      & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correc