unny line\n\nAll points of \\(D_n\\) lie on  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_D:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore \\(\\ell_D\\) is non‑sunny.  \nSince a sunny line can intersect \\(D_n\\) in at most one point, every admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain \\(\\ell_D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A second compulsory non‑sunny line for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nThe column \\(C_n\\) contains the points \\((1,1),\\dots,(1,n)\\).  \nThe point \\((1,n)\\) belongs to \\(D_n\\) and is already covered by \\(\\ell_D\\).  \nFor each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_n\\) a line intersecting the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is needed, and a sunny line can meet \\(x=1\\) in at most one point.  \nHence a vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_V:\\;x=1\n\\]\n\nmust also belong to any admissible family when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Minimal number of non‑sunny lines\n\n#### Lemma 1 ( \\(n=3\\) ).\nFor \\(n=3\\) at least two non‑sunny lines are necessary.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\n\\(D_3=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) forces \\(\\ell_D\\).  \nThe remaining points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) lie in the column \\(x=1\\); a single vertical line \\(x=1\\) covers the two points \\((1,1),(1,2)\\).  \nThus at least two non‑sunny lines (\\(\\ell_D\\) and \\(x=1\\)) are required. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 ( \\(n=4\\) ).\nFor \\(n=4\\) at least three non‑sunny lines are necessary.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAfter including \\(\\ell_D\\) and \\(\\ell_V\\) the uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nT_4=S_4\\setminus(\\ell_D\\cup\\ell_V)=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line that contains two of these three points is either horizontal (\\(y=1\\)), vertical (\\(x=2\\)), or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are non‑sunny.  \nConsequently at least one additional non‑sunny line is needed, giving a total