nny lines is\n\n\\[\nk_{\\max}=n-\\text{(minimal non‑sunny)}=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,& n=3,4,\\\\\nn-2,& n\\ge5.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining all smaller numbers of sunny lines\n\n#### Lemma 4.\nIf a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, then for every integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le k\\) there exists a configuration with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) of the configuration and a point \\(P\\) on \\(\\ell\\).  \nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by the vertical line \\(x=\\text{(the }x\\text{-coordinate of }P)\\) (or by the horizontal line \\(y=\\text{(the }y\\text{-coordinate of }P)\\)).  \nThe new line is non‑sunny, passes through \\(P\\), and therefore still covers all points that were covered by \\(\\ell\\).  \nAll other lines are unchanged, so the covering property is preserved.  \nThe new line is distinct from the previously existing lines because its slope differs from theirs (the previous lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) or the original sunny slopes, none of which equals the slope of the new vertical/horizontal line).  \nThus the number of sunny lines decreases by one. Repeating the operation yields any smaller \\(k'\\). ∎\n\n\n\nApplying Lemma 4 to the configurations of Lemma 3 (for \\(n\\ge5\\)) and to the explicit configurations for \\(n=3,4\\) (which have \\(k_{\\max}=1\\)) shows that **every** integer between \\(0\\) and the respective \\(k_{\\max}\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nCollecting the maximal numbers of sunny lines and the reduction argument we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\mathcal K_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3\\text{ or }n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThus for \\(n=3,4\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\); for every \\(n\\ge5\\) all integers from \\(0