e 2 – some column is missing a vertical line.*  \n   Let \\(a_1\\) be the smallest such column.  By Lemma 1 we need at least\n   \\(n+1-a_1\\) non‑vertical lines.  Because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total,\n   the inequality \\(n+1-a_1\\le n\\) forces \\(a_1=1\\); i.e. the **first**\n   column is the only one that may lack a vertical line.  For \\(a_1=1\\) the\n   lower bound becomes \\(n\\); therefore **all** \\(n\\) lines are non‑vertical.\n   A non‑vertical line that is not sunny would be horizontal (slope 0) or have\n   slope \\(-1\\).  Such a line can cover at most one point of the first column,\n   so using any horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line would leave fewer than \\(n\\)\n   lines available for the remaining points of the first column.  Hence every\n   non‑vertical line must be sunny, and we obtain \\(k=n\\).\n\n   Thus the only possible values are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\n4. **Realising the two extremal values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, none is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n   We give an explicit family of \\(n\\) sunny lines that covers all points.\n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = \\frac{n+2-2i}{\\,i-1\\,}\\,(x-1)+i\\qquad (i\\neq 1),\n   \\]\n   and for \\(i=1\\) put  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\; y = n\\,(x-1)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   (When \\(i=1\\) the formula above would be meaningless; the line \\(L_1\\) is\n   chosen separately.)  \n   All slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\):\n   * for \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+2-2i}{i-1}\\);\n     the numerator equals \\(- (2i-n-2)\\), which is never \\(0\\) because\n     \\(2i\\neq n+2\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) (the equality would give a horizontal\n     line, which occurs only when \\(n\\) is even and \\(i=\\frac{n}{2}+1\\); in that\n     exceptional case we 