ng a given family of lines,\nthen each non‑vertical line meets \\(C_a\\) in at most one point. Consequently,\nto cover all points of \\(C_a\\) we need at least \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) non‑vertical lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of the same column have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate; the unique\nline through them is the vertical line \\(x=a\\). Hence any line that is not vertical\ncan intersect the column in at most one point. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (all‑sunny family).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;\n\\begin{cases}\ny=n(x-1)+1, & i=1,\\\\[2mm]\ny=i+\\displaystyle\\frac{n+2-2i}{i-1}\\,(x-1), & i\\ge 2 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n* (i) every \\(L_i\\) is sunny;\n* (ii) the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are pairwise distinct;\n* (iii) for every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) the point lies on the unique line\n      \\(L_{a+b-1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope is \\(\\frac{n+2-2i}{i-1}\\).  \nThe numerator vanishes only when \\(2i=n+2\\), i.e. \\(i=\\frac{n}{2}+1\\); in that case\nthe denominator is also non‑zero, so the slope is \\(0\\) only when \\(n\\) is even and\n\\(i=\\frac{n}{2}+1\\). In this exceptional case we replace \\(L_i\\) by the line through\n\\((1,i)\\) and \\((i,n+3-i)\\); its slope \\(\\frac{n+3-2i}{i-1}\\) is never \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\).\nThus every line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and is sunny.\nDistinct values of \\(i\\) give distinct slopes, so the lines are distinct.\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) set \\(i=a+b-1\\) (clearly \\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through \\((1,i)\\) and \\((i,n+2-i)\\); substituting\n\\(x=a\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny=i+\\frac{n+2-2i}{i-1}(a-1)=b,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\). Uniqueness follows because the map\n\\((a,b)\\mapsto a+b-1\\) is injective. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for an arbitrary \\