all points of the columns\n\\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{\\,n-k}\\).\n\nNow take the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_\\ell = L_{\\,n-k+\\ell}\\qquad (\\ell=1,2,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\ni.e. the last \\(k\\) lines of the family of Lemma 2.\nBy Lemma 2, each point of a column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) belongs to exactly\none of the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\); consequently all points of the remaining\ncolumns \\(C_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,C_n\\) are covered.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them (the \\(S_\\ell\\))\nare sunny. All lines are distinct because the vertical lines have infinite slope,\nwhile the sunny lines have finite slopes different from each other.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration satisfying the\nrequirements exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of other values.\n\nBy definition \\(k\\) counts a subset of the \\(n\\) given lines, so trivially\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\). The constructions above show that every integer in this interval\nis attainable. Hence no other values are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
