ying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Definitions.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).  \n   *For \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the *\\(a\\)-th column* is  \n   \\[\n   C_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n   \\]\n   The set of points that must be covered is  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P=\\bigcup_{a=1}^{n}C_a ,\\qquad |\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Trivial bounds.**  \n   Since we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n   Hence it remains to show that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can actually be realised.\n\n3. **A family of \\(n\\) sunny lines (the “all‑sunny’’ construction).**  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=i+\\frac{n+2-2i}{\\,i-1\\,}(x-1)\\qquad (i\\neq 1),\n   \\]\n   and for \\(i=1\\) put \\(L_1:\\; y=n(x-1)+1\\).\n   All slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the\n   slopes are pairwise distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) set \\(i=a+b-1\\) (note that \\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).\n   By construction \\(L_i\\) passes through \\((1,i)\\) and \\((i,n+2-i)\\); a direct substitution\n   shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\).  \n   Hence each point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).\n\n4. **Mixed constructions for arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n