tical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\)\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  The bound is attained by the lines \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) and\n\\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nConsequently, with \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines the total number of\npoints that can be covered does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n2k+n\\bigl(n-k\\bigr)=n^{2}-(n-2)k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, (6) gives  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-(n-2)k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nK_{\\max }(n)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAny admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le K_{\\max }(n).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with no sunny lines  \n\nConsider  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{0}= \\{\\,y=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then either \\(b=1\\) (hence on \\(y=1\\)) or \\(b\\ge2\\) and\n\\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)).  Thus \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) covers\n\\(S_n\\) and contains no sunny line; therefore \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sunny lines that replace two vertical lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-2\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=x-i+2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,2),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,3),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(S_n\\) because \\(i+2\\le n\\).  Hence the two vertical lines\n\\(x=i\\) and \\(x=i+1\\) become unnecessary for the points \\(P_i,Q_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Extra diagonal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\nFor any integer \\(c\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n\\(D_c\\) is non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)) and contains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose\ncoor