ning admissible values for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) the bound (8) gives  \n\n\\[\nK_{\\max }(n)\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}<\\frac{n}{2}+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the only possible violation of \\(2k\\le n-2\\) occurs when \\(n=5\\) and\n\\(k=3\\) (because \\(K_{\\max }(5)=3\\)).  \nWe treat this single case separately.\n\n**Case \\(n=5,\\;k=3\\).**  \nTake the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&y=x-1 &&\\text{through }(2,1),(3,2),\\\\\n&y=x   &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n&y=x+1 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(2,3),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\ntogether with the non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad x+y=6 .\n\\]\n\nThese five lines are distinct, exactly three are sunny, and a direct check shows\nthat every point of \\(S_5\\) (the 15 points with \\(a+b\\le6\\)) lies on at least one of\nthem.  Hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable for \\(n=5\\).\n\nConsequently, for every \\(n\\ge5\\) and every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (9) a\nconfiguration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Exceptional small values of \\(n\\)\n\n*\\(n=3\\).*  \\(|S_3|=6\\).  Two sunny lines can cover at most four points, leaving at\nleast two uncovered; therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  The values\n\\(k=0,1,3\\) are realised by the families described in Sections 2 and 5.\n\n*\\(n=4\\).*  \\(|S_4|=10\\).  The bound (8) gives \\(k\\le3\\).  \n  – \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are obtained from Sections 2 and 5.  \n  – \\(k=3\\) is obtained by taking the three sunny lines \\(S_2,S_3,S_4\\) together\n    with \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=5\\).  \n  – \\(k=2\\) is impossible: any choice of two non‑sunny lines leaves at least three\n    points uncovered, and no sunny line can pass through two of those three\n    (a short exhaustive check of all \\(\\binom{3}{2}=3\\) families of non‑sunny\n    lines confirms this).  \n\nThus the admissible set for \\(n=4\\) is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusio