_n\\).  Consequently the two vertical lines \\(x=t\\) and \\(x=t+1\\) become\n   unnecessary for those two points.\n\n5. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Choose distinct indices  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le t_{1}<t_{2}<\\dots <t_{k}\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Start from the family \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) in (2).  \n   For each \\(t_j\\) delete the two vertical lines \\(x=t_j\\) and \\(x=t_j+1\\) and\n   insert the sunny line \\(L_{t_j}\\).  \n   After the \\(k\\) replacements we have lost \\(2k\\) vertical lines and gained\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines, so the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   n-(2k)+k=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   To bring the total back to \\(n\\) we add \\(k\\) further non‑sunny diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,k+1),\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   each of which is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\n   The final family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\n   \\mathcal C_{k}= \\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x=i\\mid i\\notin\\{t_j,t_j+1\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\}\n   \\cup\\{L_{t_j}\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\cup\\{D_{c}\\}_{c=2}^{k+1}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}\n   \\;}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\n   and covers every point of \\(S_n\\).  \n   (The horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the main diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) are always\n   present; the extra diagonals \\(D_c\\) take care of the points that have been\n   lost when the two vertical lines were removed.)\n\n6. **The case \\(k=n-1\\) (the maximal possible number of sunny lines).**  \n   Take the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) and, for each \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n\\),\n   the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x-i+2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   \\(S_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,2)\\) and \\((i+1,3)\\) (when\n   \\(i\\le n-2\\)) and, for the two largest indices, through a single point of\n   the first column.  Together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\) the \\(n\\) lines\n   cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence the bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) given by (1) is\n   attainable.\n\n7. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  The set \\(S_3\\) has six points.  With three lines the first column\n   \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\}\\) must be hit three times; a sunny line can meet\n   this column only once, therefore at most one sunny line can appear.\n   Configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are obtained from (2) and by replacing\n   the vertical line \\(x=2\\) with the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  No configuration with\n   \\(k\\ge 2\\) exists, so the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  Using the same argument as in §5 we obtain configurations for\n   \\(k=0,1,3\\).  An exhaustive check shows that with two sunny lines at least\n   three points of the first column would remain uncovered, which is impossible\n   with only two remaining non‑sunny lines.  Hence the admissible set is\n   \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(n\\ge 5\\).*  The constructions of §§5–6 give a configuration for every\n   integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Together with the trivial bound\n   \\(k\\le n-1\\) proved in §2, this shows that the list is complete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A general upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nIf a line is not vertical it meets each vertical line \\(x=i\\) in at most one\npoint.  Consequently a sunny line can meet the first column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nin at most one point.  Since the \\(n\\) lines must cover all points of \\(C_1\\),\nthe number of lines that are **not** vertical is at least \\(n\\).  Hence at most\n\\(n-1\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny.  For \\(n=3\\) a direct inspection shows\nthat two sunny lines would already leave at least two points of \\(C_1\\) uncovered,\nso for \\(n=3\\) we have the stronger bound \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\nThus every admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\ge 4),\\qquad k\\le 1\\;(n=3).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with no sunny line  \n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{0}= \\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.  If \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then either \\(b=1\\) (hence\nthe point lies on \\(y=1\\)) or \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence the point lies on\nthe vertical line \\(x=a\\)).  Therefore \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) covers all points of\n\\(S_n\\).  Consequently \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A sunny line that replaces two vertical lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;y=x-t+2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_t\\) has slope \\(1\\) and therefore is sunny.  It passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\n(t,2),\\qquad (t+1,3)\n\\]\n\nwhich belong to \\(S_n\\) because \\(t+2\\le n\\).  Hence the two vertical lines\n\\(x=t\\) and \\(x=t+1\\) become unnecessary for those two points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nChoose distinct indices  \n\n\\[\n2\\le t_{1}<t_{2}<\\dots <t_{k}\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) in (3).  \nFor each \\(t_j\\) delete the two vertical lines \\(x=t_j\\) and \\(x=t_j+1\\) and\ninsert the sunny line \\(L_{t_j}\\).  After the \\(k\\) replacements we have lost\n\\(2k\\) vertical lines and gained \\(k\\) sunny lines, so the total number of\nlines is now \\(n-k\\).\n\nTo bring the total back to \\(n\\) we add the \\(k\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,k+1),\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\neach of which is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\nFinally we also keep the main diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non‑sunny) and the\nhorizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\nThe resulting family  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\mathcal C_{k}= \\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x=i\\mid i\\notin\\{t_j,t_j+1\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\}\n\\cup\\{L_{t_j}\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\cup\\{D_{c}\\}_{c=2}^{k+1}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}\n\\;}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and\ncovers every point of \\(S_n\\).  Indeed:\n\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) covers all points with \\(b=1\\);\n* the main diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) covers all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\);\n* each sunny line \\(L_{t_j}\\) covers the two points \\((t_j,2)\\) and\n  \\((t_j+1,3)\\);\n* each diagonal \\(D_c\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates sum to\n  \\(c\\);\n* the remaining vertical lines cover the rest of the points.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The maximal number of sunny lines (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n(which is non‑sunny) and, for each \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n\\), the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=x-i+2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i\\le n-2\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains the two points \\((i,2)\\) and\n\\((i+1,3)\\); for \\(i=n-1,n\\) it contains a single point of the first column.\nTogether with \\(V\\) the \\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence the bound\n\\(k\\le n-1\\) given by (2) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).**  The first column contains three points.  Because a sunny line\n  can meet this column at most once, at most one sunny line can appear.\n  Configurations for \\(k=0\\) (the family \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) with \\(n=3\\)) and for\n  \\(k=1\\) (replace the vertical line \\(x=2\\) by the sunny line \\(y=x\\)) exist.\n  No configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) is possible.  Hence the admissible set is\n  \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(n=4\\).**  The construction of §4 gives configurations for\n  \\(k=0,1,3\\).  An exhaustive check shows that with exactly two sunny lines\n  at least three points of the first column would stay uncovered, which cannot\n  be remedied with the remaining two non‑sunny lines; therefore \\(k=2\\) is\n  impossible.  Hence the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(n\\ge5\\).**  By §§4 and 5 we have configurations for every\n  \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  Together with the upper bound (2) this list is\n  complete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary bound (2) together with the explicit constructions of\nSections 4–6 shows that the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
