------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\ge3\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nOmit the diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\) and keep the remaining\n\\(n-k\\) diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\n\nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=jx+1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with an omitted diagonal.*  \nTake a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\).  \nA point \\((a,s-a)\\) lies on \\(L_j\\) iff  \n\n\\[\ns-a=j a+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\na=\\frac{s-1}{j+1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) and \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\), the integer\n\\(\\dfrac{s-1}{j+1}\\) exists **exactly once** for each pair \\((j,s)\\):\nthe numerator \\(s-1\\) runs through \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) while the\ndenominator \\(j+1\\) runs through \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\); the division\nis integral precisely when \\(j+1\\) divides \\(s-1\\).  \nThus for a fixed \\(s\\) the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) produce\nexactly one point of \\(D_s\\) each.  \n\nHence the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) together cover **all** points\nof the omitted diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\).\n\nThe remaining diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) are taken as the\nordinary diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) (slope \\(-1\\)); they are distinct,\nnot sunny, and cover the rest of the points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_j\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\)\nnon‑sunny diagonal lines, i.e. a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\nevery point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  The cases \\(k=0,1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): use the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).\n  None of them is sunny and they cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): use the diagonal lines \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) (they miss\n  only \\((1,1)\\)) and add the sunny line \\(y=x\\), which passes through\