=1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{9q^2+12q+3}{4q+4}, & r=2 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIn every case the right–hand side is strictly less than \\(5\\) for\n\\(q\\ge1\\) (i.e. \\(n\\ge4\\)).  Hence \\(s\\le4\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) and of \\(k=3,4\\) for small \\(n\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 5.}\\quad\nk=2\\text{ is impossible for every }n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a covering uses exactly two sunny lines.\nThen \\(t=n-2\\) non‑sunny lines cover at most \\((n-2)n\\) points\n(Lemma 3).  The remaining points are at most\n\n\\[\nN-(n-2)n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n(n-2)=\\frac{n(5-n)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge6\\) this number is \\(\\le0\\); thus the two sunny lines would\nhave to cover at most \\(3\\) points (the case \\(n=6\\) gives exactly\n\\(3\\)).  The only set of three points that can remain after removing\n\\(n-2\\) vertical lines is\n\\(\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\\).  Any sunny line through two of these\npoints is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), contradicting\nthe definition of a sunny line.  Hence no covering with exactly two\nsunny lines exists.  ∎\n\n\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 6.}\\quad\nk=3,4\\text{ are impossible for }n=4,5 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  For \\(n=4\\) we have \\(M=2\\) (Lemma 1).  If \\(k=3\\) then\nthe three sunny lines cover at most \\(6\\) points, so each must cover\nexactly two.  The six points of \\(P_4\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny partition of these into three pairs forces two pairs to have equal\nsums, which would make the corresponding line horizontal, vertical\nor of slope \\(-1\\).  Thus \\(k=3\\) is impossible.  The case \\(k=4\\)\nis even more restrictive, so it is also impossible.  The same\nargument applies to \\(n=5\\) (where \\(M=2\\) again). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every admissible \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are\nnon‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) and the sunny line\nthrough the remaining point \\((n,1)\\), e.g.\n\n\\[\ny=2x-n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(a\\le n-1\\) are covered by the vertical lines; the\npoint \\((n,1)\\) lies on the sunny line.  Thus \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  \\(k=3\\)  (for \\(n=3\\) and for every \\(n\\ge6\\))\n\n*Case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:\\;y=x ,\\\\\nL_2&:\\;y=2x-1 ,\\\\\nL_3&:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\ncover the six points of \\(P_3\\).  All three are sunny, so \\(k=3\\).\n\n*Case \\(n\\ge6\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-3\\).  \nThe remaining points are\n\n\\[\n\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\;\n  (n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\;\n  (n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:\\;(n-2,1)\\text{ to }(n-1,2),\\\\\nL_2&:\\;(n-2,2)\\text{ to }(n,1),\\\\\nL_3&:\\;(n-2,3)\\text{ to }(n-1,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach pair of points lies on a line of slope \\(1\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\), and\n\\(-2\\) respectively, all of which are sunny.  These three lines cover\nexactly the six remaining points, so \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.4  \\(k=4\\)  (for every \\(n\\ge6\\))\n\nUse the \\(n-4\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-4\\).  \nThe remaining points are\n\n\\[\n\\{(n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),\\;\n  (n-2,1),(n-2,2),\