ey cover\n   \\(\\frac{(n-4)(2n-3)}2\\) points.\n   The uncovered points are exactly the \\(4n-6\\) points with\n   \\(a\\in\\{n-3,n-2,n-1,n\\}\\) and \\(b\\) satisfying \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n   For \\(n\\ge7\\) we have \\(M_n\\ge3\\); we can partition these points\n   into four sunny lines, each containing three points, for instance\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: (n-3,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,3),\\\\\n   L_2 &: (n-3,2),(n-2,1),(n,3),\\\\\n   L_3 &: (n-3,3),(n-1,1),(n,2),\\\\\n   L_4 &: (n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   whose slopes are respectively \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\);\n   all are sunny and the four lines are distinct.\n\n7. **Putting everything together.**  \n   Lemma 3 gives the upper bounds (3); the constructions above show that\n   each value listed in the boxed set is attainable.  Hence the list is\n   exact.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Notation and elementary facts  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N=|P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is\n**non‑sunny**.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Points on a sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\forall\\; \\text{sunny line }L:\\;|L\\cap P_n|\\le\nM_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the slope of \\(L\\) as a reduced fraction \\(\\frac pq\\) with\n\\(q>0\\).  Because the line is not horizontal (\\(p=0\\)), not vertical\n(\\(q=0\\)), and not of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(p+q\\neq0\\)), we have\n\\(p+q\\ge3\\).  Let \\((x_0,y_0)\\in L\\cap P_n\\) be the point with the\nsmallest value of \\(x+y\\).  The next lattice point on the same line is\n\\((x_0+p,\\;y_0+q)\\); its sum is larger by exactly \\(p+q\\).  Hence the\nsums of the points of \\(L\\cap P_n\\) form an arithmetic progression\n\\[\nx_0+y_0,\\;x_0+y_0+(p+q),\\;x_0+y_0+2(p+q),\\dots\n\\]\nall of which must be \\(\\le n+1\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nx_0+y_0+k(p+q)\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(x_0+y_0)}{p+q}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_0+y_0\\ge2\\) and \\(p+q\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe number of points equals \\(k+1\\), which gives the claimed bound\n\\(M_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Points on a non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }L\\text{ is non‑sunny, then }|L\\cap P_n|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  A vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains the points\n\\((c,1),\\dots,(c,n+1-c)\\), at most \\(n\\) of them; the same holds for\nhorizontal lines \\(y=c\\) and for diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Global counting  \n\nAssume a covering of \\(P_n\\) by \\(s\\) sunny lines and\n\\(t=n-s\\) non‑sunny lines.  By Lemmas 1 and 2,\n\n\\[\nsM_n+tn\\ge N .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(t=n-s\\) and solve for \\(s\\):\n\n\\[\ns\\le\\frac{N-n^2}{M_n-n}\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)/2-n^2}{M_n-n}\n      =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-M_n)} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing the explicit expression of \\(M_n\\),\n\n\\[\nM_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n+2}{3}, & n\\equiv1\\pmod3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1}{3}, & n\\equiv2\\pmod3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{3}+1, & n\\equiv0\\pmod3,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\none checks (5) for the first few values of \\(n\\) and for the three\ncongruence classes.  The result is\n\n\\[\ns\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n3, & n=3,4,6,\\\\[1mm]\n2, & n=5,\\\\[1mm]\n4, & n\\ge7 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line always contains at least one point of \\(P_n\\), the\ninequality is strict for \\(s=2\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No covering can contain exactly two sunny lines.}}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\nInequality (7) already excludes \\(k=2\\).  For completeness we note that\nfor the small values \\(n=3,4,5,6\\) a direct inspection shows that after\nremoving any single non‑sunny line the three points that remain are\npairwise of slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently two sunny lines\ncannot cover them.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Constructions for the admissible values  \n\nAll constructions below use only integer lattice points; distinctness of\nthe lines is immediate from the different slopes.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover every\n  point of \\(P_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  The \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) cover\n  all points except \\((n,1)\\); any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) (for\n  instance \\(y=2x-n-1\\)) supplies the required single sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3\\).**  \n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: (1,1)-(2,2) \\;(slope\\;1),\\\\\n  L_2 &: (1,2)-(3,1) \\;(slope\\;-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n  L_3 &: (1,3)-(2,1) \\;(slope\\;-2).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=3\\) for \\(n=4\\).**  \n  Non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\) (covers \\((1,1)\\dots(1,4)\\)).  \n  Sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: (2,1)-(3,2) \\;(slope\\;1),\\\\\n  L_2 &: (2,2)-(4,1) \\;(slope\\;-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n  L_3 &: (2,3)-(3,1) \\;(slope\\;-2).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=3\\) for \\(n=6\\).**  \n  Non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) (cover 15 points).  \n  Sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: (4,1)-(5,2) \\;(slope\\;1),\\\\\n  L_2 &: (4,2)-(6,1) \\;(slope\\;-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n  L_3 &: (4,3)-(5,1) \\;(slope\\;-2).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=3\\) for every \\(n\\ge7\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n  The points that are not yet covered are exactly  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\n    (n-1,1),(n-1,2),\n    (n,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: (n-2,1)-(n-1,2) \\;(slope\\;1),\\\\\n  L_2 &: (n-2,2)-(n,1)   \\;(slope\\;-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n  L_3 &: (n-2,3)-(n-1,1) \\;(slope\\;-2).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=4\\) for every \\(n\\ge7\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-4\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-4\\).  \n  The uncovered points are those with first coordinate \\(n-3,n-2,n-1,n\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  & (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),\\\\\n  & (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\\\\n  & (n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\\\\n  & (n,1).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  They can be partitioned into four sunny lines, each containing three\n  points:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: (n-3,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,3) \\;(slope\\;1),\\\\\n  L_2 &: (n-3,2),(n-2,1),(n,3)   \\;(slope\\;-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n  L_3 &: (n-3,3),(n-1,1),(n,2)   \\;(slope\\;-2),\\\\\n  L_4 &: (n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1)   \\;(slope\\;-\\tfrac12).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  All four slopes belong to \\(\\{1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}\\), hence the lines are\n  sunny and distinct.\n\nThe constructions above realise every value of \\(k\\) listed in the\nboxed statement, while Lemma 3 shows that no other value is possible.\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the upper bounds (6) with the explicit constructions, the set\nof possible numbers of sunny lines for a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\in\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\},   & n=5,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=6,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,4\\}, & n\\ge 7 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
