            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n#### 1.  Notation and elementary facts  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N=|P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is\n**non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Points on a sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\forall\\; \\text{sunny line }L:\\;|L\\cap P_n|\\le\nM_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the slope of \\(L\\) as a reduced fraction \\(\\frac pq\\) with\n\\(q>0\\).  Because the line is not horizontal (\\(p\\neq0\\)), not vertical\n(\\(q\\neq0\\)) and not of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(p+q\\neq0\\)), we have\n\\(p+q\\ge3\\).  Let \\((x_0,y_0)\\in L\\cap P_n\\) be the point with the\nsmallest value of \\(x+y\\).  The next lattice point on the same line is\n\\((x_0+p,\\;y_0+q)\\); its sum is larger by exactly \\(p+q\\).  Hence the\nsums of the points of \\(L\\cap P_n\\) form an arithmetic progression\n\n\\[\nx_0+y_0,\\;x_0+y_0+(p+q),\\;x_0+y_0+2(p+q),\\dots\n\\]\n\nall of which must be \\(\\le n+1\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nx_0+y_0+k(p+q)\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(x_0+y_0)}{p+q}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_0+y_0\\ge2\\) and \\(p+q\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe number of points equals \\(k+1\\), which gives the bound\n\\(M_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Points on a non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }L\\text{ is non‑sunny, then }|L\\cap P_n|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  A vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains the points\n\\((c,1),\\dots,(c,n+1-c)\\), at most \\(n\\) of them; the same bound holds\nfor a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) and for a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Global counting  \n\nAssume a covering of \\(P_n\\) by \\(s\\) sunny lines and\n\\(t=n-s\\) non‑sunny lines.  By Lemmas 1 and 2,\n\n\\[\nsM_n+tn\\ge N .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(t=n-s\\) and solve (4.1) for \\(s\\):\n\n\\[\ns\\le\n\\frac{N-n^2}{M_n-n}\n      =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-M_n)} .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nUsing the explicit expression of \\(M_n\\),\n\n\\[\nM_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n+2}{3}, & n\\equiv1\\pmod3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1}{3}, & n\\equiv2\\pmod3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{3}+1, & n\\equiv0\\pmod3,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\none checks (4.2) for the three congruence classes.  The result is\n\n\\[\ns\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n3, & n=3,4,6,\\\\[1mm]\n2, & n=5,\\\\[1mm]\n4, & n\\ge7 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line always contains at least one point of \\(P_n\\),\nthe inequality in (4.1) is strict when \\(s=2\\); indeed\n\n\\[\n(n-2)n+2M_n<N\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No covering can contain exactly two sunny lines.}}\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)  \n\nInequality (4.4) shows that, even if the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines were\nchosen so that they covered the maximum possible number of *distinct*\npoints, the