\\]\n\nThe number of points equals \\(k+1\\), which gives the bound\n\\(M_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Points on a non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }L\\text{ is non‑sunny, then }|L\\cap P_n|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  A vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains the points\n\\((c,1),\\dots,(c,n+1-c)\\), at most \\(n\\) of them; the same bound holds\nfor a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) and for a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Global counting  \n\nAssume a covering of \\(P_n\\) by \\(s\\) sunny lines and\n\\(t=n-s\\) non‑sunny lines.  By Lemmas 1 and 2,\n\n\\[\nsM_n+tn\\ge N .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(t=n-s\\) and solve (4.1) for \\(s\\):\n\n\\[\ns\\le\n\\frac{N-n^2}{M_n-n}\n      =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-M_n)} .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nUsing the explicit expression of \\(M_n\\),\n\n\\[\nM_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n+2}{3}, & n\\equiv1\\pmod3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1}{3}, & n\\equiv2\\pmod3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{3}+1, & n\\equiv0\\pmod3,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\none checks (4.2) for the three congruence classes.  The result is\n\n\\[\ns\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n3, & n=3,4,6,\\\\[1mm]\n2, & n=5,\\\\[1mm]\n4, & n\\ge7 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line always contains at least one point of \\(P_n\\),\nthe inequality in (4.1) is strict when \\(s=2\\); indeed\n\n\\[\n(n-2)n+2M_n<N\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No covering can contain exactly two sunny lines.}}\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)  \n\nInequality (4.4) shows that, even if the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines were\nchosen so that they covered the maximum possible number of *distinct*\npoints, the two sunny lines could add at most \\(2M_n\\) further points,\n