2\\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: (1,1)-(2,2) &&(\\text{slope }1),\\\\\n   L_2 &: (1,2)-(3,1) &&(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n   L_3 &: (1,3)-(2,1) &&(\\text{slope }-2)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   together with the remaining \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover all\n   points of \\(P_n\\).\n\n   – For every \\(n\\ge7\\) proceed as follows.  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines\n   \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The six points that are left are  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\n   (n-1,1),(n-1,2),\n   (n,1).\n   \\]\n\n   They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: (n-2,1)-(n-1,2) &&(\\text{slope }1),\\\\\n   L_2 &: (n-2,2)-(n,1)   &&(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n   L_3 &: (n-2,3)-(n-1,1) &&(\\text{slope }-2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All three lines are distinct and sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=4\\) (only for \\(n\\ge7\\)).*  \n   Use the \\(n-4\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-4\\).  The ten points that remain are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &(n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),\\\\\n   &(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\\\\n   &(n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\\\\n   &(n,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   They are partitioned into four sunny lines, each containing three points:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: (n-3,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,3) &&(\\text{slope }1),\\\\\n   L_2 &: (n-3,2),(n-2,1),(n,3)   &&(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n   L_3 &: (n-3,3),(n-1,1),(n,2)   &&(\\text{slope }-2),\\\\\n   L_4 &: (n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1)   &&(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All four slopes belong to \\(\\{1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}\\); the lines are sunny and distinct.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 3 shows that two sunny lines are impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Lemma 6 gives the sharp upper bounds \\(k\\le3\\) for \\(n\\le6\\) and \\(k\\le4\\) for\n   \\(n\\ge7\\).  The explicit families of configurations displayed in §6 realise every\n   admissible value, and no other value can occur.  Hence the answer is exactly the\n   set stated in the boxed formula. ∎"
