e triangle)\n\nLet a sunny line intersect \\(T_s\\).  As in Lemma 1 the points of the line in\n\\(T_s\\) have sums \\(x+y\\) forming an arithmetic progression with step\n\\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).  Because the smallest possible sum in \\(T_s\\) equals \\(2\\) and\nthe largest equals \\(s+1\\), the progression can contain **at most two**\nterms.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line meets }T_s\\text{ in at most two points.}}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nThe \\(s\\) sunny lines must cover all points of \\(T_s\\); by (4) they can cover\nat most \\(2s\\) points.  Using (3),\n\n\\[\n2s\\ge\\frac{s(s+1)}2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad s\\le3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus a covering with \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain **at most three** sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (exactly two sunny lines are impossible)\n\nSuppose a covering contains exactly two sunny lines.\nRemove the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\); the uncovered points\nare  \n\n\\[\n\\{(n-1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(n+3\\) points.  By Lemma 1 each sunny line can contain at most\n\\(M_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) of them, so the two\nsunny lines together cover at most \\(n-1\\) points, which is strictly fewer than\n\\(n+3\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence a covering with exactly two sunny lines\ndoes not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of the three admissible values\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover all points of \\(P_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); the only uncovered\n  point is \\((n,1)\\).  Any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) supplies the single\n  sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\)).**  \n  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The six points that are\n  left are  \n\n  \\[\n  (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n  \\]\n\n  They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: (n-2,1)-(n-1,2) &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\n  L_2 &: (n-2,2)-(n,1)   &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n  L_3 &: (n-2,3)-(n-1,1) &&\\text{slope }-2,\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  which are distinct and sunny.  Hence a covering with exactly three sunny\n  lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma 5 shows that a covering cannot contain four or more sunny lines; Lemma 4\nexcludes the case of exactly two sunny lines.  The explicit families of\nconfigurations displayed above realise the remaining possibilities\n\\(k=0,1,3\\).  Therefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the admissible numbers of sunny lines. ∎"
