            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\nD_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); it contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf a line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) then it meets the anti‑diagonal \\(D_n\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of \\(D_n\\) have slope \\(-1\\). Hence any line of slope \\(\\neq-1\\) can contain at most one of them. ∎\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain:\n\n*Corollary 2.*  \n\n* (i) For every \\(n\\ge4\\) any family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(S_n\\) contains at least one non‑sunny line.  \n* (ii) For every \\(n\\ge5\\) any such family contains at least two non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Proof of (i).*  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each could cover at most one point of \\(D_n\\); the \\(n\\) points of \\(D_n\\) would then require at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, leaving no line to cover the interior points. Hence at least one line must be non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof of (ii).*  \nAssume a covering uses exactly one non‑sunny line. By Lemma 1 the only way to cover all points of \\(D_n\\) with a single non‑sunny line is to take the anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nRemoving the points of \\(L_0\\) leaves the set  \n\n\\[\nS_{n-1}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich must be covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines. Since \\(n-1\\ge4\\), Corollary 2(i) applied to \\(S_{n-1}\\) forces another non‑sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining the lower bound\n\nWe prove by induction the following statement.\n\n**Lemma 3 (inductive construction).**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer  \n\n\\[\nt\\in\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge5,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) that contains exactly \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe argue by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe six points of \\(S_3\\) are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\nAdding any vertical, horizontal or anti‑diagonal line (which are non‑sunny) yields configurations with \\(t=1,2,3\\). Thus the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the claim true for some \\(n\\ge3\\). We treat the three possible ranges for the desired \\(t\\).\n\n1. **\\(n+1=4\\).**  \n   Take the anti‑diagonal \\(L_0:x+y=5\\) (non‑sunny). The remaining points are exactly \\(S_3\\); cover them by the three sunny lines of the base case. This gives a configuration with \\(t=1\\). Adding extra non‑sunny lines among the three sunny ones yields any \\(t\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\).\n\n2. **\\(n+1\\ge5\\).**  \n   Choose the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_0:\\;x+y=n+2,\\qquad\n   V_1:\\;x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   After removing the points covered by \\(L_0\\cup V_1\\) the uncovered set is\n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+2\\}\n   =\\{(a',b)\\mid a'\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le n+1\\}=S_n .\n   \\]\n\n   By the induction hypothesis we can cover \\(S_n\\) with \\(n\\) lines containing any admissible number of non‑sunny lines.  \n   - To obtain exactly **two** non‑sunny lines in the whole configuration we select **zero** additional non‑sunny lines for the covering of \\(S_n\\).  \n   - To obtain a larger number \\(t\\) we simply replace the required number of sunny lines in the covering of \\(S_n\\) by vertical, horizontal or anti‑diagonal lines; this does not affect the covering property.\n\n   Hence for \\(n+1\\ge5\\) we can realise every \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus Lemma 3 holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of the possible numbers of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(t\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(S_n\\).  \nFrom Corollary 2 we have the necessary lower bounds\n\n\\[\nt\\ge\n\\begin{cases}\n0, & n=3,\\\\\n1, & n=4,\\\\\n2, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nLemma 3 shows that every integer \\(t\\) satisfying these bounds can indeed be achieved.  \nSince the number of sunny lines is \\(k=n-t\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\nk=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,1,2,3, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n0,1,2,3, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n=4:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\ge5:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThus for \\(n\\ge5\\) at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, and the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is sharp.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO gra