t**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\},\n   \\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The sets \\(D_s\\) are the \\(n\\) “diagonal’’ collections of lattice points, each lying on a line\n   parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n2. **Intersection property.**  \n   Any line whose slope is **not** \\(-1\\) (i.e. any line that is *not* parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) meets each\n   diagonal \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point.  This holds for sunny lines as well as for horizontal\n   and vertical lines.\n\n3. **Counting argument.**  \n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of the triangle\n   \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   Denote  \n\n   * \\(o\\) – the number of lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) whose slope is **not** \\(-1\\) (these are the\n     “other’’ lines; they may be sunny, horizontal or vertical);\n   * \\(d=n-o\\) – the number of diagonal lines (slope \\(-1\\)) that belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n   * \\(k\\) – the number of sunny lines among the \\(o\\) other lines.\n\n   The set of diagonals that are **not** present as lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) has cardinality exactly\n   \\(o\\) (because the total number of diagonals is \\(n\\)).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal M=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid D_s\\text{ is not a line of }\\mathcal L\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal M|=o .\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\(s\\in\\mathcal M\\) the diagonal \\(D_s\\) must be covered solely by the \\(o\\) other lines,\n   and each of those lines contributes at most one point of \\(D_s\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   o\\;\\ge\\;|D_s|=s-1\\qquad\\forall\\,s\\in\\mathcal M . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(s_{\\max}=\\max\\mathcal M\\) (if \\(\\mathcal M\\neq\\varnothing\\)).  From (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   o\\ge s_{\\max}-1 . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(|\\mathcal M|=o\\), the only way to satisfy (2) is to choose  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal M=\\{2,3,\\dots ,o+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently **any** integer \\(o\\) with \\(0\\le o\\le n\\) is admissible, and for a given \\(o\\) the\n   number of sunny lines \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(o\\) (the remaining\n   \\(o-k\\) other lines may be taken horizontal or vertical).\n\n4. **Existence constructions.**  \n   For each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we exhibit an explicit family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Choose the first \\(k\\) sunny lines*  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 ,\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k,\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (slope \\(1\\), therefore sunny).  \n\n   *Choose the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines* as vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x = k+j ,\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,n-k .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k,V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and\n   contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n   **Coverage verification.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   * If \\(a\\le k\\) then the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\) because (3) gives\n     \\(b = a-(a-1)=1\\) for the first point and, more generally,\n     \\(b\\) can be any integer with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); all such points satisfy the equation of\n     \\(S_a\\).\n\n   * If \\(a>k\\) then \\(a=k+j\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), and the point lies on the vertical\n     line \\(V_j\\) (equation (4)).\n\n   Thus every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n   The construction works for every \\(k\\) in the stated range, proving that all those values are\n   attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_s\\) are pairwise disjoint, each lies on the line \\(a+b=s\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)),\nand  \n\n\\[\n|D_s|=s-1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Intersection property\n\nA line whose slope is different from \\(-1\\) (i.e. any line that is **not** parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\)) intersects a given diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point: solving the two linear\nequations  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na+b=s,\\\\\n\\text{equation of the line},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\ngives a unique solution unless the two equations are dependent, which would mean the line\nitself is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every line with slope }\\neq -1\\text{ meets each }D_s\\text{ in }\\le 1\\text{ point.}}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  General counting bound\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\nPartition \\(\\mathcal L\\) as follows:\n\n* **Diagonal lines** (slope \\(-1\\)): let their number be \\(d\\).\n* **Other lines** (slope \\(\\neq -1\\)): let their number be \\(o=n-d\\).\n\nAmong the \\(o\\) other lines, denote by \\(k\\) the number of *sunny* lines (i.e. those not\nparallel to the \\(x\\)-axis nor to the \\(y\\)-axis).  The remaining \\(o-k\\) other lines are\nhorizontal or vertical.\n\nBecause there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\), the set of diagonals that are\n*not* present as lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) has cardinality \\(o\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal M=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid D_s\\text{ is not a line of }\\mathcal L\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal M|=o . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\(s\\in\\mathcal M\\) the points of \\(D_s\\) must be covered by the \\(o\\) other lines.\nBy (6) each of those lines contributes at most one point of \\(D_s\\); therefore\n\n\\[\no\\;\\ge\\;|D_s|=s-1\\qquad\\forall\\,s\\in\\mathcal M . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(s_{\\max}=\\max\\mathcal M\\) (if \\(\\mathcal M\\neq\\varnothing\\)).  From (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\no\\ge s_{\\max}-1 . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal M|=o\\), the only way to satisfy (9) is to choose  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal M=\\{2,3,\\dots ,o+1\\}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, (10) gives \\(|\\mathcal M|=o\\) and \\(s_{\\max}=o+1\\), making (9) an equality.\nConsequently **any** integer \\(o\\) with \\(0\\le o\\le n\\) is possible, and for a fixed \\(o\\) the\nnumber of sunny lines \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(o\\) (the remaining\n\\(o-k\\) lines may be taken horizontal or vertical).\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Explicit constructions for every \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*For the sunny lines* take, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\),\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); hence it is sunny.\n\n*For the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines* take vertical lines\n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x = k + j ,\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,n-k . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThese are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n**Coverage proof.**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le k\\)*, then \\(a\\) equals some index \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  Substituting \\(a=i\\) into\n(12) gives \\(b = a - i + 1 = 1\\); more generally, (12) can be rewritten as \\(b = a - i + 1\\),\nso for any admissible \\(b\\) (i.e. \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\)) the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of\n\\(S_a\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_a\\).\n\n*If \\(a>k\\)*