Let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_s\\) are pairwise disjoint, each lies on the line \\(a+b=s\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)),\nand  \n\n\\[\n|D_s|=s-1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Intersection property\n\nA line whose slope is different from \\(-1\\) (i.e. any line that is **not** parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\)) intersects a given diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point: solving the two linear\nequations  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na+b=s,\\\\\n\\text{equation of the line},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\ngives a unique solution unless the two equations are dependent, which would mean the line\nitself is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every line with slope }\\neq -1\\text{ meets each }D_s\\text{ in }\\le 1\\text{ point.}}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  General counting bound\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\nPartition \\(\\mathcal L\\) as follows:\n\n* **Diagonal lines** (slope \\(-1\\)): let their number be \\(d\\).\n* **Other lines** (slope \\(\\neq -1\\)): let their number be \\(o=n-d\\).\n\nAmong the \\(o\\) other lines, denote by \\(k\\) the number of *sunny* lines (i.e. those not\nparallel to the \\(x\\)-axis nor to the \\(y\\)-axis).  The remaining \\(o-k\\) other lines are\nhorizontal or vertical.\n\nBecause there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\), the set of diagonals that are\n*not* present as lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) has cardinality \\(o\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal M=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid D_s\\text{ is not a line of }\\mathcal L\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal M|=o . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\(s\\in\\mathcal M\\) the points of \\(D_s\\) must be covered by the \\(o\\) other lines.\nBy (6) each of those lines contributes at most one point of \\(D_s\\); therefore\n\n\\[\no\\;\\ge\\;|D_s|=s-1\\qquad\\forall\\,s\\in\\mathcal M . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(s_{\\max}=\\max\\mathcal M\\) (if \\(\\mathcal M\\neq\\varnothing\\)).  From (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\no\\ge s_{\\max}-1 . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal M|=o\\), the only way to satisfy (9) is to choose  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal M=\\{2,3,\\dots ,o+1\\}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, (10) gives \\(|\\mathcal M|=o\\) and \\(s_{\\max}=o+1\\), making (9) an equality.\nConsequently **any** integer \\(o\\) with \\(0\\le o\\le n\\) is possible, and for a fixed \\(o\\) the\nnumber of sunny lines \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(o\\) (the remaining\n\\(o-k\\) lines may be taken horizontal or vertical).\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Explicit constructions for every \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*For the sunny lines* take, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\),\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); hence it is sunny.\n\n*For the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines* take vertical lines\n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x = k + j ,\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,n-k . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThese are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n**Coverage proof.**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le k\\)*, then \\(a\\) equals some index \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  Substituting \\(a=i\\) into\n(12) gives \\(b = a - i + 1 = 1\\); more generally, (12) can be rewritten as \\(b = a - i + 1\\),\nso for any admissible \\(b\\) (i.e. \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\)) the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of\n\\(S_a\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_a\\).\n\n*If \\(a>k\\)*, write \\(a=k+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\).  Then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the vertical\nequation \\(x=k+j\\), i.e. it lies on \\(V_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\), and \\(\\mathcal\nL_{k}\\) uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince a construction exists for each integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\), and the counting\nargument shows that no other \\(k\\) is possible, we have completely solved the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
