ecisely which \\(k\\) are feasible; the provided solution’s statement “\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)” is too generous.\n\n### BOUNDARY_CASES ###\nBOUNDARY_1: \\(n=3\\) – examined above; \\(k=3\\) fails.  \nBOUNDARY_2: \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\) – the construction with two sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=x-1\\) plus one vertical line \\(x=3\\) does cover all points, so \\(k=2\\) is possible.  \nBOUNDARY_3: \\(n=4,\\;k=4\\) – analogous construction would give four sunny lines \\(y=x, y=x-1, y=x-2, y=x-3\\). The point \\((1,4)\\) is not on any of them, showing the same failure pattern for \\(k=n\\).  \n\n### ASSUMPTION_CHALLENGES ###\nCHALLENGE_1: The solution assumes that the family of sunny lines \\(y = x - i + 1\\) (slope = 1) can cover every point of the triangle for any \\(k\\). This fails when the first coordinate \\(a\\) is small and the second coordinate \\(b\\) is large (e.g., \\((1, n)\\)).  \nCHALLENGE_2: Implicitly assumes that “other lines” (non‑diagonal) can always be taken as vertical/horizontal without affecting coverage, but when all “other lines” are required to be sunny, vertical/horizontal are disallowed, breaking the covering argument for large \\(k\\).  \n\n### CRITICAL_FLAWS ###\nFLAW_1: **Incorrect coverage proof for the construction with all sunny lines.** The argument “if \\(a\\le k\\) then the point lies on sunny line \\(S_a\\)” is false; the equation of \\(S_a\\) forces \\(b=1\\), not all admissible \\(b\\). Hence many points are left uncovered.  \nFLAW_2: **Overgeneralization from the counting argument.** The counting bound only shows that the number of non‑diagonal lines \\(o\\) can range from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\); it does **not** guarantee that all of those \\(o\\) lines can be sunny simultaneously. The solution conflates “possible \\(o\\)” with “possible \\(k=o\\)”.  \n\n### SEVERITY ###\nCRITICAL_COUNT: 2  \nMAJOR_COUNT: 1  \nMINOR_COUNT: 0\n\n### CRITICAL: ANSWER VERIFICATION CHECKPOINT ###\n\n**BEFORE defending your proof, answer these questions HONESTLY:**