 the number of *sunny* lines among the \\(o\\) other lines\n  (a sunny line is one whose slope is also different from \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\)).\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  \nIf a diagonal \\(D_s\\) itself belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) we say it is *present*;\notherwise it is *missing*.  Because there are \\(d\\) present diagonals, the set of\nmissing diagonals has cardinality  \n\n\\[\n|\\{s\\mid D_s\\text{ missing}\\}| = n-d = o. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal M=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid D_s\\text{ is missing}\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal M|=o. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\(s\\in\\mathcal M\\) the points of \\(D_s\\) must be covered by the \\(o\\) other\nlines.  By (2) each of those lines contributes at most one point of \\(D_s\\); therefore\n\n\\[\no\\;\\ge\\;|D_s|=s-1\\qquad\\forall\\,s\\in\\mathcal M . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every missing diagonal satisfies \\(s\\le o+1\\).  Since \\(|\\mathcal M|=o\\),\nthe only possible choice is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal M=\\{2,3,\\dots ,o+1\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the present diagonals are exactly  \n\n\\[\nx+y=o+2,\\;x+y=o+3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) we obtain the **necessary inequalities**\n\n\\[\n0\\le o\\le n,\\qquad 0\\le k\\le o . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for all admissible \\((o,k)\\) except \\((2,2)\\) when \\(n=3\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every pair \\((o,k)\\) satisfying (8)\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nThe construction proceeds in two steps.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The diagonal lines\n\nTake the \\(d=n-o\\) diagonal lines prescribed by (7); they are automatically non‑sunny\nand already cover all points belonging to the diagonals \\(D_{o+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Covering the first \\(o\\) diagonals\n\nThe points that remain uncovered are precisely those of the set  \n\n\\[\nU_o=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{o+1} D_s\n      =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le o+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must cover \\(U_o\\) with exactly \\(o\\) lines, of which \\(k\\) are sunny.\nWrite \\(r=o-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines among the \\(o\\) lines).\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nChoose the \\(r\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x = k+j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,r). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(U_o\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least \\(k+1\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nIt remains to cover the points of the smaller triangle  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\le k,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x -\\frac{(i-1)(i-2)}{2}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\); hence it is different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\) for\nall \\(i\\ge2\\), and \\(S_1\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) which does **not** meet any\npoint of \\(U_k\\); therefore we replace \\(S_1\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nS_1:\\; y = x . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBoth (10) (for \\(i\\ge2\\)) and (11) are sunny.\n\n*Intersection with the diagonals.*  \nFix a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\).  Solving the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na+b=s,\\\\[2pt]\nb = (i-1)a-\\dfrac{(i-1)(i-2)}{2},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\ngives the unique integer solution  \n\n\\[\na=i,\\qquad b=s-i .\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(S_i\\) meets the diagonal \\(D_s\\) exactly at the point \\((i,s-i)\\),\nwhich belongs to \\(U_k\\).  Consequently the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) cover **all**\npoints of \\(U_k\\), each diagonal contributing exactly one point to each line.\n\nTogether the vertical lines (9) and the sunny lines (10)–(11) cover the whole set\n\\(U_o\\).  All lines are distinct, and the number of sunny lines among them is exactly\n\\(k\\).  Adding the diagonal lines (7) we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  The exceptional case \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) we have \\(o\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \nIf \\(o=2\\) then the missing diagonals are \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\) (sizes \\(1\\) and \\(2\\)).\nWith only two sunny lines each can meet a given diagonal in at most one point, so at\nmost two of the three points of \\(D_2\\cup D_3\\) can be covered – one point inevitably\nremains uncovered.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=2\\) sunny lines does **not** exist.\nAll other values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are realised by the constructions above (take \\(o=0,1,3\\)\nrespectively).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\ \\text{and}\\ (n,k)\\neq(3,2)\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for \\(n=3\\) the possible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\); for all \\(n\\ge4\\) every\ninteger \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be achieved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
