e the \\(r\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x = k+j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,r). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(U_o\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least\n\\(k+1\\).  The remaining points form the smaller triangle  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\le k,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDefine the following \\(k\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_1 &: y = x+1,\\\\\nS_i &: y=(i-1)x-\\dfrac{(i-1)(i-2)}{2}\\qquad (i=2,\\dots ,k). \\tag{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope equals \\(i-1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); for \\(S_1\\) the slope is\n\\(1\\), also sunny.  Solving the system  \n\n\\[\na+b=s,\\qquad b=(i-1)a-\\frac{(i-1)(i-2)}{2}\n\\]\n\ngives the unique integer solution \\((a,b)=(i,s-i)\\).  Hence for every diagonal\n\\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\) the line \\(S_i\\) meets \\(D_s\\) exactly at the\npoint \\((i,s-i)\\).  Consequently the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) cover **all**\npoints of the triangle \\(U_k\\).\n\nTogether the vertical lines (4) and the sunny lines (6) cover the whole set\n\\(U_o\\).  Adding the diagonal lines prescribed by (1) yields a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=o\\) (all non‑diagonal lines are sunny)\n\nHere we simply omit the vertical lines and use the sunny family (6) with\n\\(k=o\\).  The same verification as above shows that the triangle \\(U_o\\) is\ncovered, and the diagonal lines (1) are added as before.  Thus every pair\n\\((n,k)\\) with \\(k=o\\) and \\(k\\neq2,3\\) is attainable, **except** the two\nsmall values treated next.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Exceptional small values \\((n,k)=(4,4)\\) and \\((5,5)\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=4\\) (resp. \\(n=5\\)) we would need to cover the triangle \\(U_4\\)\n(resp. \\(U_5\\)) with four (resp. five) sunny lines