                "content": "**Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) put  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in \\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(s\\ge 2\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union is\n\\(T_n\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(n\\) we are asked for which integers \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\) there\nexist  \n\n* \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L\\) covering every point of \\(T_n\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ all integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\n\\text{ are attainable.}}\n\\]\n\nThus the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is the whole interval\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Proof  \n\nWe prove the statement by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Basis \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains three points.  The following four families of three distinct lines\nshow that every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) can be realised.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The three non‑sunny diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4 .\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  One sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the two diagonal lines\n  \\(x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\).\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  Two sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1\n  \\]\n\n  (both pass through \\((1,1)\\)) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=3\\) covering the\n  remaining two points.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  Three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y=-2x+5 &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1),\\\\\n  L_2 &: y= x   &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n  L_3 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  which cover all points of \\(T_3\\).\n\nHence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe prove that the same is true for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2.1  From \\(k\\le n\\) to the same \\(k\\) for \\(n+1\\)\n\nTake the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) for \\(T_n\\).  \nThe new points that appear in \\(T_{n+1}\\) are precisely the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+2}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b=n+2\\},\n\\qquad |D_{n+2}|=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAdd to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) the non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nIt is distinct from all lines already present (they have constants\n\\(2,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  The added line covers the whole set \\(D_{n+2}\\); therefore\nthe enlarged family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{x+y=n+2\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_{n+1}\\) and still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nThus every \\(k\\le n\\) is attainable for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2.2  From \\(k=n\\) to \\(k=n+1\\)\n\nAssume we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) consisting of \\(n\\) sunny\nlines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nConsider the new diagonal \\(D_{n+2}\\) (again of size \\(n+1\\)).  \nEach of the \\(n\\) existing sunny lines meets \\(D_{n+2}\\) in at most one point,\nbecause a line whose slope is different from \\(-1\\) can intersect a line\n\\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point.  Consequently at most \\(n\\) points of\n\\(D_{n+2}\\) are already covered; at least one point \\(Q\\in D_{n+2}\\) remains\nuncovered.\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slope of any line of }\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\}.\n\\]\n\nSuch an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because only finitely many slopes are forbidden.\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-Q_y=\\alpha\\,(x-Q_x).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) passes through the uncovered point \\(Q\\), its slope is\n\\(\\alpha\\) (hence \\(\\ell\\) is sunny), and \\(\\ell\\) is distinct from all lines of\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,n+1}= \\mathcal L_{n,n}\\cup\\{\\ell\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,n+1}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) sunny lines and covers\n\\(T_{n+1}=T_n\\cup D_{n+2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2.3  Summary of the inductive step  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* for every \\(k\\le n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists\n  for \\(n+1\\);\n* a configuration with \\(k=n+1\\) sunny lines also exists for \\(n+1\\).\n\nHence for the integer \\(n+1\\) all values \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\) are\nrealizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe basis \\(n=3\\) and the inductive step together prove that for **every**\n\\(n\\ge 3\\) and **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**All cases are covered, and the answer is complete.**"
            },
