e points that must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  \nThe boundary of the triangle \\(T_n\\) consists of the points with  \n\\(a=1\\), \\(b=1\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nAll other points are **interior** points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b=1\\) (hence lies on \\(y=1\\)).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis or to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the same vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) and replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x-(n-1).\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) has slope \\(1\\), so it is sunny.  \nThe only point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\), and \\(L\\) passes through it.  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and are covered by the vertical lines.  \nHence all points of \\(T_n\\) lie on one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly one of them is sunny.  \nThus \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\nLet \\(I_n\\subset T_n\\) be the set of interior points, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of interior points is\n\n\\[\n|I_n|=\\sum_{a=2}^{\\,n-1}\\bigl((n+1-a)-1\\bigr)\n      =\\sum_{t=1}^{\\,n-2}t\n      =\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) interior points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any side of the triangle, so it meets the triangle in a segment whose endpoints lie on two distinct sides.  \nAlong that segment the \\(x\\)-coordinates of lattice points increase by at least \\(1\\) each time, and the segment is contained in the strip \\(1\\le x\\le n\\).  \nHence the segment contains at most \\(n-1\\) lattice points in total, and at most \\(\\frac{n-1}2\\) of them can have \\(b\\ge2\\) (otherwise the segment would leave the triangle).  \nThus the number of interior lattice points on a sunny line is at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}2\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Corollary  \nWith \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}2\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ninterior points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Proof of impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with \\(k=2\\) sunny lines exists.  \nBy the corollary, the two sunny lines cover at most \\(n-1\\) interior points.  \nFrom (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2>n-1\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge4.\n\\]\n\nHence for \\(n\\ge4\\) the two sunny lines cannot cover all interior points, a contradiction.\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the triangle \\(T_3\\) has only one interior point \\((2,2)\\).  \nIf we had two sunny lines, we would need at least the three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=4\\) to cover the boundary points.  \nThat would already give \\(3+2>n=3\\) lines, impossible.  \nThus \\(k=2\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\) as well.\n\nTherefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe only admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\text{ or }1}.\n\\]\n\nBoth values are attainable by the constructions in Sections 1 and 2, and \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible by the interior‑point argume