ause otherwise \\(\\ell_i\\) would be\nredundant).  Replace \\(\\ell_i\\) by the vertical line through that point.\nThe replacement does not increase the number of sunny lines, keeps all lines\ndistinct, and still covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nRepeating this for all \\(\\ell_i\\) we obtain a new covering \\(\\mathcal F'\\) with\nthe same \\(k\\) sunny lines and **all** non‑sunny lines vertical.\nThe set of points not lying on any vertical line of \\(\\mathcal F'\\) is\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a right‑angled triangular lattice of size \\(k\\).  Hence any covering with\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines yields a covering of \\(T_k\\) by those \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility for \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nApply Lemma 1 to the triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2t\\).  Then each sunny line meets \\(T_k\\) in at\nmost \\(t\\) points, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most\n\\(kt=t\\cdot2t=k^{2}/2\\) points.  But  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2>\\frac{k^{2}}2,\n\\]\n\na contradiction.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd*, write \\(k=2t+1\\).  Lemma 1 gives the bound\n\\(t+1\\) points per sunny line, and Lemma 2 tells us that **only** the three\nlines listed in (3) can actually attain this bound.  Consequently at most\nthree of the \\(k\\) sunny lines contain \\(t+1\\) points; the remaining\n\\(k-3\\) lines contain at most \\(t\\) points each.  Hence the total number of\ncovered points is at most  \n\n\\[\n3(t+1)+(k-3)t=\\frac{k^{2}-1}{2}<\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_k|,\n\\]\n\nagain impossible.\n\nThus no covering exists for any \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\nIf \\(k=2\\), Lemma 3 reduces the problem to covering  \n\n\\[\nT_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nby two sunny lines.  Any line through two of these points has slope\n\\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), al