 all vertical except one sunny line; all vertical except three extremal sunny lines) that work for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a multiple of  \n\\((1,0)\\) (horizontal), \\((0,1)\\) (vertical) or \\((1,1)\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write its slope in reduced form \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\((p,q)\\neq(0,1),(1,0),(1,1)\\).\nThe equation of \\(L\\) can be written as  \n\n\\[\nqb=pa+c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor integer points \\((a,b)\\) on \\(L\\) we have \\(a\\equiv a_0\\pmod q\\) for a fixed\nresidue \\(a_0\\); consequently the admissible \\(a\\)-coordinates form an arithmetic\nprogression with step \\(q\\).  Since \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), the number of possible\n\\(a\\)-values is at most \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n}{q}\\big\\rceil\\).\n\n*If \\(q\\ge2\\).*  Then \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n}{q}\\big\\rceil\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).\n\n*If \\(q=1\\).*  Equation (1) becomes \\(b=pa+c\\) with \\(|p|\\ge2\\) (the cases\n\\(p=0,1,-1\\) correspond to the forbidden directions).  For a fixed \\(a\\) the\nvalue of \\(b\\) is uniquely determined, so the points of \\(L\\cap S_n\\) are\nexactly those \\((a,pa+c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le pa+c\\le n+1-a\\).\nThe inequalities force  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1}{|p|+1}\\le\\frac{n+1}{3},\n\\]\n\nhence at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points as well.\n\nThus every sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM_n:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Lines attaining the bound)\n\nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in exactly \\(M_n\\) points.\nFrom the proof of Lemma 1 we must have \\(q=1\\) and \\(|p|=2\\); otherwise the\nprogression step would be at least \\(2\\) and we could not reach the bound.\nHence the reduced slope of \\(L\\) is either \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\).\nThe third possibility is slope \\(1\\), which also gives \\(M_n\\) points (it\ncorresponds to the case \\(p=1,q=1\\) but the line is not parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) because its intercept is different).\n\nA direct computation shows that the only lines of these three slopes that\ncontain \\(M_n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{(1)} &: b=a,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{(-2)}&: b=-2a+n+2,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{(-\\frac12)}&: 2b+a=n+2 .\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nNo other line of the same slope can contain as many points, because moving the\nintercept up or down removes at least one lattice point from the triangle.\nThus the three lines in (3) are the **only** sunny lines attaining the bound\n\\(M_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Reduction to a configuration with only vertical non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(S_n\\) and contain\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, i.e.\n each is parallel to one of the three directions\n\\(\\mathbf v_1=(1,0),\\ \\mathbf v_2=(0,1),\\ \\mathbf v_3=(1,1)\\).\n\nBecause there are three directions, at least one of them occurs among the\nnon‑sunny lines at least  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-k}{3}\\Big\\rceil\n\\]\n\ntimes.  The affine map  \n\n\\[\n\\Phi_{12}:(x,y)\\mapsto (y,x),\\qquad\n\\Phi_{13}:(x,y)\\mapsto (y,\\;n+1-x-y)\n\\]\n\npermutes the three directions and leaves the set \\(S_n\\) invariant.\nApplying the appropriate \\(\\Phi\\) we may assume that the direction occurring\n\\(t\\) times is the vertical direction \\(\\mathb