roved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **The point set.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\) define the anti‑diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\n   \\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **A convenient family of lines.**  \n   For every integer \\(t\\;(1\\le t\\le n)\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\;y=x+(n+1-2t) .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_t\\) has slope \\(1\\); hence it is **sunny** (it is not parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Moreover \\(L_t\\) passes through the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_t=(t,\\;n+1-t)\\in D_{\\,n+1},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. through the \\(t\\)-th point of the longest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).\n\n3. **Covering the whole set with \\(n\\) lines.**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *Take the \\(k\\) sunny lines*  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{n-k+1},L_{n-k+2},\\dots ,L_{n}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (these are distinct because they have distinct intercepts).  \n   *Take the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines*  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The family consisting of the lines in (1) together with those in (2) consists\n   of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; the lines in (2) are parallel to the\n   \\(y\\)-axis and therefore are **non‑sunny**, while the lines in (1) are sunny.\n   Hence the total number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n4. **Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and put \\(s=a+b\\).\n\n   *Case 1 – \\(a\\le n-k\\).*  \n  