dd** for every integer \\(\\sigma_i\\).  \n   Hence \\(x_i\\) is never an integer; consequently **no sunny line meets the anti‑diagonal \\(D_n\\) at an integer point**.  \n\n   But \\(|D_n|=n-1\\) and every point of \\(D_n\\) must be covered by one of the \\(n\\) lines.  The contradiction shows that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) for odd \\(n\\).**  \n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd) and suppose exactly one line is non‑sunny.  \n   As in Lemma 2, the \\(n\\) lines must occupy all points of the longest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n   The unique non‑sunny line can be taken vertical, say \\(x=c\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are not vertical; each meets the next longest diagonal \\(D_n\\) in at most one point.  \n   Since \\(|D_n|=n-1\\), they must meet **all** points of \\(D_n\\).  \n   A non‑vertical line that meets both \\(D_{n+1}\\) and \\(D_n\\) must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), which is forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence such a configuration cannot exist; therefore \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for odd \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Construction for all admissible values.**  \n   Let \\(m=n-k\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines (so \\(0\\le m\\le n\\)).  \n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  Use the vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   (If \\(m=0\\) there are none.)  \n\n   *Sunny lines.*  For each \\(t=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t}{m+1}\\,x +\\Bigl(n+1-\\frac{t(m+2)}{m+1}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{t}{m+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), undefined, or \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\((a,b)=(m+t,\\;k+1-t)\\) into (4) gives\n   \\[\n   k+1-t = -\\frac{t}{m+1}(m+t)+n+1-\\frac{t(m+2)}{m+1},\n   \\]\n   which simplifies to an identity; therefore each \\(L_t\\) passes through the lattice point \\((m+t,\\;k+1-t)\\in S_n\\).  \n\n   The point \\((m+t,\\;k+1-t)\\) lies on the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1-(t-1)}\\); consequently \\(L_t\\) also contains every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a>m\\) and \\(b\\le k+1-(a-m)\\).  \n   Together the vertical lines (covering all points with \\(a\\le m\\)) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover **all** points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Special cases.*  \n   - \\(k=0\\): only the \\(n\\) vertical lines (3) are used.  \n   - \\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) odd: set \\(m=0\\) in (4); the lines become \\(y=-t x + (n+1-t)\\) for \\(t=1,\\dots ,n\\), which are all sunny and, by the same calculation as above, cover \\(S_n\\).  \n   - \\(k=n-1\\) with \\(n\\) odd is excluded by the impossibility proved in step 4.  \n   - \\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) even is excluded by the impossibility proved in step 3.\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing all points of \\(S_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd : }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\;}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
