h the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the set \\(\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid k\\le n,\\;k\\equiv1\\pmod 2\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem.**  \n   Choose \\(m=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n   \\]\n   After deleting the points whose first coordinate is \\(\\le m\\) we are left with the\n   triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n   the set \\(S_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n2. **A necessary parity condition.**  \n   For a sunny line the three forbidden directions are horizontal,\n   vertical and slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Consequently a sunny line meets each anti‑diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=s\\},\\qquad 2\\le s\\le k+1,\n   \\]\n\n   in **at most one** lattice point.  \n   Since \\(|D_{k+1}|=k\\), the \\(k\\) lines must meet the longest anti‑diagonal\n   \\(D_{k+1}\\) in exactly one point each.  \n   Write these points as  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;k+1-i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   If a line also meets the next anti‑diagonal \\(D_k\\) in a lattice point,\n   that point must be \\((j,k-j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i,i+1\\) (otherwise the line would be\n   vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Thus we need a bijection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   such that \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i+1\\) for every \\(i\\).\n   Such a permutation exists **iff** \\(k\\) is odd (a short proof is given in\n   Lemma 2).  When \\(k\\) is even the required bijection does not exist,\n   hence a covering by \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible.\n\n3. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k=2r+1\\) (so \\(r\\ge1\\)).  \n   Define for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)\n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=-\\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x+(k+1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{k+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), never undefined and never \\(-1\\);\n   therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n   For a fixed anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\;(2\\le s\\le k+1)\\) solving the system  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   y=-\\dfrac{i}{k+1}x+(k+1),\\\\[2mm]\n   y=s-x,\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   gives  \n\n   \\[\n   x=s-\\frac{i(s-1)}{k+1-i}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\gcd(i,k+1-i)=1\\) (the two numbers differ by an odd integer),\n   the denominator divides the numerator, so the right–hand side of (3) is an\n   integer.  Hence the intersection point belongs to \\(D_s\\).\n   For a fixed \\(s\\) the map \\(i\\mapsto x\\) is injective; consequently the\n   \\(k\\) points obtained are exactly the \\(k\\) lattice points of \\(D_s\\).\n   Thus every anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) is completely covered, and all points of\n   \\(S_k\\) lie on the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n   *If \\(k=0\\)* we simply take the \\(n\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines\n   \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n   *If \\(k\\) is odd*, choose \\(m=n-k\\) vertical non‑sunny lines\n   \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) and cover the remaining triangle \\(S_k\\) with the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) constructed in step 3.  \n   *If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k>0\\)*, Lemma 2 shows that the triangle \\(S_k\\) cannot\n   be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines; consequently no configuration with exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\qquad (n\\ge3)\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden\ndirections: horizontal \\((0)\\), vertical \\((\\infty)\\) and slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem  \n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nDenote by \\(m=n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\nEvery non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\nBy permuting the coordinate axes (swap \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) or reflect in the line\n\\(x+y=0\\)) we may assume that all non‑sunny lines are **vertical**; this does\nnot change the number of sunny lines nor the set \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence we may write the non‑sunny lines as  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is \\(\\le m\\) are already covered.\nThe uncovered points are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nU_{m}&=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge m+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\\\\n     &=\\{(a',b)\\mid a'=a-m\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le n-m+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus, after the translation \\((a',b)\\mapsto(a,b)=(a'+m,b)\\), the set \\(U_m\\) is\nexactly  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad k=n-m .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\nthe triangle \\(S_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\nThe problem is therefore reduced to the following.\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge1\\) decide whether the lattice set  \n> \\[\n> S_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\}\n> \\]\n> can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A necessary parity condition  \n\nFor \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_s:=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=s\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the *anti‑diagonal* of level \\(s\\); it contains \\(s-1\\) points.\nA sunny line is not parallel to any anti‑diagonal, hence it meets each\n\\(D_s\\) in **at most one** lattice point.\n\nThe longest anti‑diagonal is \\(D_{k+1}\\) and it contains exactly \\(k\\) points,\nnamely  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;k+1-i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf a covering by \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, each line must contain one of the\npoints \\(P_i\\); otherwise some point of \\(D_{k+1}\\) would remain uncovered.\n\nAssume a line \\(L_i\\) containing \\(P_i\\) also meets the next anti‑diagonal\n\\(D_k\\) in a lattice point \\(Q_i=(j,k-j)\\).  \nBecause \\(L_i\\) is not vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), the\ncoordinates \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nj\\neq i,\\qquad j\\neq i+1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n* \\(j=i\\) would give a vertical line,\n* \\(j=i+1\\) would give a horizontal line,\n* \\(j=i-1\\) would give slope \\(-1\\).\n\nThus the points of \\(D_k\\) that are used by the covering give a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\n\\]\n\nwith the property \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i,\\ \\sigma(i)\\neq i+1\\) for every \\(i\\).\nThe existence of such a permutation is characterised by the following\nlemma.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Permutation without neighbours)\n\nLet \\(k\\ge2\\).  \nThere exists a bijection \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) satisfying\n\\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i+1\\) for all \\(i\\) **iff** \\(k\\) is odd.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(k=2r\\) (even).  \nConsider the element \\(i=1\\). Condition (4) forces \\(\\sigma(1)=r+1\\) (the only\nvalue different from \\(1\\) and \\(2\\)).  \nNow look at \\(i=r+1\\); the only admissible image is \\(1\\), which is already\nused.  Hence no such bijection exists when \\(k\\) is even.\n\nConversely, let \\(k=2r+1\\) (odd).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=\n\\begin{cases}\ni+r+1 &\\text{if }1\\le i\\le r,\\\\[2mm]\ni-r   &\\text{if }r+1\\le i\\le 2r+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nOne checks directly that \\(\\sigma\\) maps \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) onto\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and that \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i,\\sigma(i)\\neq i+1\\) for every\n\\(i\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain:\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even, a covering of \\(S_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible.*\n\nIndeed, the points of \\(D_{k+1}\\) must be distributed among the \\(k\\) lines,\nand the argument above shows that the required permutation \\(\\sigma\\) does not\nexist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2r+1\\) (odd).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=-\\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x+(k+1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  \nThe slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{k+1}\\) is never \\(0\\) (because \\(i\\neq0\\)), never\nundefined (the denominator is non‑zero), and never \\(-1\\) (that would require\n\\(i=k+1\\), which does not occur).  Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the\n\\(L_i\\) are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n*Intersection with an anti‑diagonal.*  \nFix \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) and solve the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\ny=-\\dfrac{i}{k+1}x+(k+1),\\\\[2mm]\ny=s-x .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe unique solution is  \n\n\\[\nx=s-\\frac{i(s-1)}{k+1-i}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd\\bigl(i,k+1-i\\bigr)=1\\) (the two numbers differ by the odd\ninteger \\(k+1-2i\\)), the denominator divides the numerator; consequently the\nright–hand side of (7) is an integer.  Substituting this integer into\n\\(y=s-x\\) yields an integer \\(y\\).  Hence the intersection point belongs to the\nanti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(s\\) the map \\(i\\mapsto x\\) given by (7) is injective (different\nvalues of \\(i\\) give different slopes, therefore different intersection points).\nSince \\(|D_s|=s-1=k-(k+1-s)\\) and we have exactly \\(k\\) values of \\(i\\), the\n\\(k\\) points obtained are precisely the \\(k\\) lattice points of \\(D_s\\).\n\nThus **every** anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) is completely covered by the family\n\\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\); consequently all points of the triangle \\(S_k\\) lie on\nthose \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the original problem  \n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  They are\npairwise distinct and cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Case \\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \nPut \\(m=n-k\\) and use the vertical lines (1) for the non‑sunny part.\nThe remaining points form the triangle \\(S_k\\); by Section 3 they are covered\nby the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).*  \nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines existed.\nRemoving the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny vertical lines leaves the triangle \\(S_k\\),\nwhich would then be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines – contradicting the\nimpossibility proved in Section 2.  Hence no such configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Answer  \n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, when \\(n\\) is even the largest possible value of \\(k\\) is\n\\(n-1\\); when \\(n\\) is odd the largest possible value is \\(n\\).  This completes\nthe determination of all possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
