s, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction.**  \n   Choose the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   After removing the points whose first coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\) we are left with the\n   triangular set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n   the set \\(S_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n2. **Parity obstruction.**  \n   A sunny line meets each anti‑diagonal  \n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=s\\},\\qquad 2\\le s\\le k+1,\n   \\]\n   in at most one lattice point.  \n   The longest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\) contains the \\(k\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,k+1-i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently every covering by \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain exactly one of the\n   points \\(P_i\\) on each line.  \n   If a line also contains a point of the next anti‑diagonal \\(D_k\\) then,\n   because the line must not be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\),\n   the second point must be \\((\\sigma(i),k-\\sigma(i))\\) with a map  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\n   \\qquad\\text{satisfying}\\qquad\n   \\sigma(i)\\neq i,\\ \\sigma(i)\\neq i-1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   **Lemma 1.** Such a