dulo its own modulus \\(m_i\\)),\n   for a given \\(s\\) the condition \\(m_i\\mid (s-b_i)\\) holds precisely for the\n   \\(s-1\\) indices \\(i\\) with \\(m_i\\le s-1\\).  Those indices are exactly\n   \\(\\{\\,i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1-i\\le s-1\\,\\}\\), i.e. the set\n   \\(\\{\\,i\\mid i\\ge k+2-s\\,\\}\\) of size \\(s-1\\).\n\n   Hence **for each anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) the lines \\(L_i\\) with\n   \\(i\\ge k+2-s\\) intersect \\(D_s\\) in distinct lattice points, and the set of\n   intersection points is precisely the whole \\(D_s\\).**  In particular every\n   point of the triangle \\(S_k\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n4. **Completion of the original problem.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   *\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n   Use the vertical lines (1) for the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and the\n   sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) constructed in step 3 for the remaining\n   triangle \\(S_k\\).  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\n   which are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at\n   least one of them.\n\n   *\\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).*  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines existed.\n   Removing the \\(n-k\\) vertical non‑sunny lines leaves the triangle \\(S_k\\),\n   which would then be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines – impossible by Lemma 1.\n   Hence no such configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Answer** – The possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nFor every admissible \\(k\\) an explicit construction has been given, and for\neven \\(k>0\\) a rigorous impossibility proof has been provided.\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: