            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have not been able to produce a completely rigorous construction for all odd values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the *necessary* part of the answer:\n\n*If a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then  \n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ is odd.}\n\\]*  \n\nI also give explicit constructions for the two extreme admissible values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) (the latter works for every \\(n\\ge 3\\)). The existence of configurations for all odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) remains unproved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular set.**  \n   Choose the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   After deleting the points whose first coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\) we are left with the integer triangle  \n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n2. **Parity obstruction (even \\(k\\) impossible).**  \n   *Anti‑diagonals.* For \\(s=2,\\dots ,k+1\\) let  \n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=s\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   A sunny line meets each \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** lattice point.  \n   The longest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\) contains the \\(k\\) points  \n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,k+1-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Consequently any covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain exactly one of the points \\(P_i\\) on each line.\n\n   *Matching with the next anti‑diagonal.*  \n   The next anti‑diagonal \\(D_k\\) consists of the points  \n   \\[\n   Q_j=(j,k-j)\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k-1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   If a line contains both a point \\(P_i\\) and a point \\(Q_j\\) then \\(j\\neq i\\) (otherwise the line would be vertical) and \\(j\\neq i-1\\) (otherwise the line would be horizontal).  \n   Hence the set of lines that meet \\(D_k\\) yields an **injective map**\n   \\[\n   \\sigma:\\{i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\mid\\text{the line through }P_i\\text{ meets }D_k\\}\n          \\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   satisfying\n   \\[\n   \\sigma(i)\\neq i,\\qquad \\sigma(i)\\neq i-1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Lemma 1.* Such a map \\(\\sigma\\) exists **iff** \\(k\\) is odd.  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose \\(k\\) is even, write \\(k=2r\\).  \n   Take the subset \\(S=\\{1,2,\\dots ,r\\}\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \n   For every \\(i\\in S\\) the forbidden values \\(\\{i,i-1\\}\\) lie in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,r\\}\\).  \n   Hence the neighbour set \\(N(S)\\) is contained in \\(\\{r+1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\), which has size \\(r-1\\).  \n   Since \\(|S|=r>|N(S)|\\), Hall’s marriage theorem shows that no injective map satisfying (7) can exist.  \n\n   Conversely, let \\(k=2r+1\\) be odd. Define\n   \\[\n   \\sigma(i)=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   i+r      & (1\\le i\\le r),\\\\[2mm]\n   i-r-1    & (r+2\\le i\\le 2r+1).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   One checks directly that \\(\\sigma\\) maps \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\setminus\\{r+1\\}\\) bijectively onto \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and satisfies (7). ∎  \n\n   Because the existence of a covering by \\(k\\) sunny lines would produce a map \\(\\sigma\\) as above, Lemma 1 shows that **no covering exists when \\(k\\) is even and \\(k>0\\).**\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   They are all parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and together cover every point of the required triangle. Hence \\(k=0\\) is admissible.\n\n4. **Existence for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Keep the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines (1) and add a single sunny line, for instance\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y=2x-(n+1).\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   The line \\(L\\) passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) of the reduced triangle \\(T_1\\); together with the vertical lines it covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Thus \\(k=1\\) is admissible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Partial progress for larger odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Lemma 1 supplies the combinatorial backbone that any admissible configuration must realise a map \\(\\sigma\\) of the form (8). The difficulty lies in turning such a map into explicit sunny lines that also intersect every lower anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\)) in lattice points.  \n   The construction of such lines remains open; the obstacle is to choose slopes so that the intersection\n   \\[\n   L_i\\cap D_s=\\bigl(x_{i,s},\\,s-x_{i,s}\\bigr)\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n   is integral for **all** \\(s\\). The parity argument guarantees that a solution should exist for every odd \\(k\\), but a concrete family of equations has not been produced here.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Answer (partial).**  \nThe necessary condition is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd}\\;}\n\\]\n\nand the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are indeed attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nThe existence of configurations for all odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\