is, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have not been able to produce a completely rigorous construction for all odd values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the *necessary* part of the answer:\n\n*If a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then  \n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ is odd.}\n\\]*  \n\nI also give explicit constructions for the two extreme admissible values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) (the latter works for every \\(n\\ge 3\\)). The existence of configurations for all odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) remains unproved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular set.**  \n   Choose the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   After deleting the points whose first coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\) we are left with the integer triangle  \n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n2. **Parity obstruction (even \\(k\\) impossible).**  \n   *Anti‑diagonals.* For \\(s=2,\\dots ,k+1\\) let  \n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=s\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   A sunny line meets each \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** lattice point.  \n   The longest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\) contains the \\(k\\) points  \n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,k+1-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Consequently any cove