ny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – The previous answer was **incorrect**.  \nThe counter‑example for \\(n=4,\\;k=4\\) is valid: a sunny line can contain at most  \n\\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\) points of the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nso for \\(n=4\\) a sunny line contains at most two points.  \nFour sunny lines can therefore cover at most eight points, whereas \\(|T_4|=10\\).  \nHence \\(k=n\\) is impossible for \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n**b. New answer** –  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{For }n=3:& k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{For }n\\ge 4:& k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn words, for \\(n=3\\) every \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(3\\) is attainable, while for\n\\(n\\ge4\\) all values \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) are attainable and \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nThe proof consists of two parts:\n\n* **Upper bound** – a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\); consequently \\(k=n\\) cannot be realised when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n* **Construction** – for every admissible \\(k\\) we exhibit an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A geometric limitation for sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line, written as \\(y=mx+b\\) with\n\\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) rational (otherwise \\(L\\) contains at most one lattice\npoint). 