\\(q>0\\).\nFor a lattice point \\((x,y)\\in L\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ny= \\frac{p}{q}x+b\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x+y=\n\\Bigl(1+\\frac{p}{q}\\Bigr)x+b=\n\\frac{p+q}{q}\\,x+b .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\in T_n\\) then \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  From (2) we see that when\n\\(x\\) is increased by \\(q\\), the sum \\(x+y\\) increases by \\(p+q\\ge 2\\)\n(because \\(p\\neq -q\\) and both \\(p,q\\) are non‑zero).  Hence the points of\n\\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression in the variable\n\\(x\\) with step \\(q\\); consequently their number does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-b}{p+q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus every sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, by (3) each of them would contain at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) the right‑hand side is strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\);\ntherefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist when \\(n\\ge4\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for any \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=x+i .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines (4) have slope \\(1\\); consequently they are sunny.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(S_i\\) exactly when \\(b-a=i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Points not covered by the sunny lines  \n\nA point of \\(T_n\\) that is not on any \\(S_i\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a\\le n+1-k,\\qquad 2b\\le n+1-k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence every uncovered point belongs to the square  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m,\\;1\\le b\\le m\\Bigr\\},\n\\qquad \nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Non‑sunny covering of the remaining points  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le m\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore non‑sunny.\nBecause of (6), any point of \\(R_k\\) has its abscissa \\(a\\) in the set\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) and consequently lies on one of the lines \\(V_c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.4  Counting the lines  \n\nThe construction uses  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\);\n* \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\).\n\nFrom the definition of \\(m\\),\n\n\\[\nm=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n-k .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m<n-k\\) we simply add the extra vertical lines\n\\(x=m+1,\\dots ,n-k\\); they do not affect the covering because every point of\n\\(T_n\\) is already covered by either a sunny line or one of the first \\(m\\)\nvertical lines.  Thus the total number of distinct lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.5  Verification  \n\n*All points are covered.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) either satisfies \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and\nbelongs to a sunny line \\(S_i\\), or satisfies \\(b-a\\ge k\\) and, by (6), has\n\\(a\\le m\\); consequently it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n*Exactly \\(k\\) lines are sunny.*  \nOnly the lines \\(S_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); all the vertical lines have slope\n\\(\\infty\\).  Hence the number of sunny lines equals the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\nTherefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have produced a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) is covered by the following families,\nwhich contain respectively \\(0,1,2,3\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{family of three lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 & \\{x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\}\\\\\n1 & \\{y=x,\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\\\\n2 & \\{y=x,\\;y=x+1,\\;x=2\\}\\\\\n3 & \\{y=x,\\;y=x+1,\\;y=2x-3\\}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\n* For \\(n=3\\) every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is attainable.  \n* For any \\(n\\ge4\\) the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\);\n  every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realised by the\n  construction of §4.  \n* The value \\(k=n\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge4\\) by the upper‑bound argument\n  of §3.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the\nboxed answer. ∎"
