---------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)  \n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, by (2.1) each could contain at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) the right‑hand side is strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\);\ntherefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) does not exist. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume \\(k=n-1\\).  Only one non‑sunny line is available.\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(2,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(3,1),\\qquad P_{3}=(3,2),\n\\]\n\nall belonging to \\(T_{n}\\) when \\(n\\ge5\\).  \nNo non‑sunny line can contain the three simultaneously:\n\n* the line through \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) is horizontal (\\(y=1\\)) and misses \\(P_{3}\\);\n* the line through \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{3}\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is sunny;\n* the line through \\(P_{2}\\) and \\(P_{3}\\) is vertical (\\(x=3\\)) and misses \\(P_{1}\\).\n\nThus a single non‑sunny line cannot cover all points with \\(a>b\\); consequently\n\\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge5\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) satisfying the conditions of the final answer.\n\n#### 5.1  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\;y=x+i .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines (5.1) have slope \\(1\\); hence they are sunny.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on some \\(S_{i}\\) exactly when  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.2  Points not covered by the sunny lines  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) does **not** satisfy (5.2) then either  \n\n\\[\n\\text{(i)}\\;b-a\\ge k,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\n\\text{(ii)}\\;a>b