duce the vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   V_{c}:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,\\dots ,m_{1}),\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   and the horizontal lines  \n   \\[\n   H_{c}:\\;y=c\\qquad(c=1,\\dots ,m_{2}).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Both families are non‑sunny.  By (6) every point of \\(T_{n}\\) that is not on a sunny line belongs to at least one of the lines (7) or (8).\n\n   *Counting the lines.*  \n   The construction uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(m_{1}+m_{2}\\) non‑sunny lines.  A short parity check shows\n   \\[\n   m_{1}+m_{2}=n-k .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Indeed,\n   \\[\n   m_{1}+m_{2}\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   =n-k .\n   \\]\n   Consequently the total number of distinct lines is\n   \\[\n   k+(m_{1}+m_{2})=k+(n-k)=n .\n   \\]\n   Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line.  Hence the construction works for every \\(k\\) listed in the final answer.\n\n5. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  The set \\(T_{3}\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   A configuration with three sunny lines would require three distinct lines of slope \\(1\\), but only two such lines intersect \\(T_{3}\\); therefore \\(k\\le1\\).  Both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realised by the construction of step 4.\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  Here \\(|T_{4}|=10\\).  The construction yields \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \n   The case \\(k=4\\) is impossible because of the bound (2).  Thus the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(n\\ge5\\).*  The construction gives all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\); the impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) has been proved in steps 2–3.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many points of \\(T_{n}\\) can lie on a sunny line?\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write it as \\(y=mx+b\\) with rational slope\n\\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms, \\(q>0\\).  Because the line is sunny we have\n\\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).\n\nAll lattice points of \\(L\\) are\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_{0}+tq,\\;y_{0}+tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\\[\nx+y=(x_{0}+y_{0})+t(p+q).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|p+q|=1\\) then \\(p+q=\\pm1\\).  The coprime solutions of this equation are\n\\((p,q)=(1,0),(0,1),(-1,2),(2,-1),\\dots\\).  The first two give a horizontal\nor a vertical line, the third gives slope \\(-1\\); all three are **non‑sunny**.\nThus for a sunny line we must have \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).\n\nInside the triangle \\(T_{n}\\) the sum \\(x+y\\) can take only the integers\n\\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) – exactly \\(n\\) different values.  From (2.2) the values\nrealised on \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression with step \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\); therefore\nthe number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) lying on \\(L\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus **any sunny line meets \\(T_{n}\\) in at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\) points.** ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bounds for the number of sunny lines\n\n#### 3.1  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, (2.3) gives a maximal total of\n\\(n\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points, whereas \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\\[\nn\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor<\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\nso a configuration with \\(k=n\\) does not exist. ∎\n\n#### 3.2  Exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (\\(k=n-1\\)) for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume we have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line.\nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (the longest such line is \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) or\n\\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Using (2.3) for the sunny lines we obtain the maximal possible\ncoverage\n\\[\n(n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+n .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2m+1\\).  Then (3.1) equals\n\\[\n(2m)\\,(m+1)+2m+1=2m^{2}+3m+1< (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\nHence the points of \\(T_{n}\\) cannot all be covered.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, write \\(n=2m\\).  Then (3.1) equals\n\\[\n(2m-1)\\,m+2m=2m^{2}+m=m(2m+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\nEquality can hold only when every sunny line attains the bound (2.3), i.e. when\n\\(|p+q|=2\\).  The only sunny slopes with \\(|p+q|=2\\) are \\(m=1\\); consequently\nall sunny lines must have slope \\(1\\) and are of the form \\(y=x+c\\) with distinct\nconstants \\(c\\).\n\nThese parallel lines partition \\(T_{n}\\) according to the value of\n\\(b-a\\).  With \\(n-1\\) such lines we miss exactly one value of \\(b-a\\); the\ncorresponding points form a diagonal of slope \\(1\\) and contain\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\ge3\\) points because \\(n\\ge5\\).\nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most one\npoint of that diagonal, so the remaining points cannot be covered.\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) does not exist for \\(n\\ge5\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy the description in the final answer.\n\n#### 4.1  Sunny lines  \n\nFor \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\\[\nS_{i}:\\;y=x+i .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nThese lines have slope \\(1\\) and are therefore sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on some \\(S_{i}\\) exactly when\n\\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).\n\n#### 4.2  Points not yet covered  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) does **not** satisfy the above, then either  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge k\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad a>b .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain the bounds  \n\n\\[\na\\le m_{1}:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\quad\\text{in the first case},\n\\qquad\nb\\le m_{2}:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\quad\\text{in the second case}.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.3  Non‑sunny covering  \n\nIntroduce the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_{c}:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,\\dots ,m_{1}),\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{c}:\\;y=c\\qquad(c=1,\\dots ,m_{2}).\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\nBoth families are non‑sunny.  By (4.3) every point of \\(T_{n}\\) that is not on a\nsunny line belongs to at least one of the lines (4.4) or (4.5).\n\n#### 4.4  Counting the lines  \n\nThe construction uses  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{0},\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\);\n* \\(m_{1}+m_{2}\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nA direct parity computation shows  \n\n\\[\nm_{1}+m_{2}=n-k .\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\nm_{1}+m_{2}\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n=n-k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the total number of distinct lines equals  \n\n\\[\nk+(m_{1}+m_{2})=k+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one\nline.  Hence the construction works for every \\(k\\) listed in the final answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).**  The set \\(T_{3}\\) consists of \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  At most one of the three required lines can be sunny (otherwise two distinct\n  lines of slope \\(1\\) would be needed, but only two such lines intersect\n  \\(T_{3}\\)).  Both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realised by the construction of\n  Section 4.\n\n* **\\(n=4\\).**  The construction yields \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \n  The bound (2) excludes \\(k=4\\).  Hence the admissible set is\n  \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(n\\ge5\\).**  Sections 2–4 give that all integers \\(k\\) with\n  \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) are attainable and that \\(k=n-1,n\\) are impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a family\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\] ∎"
