ctly when\n\\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).\n\n#### 4.2  Points not yet covered  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) does **not** satisfy the above, then either  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge k\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad a>b .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain the bounds  \n\n\\[\na\\le m_{1}:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\quad\\text{in the first case},\n\\qquad\nb\\le m_{2}:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\quad\\text{in the second case}.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.3  Non‑sunny covering  \n\nIntroduce the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_{c}:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,\\dots ,m_{1}),\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{c}:\\;y=c\\qquad(c=1,\\dots ,m_{2}).\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\nBoth families are non‑sunny.  By (4.3) every point of \\(T_{n}\\) that is not on a\nsunny line belongs to at least one of the lines (4.4) or (4.5).\n\n#### 4.4  Counting the lines  \n\nThe construction uses  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{0},\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\);\n* \\(m_{1}+m_{2}\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nA direct parity computation shows  \n\n\\[\nm_{1}+m_{2}=n-k .\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\nm_{1}+m_{2}\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n=n-k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the total number of distinct lines equals  \n\n\\[\nk+(m_{1}+m_{2})=k+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one\nline.  Hence the construction works for every \\(k\\) listed in the final answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).**  The set \\(T_{3}\\) consists of \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  At most one of the three required lines can be sunny (otherwise two distinct\n  lines of slope \\(1\\) would be needed, but only two such lines intersect\n  \\(T_{3}\\)).  Both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realised by the construction of\n  Section 4.\n\n* **\\(n=4\\).**  The construction yields \\(