ide the triangle \\(T_{n}\\) the sum \\(x+y\\) can take only the \\(n\\) values\n\\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  From (2.2) the values realised on the line form an\narithmetic progression with step \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\); consequently the number of\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\) on the line is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bounds for the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### 3.1  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, (2.3) gives a maximal total of  \n\n\\[\nn\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_{n}|\\qquad(n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\nso a configuration with \\(k=n\\) does not exist.\n\n#### 3.2  Exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume we have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line.\nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  Using (2.3) for the sunny lines we obtain the\nmaximal possible coverage  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+n .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2m+1\\); then the right–hand side of (3.1) equals\n\\(2m^{2}+3m+1< (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, write \\(n=2m\\); then (3.1) equals\n\\(2m^{2}+m=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  Equality would require every sunny line to have\n\\(|p+q|=2\\), i.e. slope \\(1\\).  All such lines are parallel and partition\n\\(T_{n}\\) according to the value of \\(b-a\\).  With \\(n-1\\) parallel lines one\nvalue of \\(b-a\\) is missing; the corresponding points form a diagonal of slope\n\\(1\\) containing \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\ge3\\) points (because\n\\(n\\ge5\\)).  A non‑sunny line can meet that diagonal in at most one point, so\nthe missing points cannot be covered.  Hence a configuration with\n\\(k=n-1\\) does not exist for \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy the description in the final answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k\\le n-3\\) (including all odd \\(n\\))\n\nDefine the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) not lying on any \\(S_i\\) we have either  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge k\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad a>b .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(b-a\\ge k\\)*, from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a+k\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a\\le\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor=:m_{1}.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nThus such points are covered by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,\\dots ,m_{1}).\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(a>b\\)*, then \\(2b+1\\le n+1\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, when \\(a>m_{1}\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n-m_{1}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently every point with \\(a>b\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Big\\rceil=:m_{2}.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\n\nHence these points are covered by the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_c:\\;y=c\\qquad(c=1,\\dots ,m_{2}).\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\n\nBecause  \n\n\\[\nm_{1}+m_{2}\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n=n-k,\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\n\nthe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk\\;( \\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(m_{1}+m_{2})\\;( \\text{non‑sunny})=n .\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(k\\le n-3\\) we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny ones covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nHere we need exactly two non‑sunny lines.  \nTake the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-3)      \\quad(\\text{there 