                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bounds.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   Proof uses the fact that for a sunny line the quantity \\(x+y\\) changes by at least \\(2\\) when moving from one lattice point of the line to the next.\n\n   *Lemma 2.* If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny then they cannot cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) for any \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n   Indeed, by Lemma 1 the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_{n}|\\qquad (n\\ge4).\n   \\]\n\n   *Lemma 3.* For \\(n\\ge5\\) a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is impossible.  \n   One non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\); together with Lemma 1 we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+n\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge5).\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(k\\le n-2\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\); for \\(n=4\\) the bound of Lemma 3 does not apply, and for \\(n=3\\) the bound of Lemma 2 is not strict.\n\n3. **Constructions.**  \n   *Construction A (all \\(k\\le n-3\\)).*  \n   For any \\(k\\le n-3\\) define the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{1}:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\