ery point of \\(T_{n}\\) is on at least one of the lines (4.1)–(4.4).\n\nBecause  \n\n\\[\nm_{1}+m_{2}\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n=n-k,\n\\]\n\nthe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(m_{1}+m_{2})\\;(\\text{non‑sunny})=n .\n\\]\n\nHence for every \\(k\\le n-3\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=n-2\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\))\n\nTake the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-3)\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\n(\\(n-2\\) of them) and the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nV_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad H_1:\\;y=1 .\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) one of the following holds:\n\n* \\(a\\le1\\) – the point lies on \\(V_{1}\\);\n* \\(b\\le1\\) – the point lies on \\(H_{1}\\);\n* \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) – then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\),\n  so the point lies on one of the lines \\(S_i\\).\n\nThus all points are covered and exactly \\(k=n-2\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n#### 4.3  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n* \\(n=3\\).  \n  The families constructed in Sections 4.1 and 4.2 give \\(k=0,1,2\\).  \n  Adding the single sunny line \\(y=2x-3\\) (which passes only through \\((2,1)\\))\n  yields a configuration with \\(k=3\\). Hence every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is\n  attainable.\n\n* \\(n=4\\).  \n  Sections 4.1 and 4.2 provide \\(k=0,1,2\\).  \n  The four lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\;y=x+1,\\;y=x+2,\\;x=1\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct, three of them are sunny, and they cover all points of \\(T_{4}\\);\n  therefore \\(k=3\\) is also possible. No configuration with \\(k=4\\) exists by\n  Lemma 2.\n\nThus the admissible values for \\(n=3,4\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility results of Lemmas 2 and 3 with the explicit\nconstructions of Sections 4.1–4.3, we obtain the complete description of the\npossible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,