very sunny line contains at most }\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\n   \\text{ points of }T_{n}.}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Necessary restriction on \\(k\\).**  \n   *All‑sunny case.*  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 4\\) one has \\(n\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_{n}|\\); hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny\n   lines cannot exist when \\(n\\ge4\\). (For \\(n=3\\) equality holds, so\n   \\(k=n\\) is possible.)\n\n   *Exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.*  \n   One non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\)\n   (a vertical, a horizontal or a line of slope \\(-1\\) reaches the whole\n   diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Using (2.3),\n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+n\n   \\ge |T_{n}|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality is an equality for even \\(n\\) and is strict for odd\n   \\(n\\); therefore the bound does **not** forbid the existence of a\n   configuration with \\(k=n-1\\).  In fact such a configuration can be\n   built for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (see the construction in §5).\n\n   *Conclusion.*  \n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-1\\); for \\(n=3\\) we may also have\n   \\(k=3\\).\n\n4. **Constructions.**  \n   We exhibit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that realise every admissible\n   value of \\(k\\).\n\n   *4.1  \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \n   Choose the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   a:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor ,\\qquad\n   b:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Add the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,a),\n   \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   and the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,b).\n   \\tag{4.4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b=n-k\