se, because the bound\n   \\(R_{k}\\le k\\,M(n)\\) is strict for \\(k\\neq \\frac{n}{2}\\).\n\n   *The exceptional case \\(k=\\frac{n}{2}\\) (even \\(n\\)).*  \n   Suppose \\(n=2m\\) and \\(k=m\\).  \n   After removing the \\(n-k=m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) the uncovered points are\n\n   \\[\n   R_{m}= \\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Each sunny line can contain at most \\(M(n)=m\\) points, so to cover \\(R_{m}\\) points we\n   would need exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines each containing **exactly** \\(m\\) points.\n   The only sunny lines that can contain \\(m\\) points of the triangle are the lines\n   of slope \\(-2\\) (they intersect the triangle in the points\n   \\((i,\\,2m+2-2i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\)).  \n   All such lines are parallel to each other, hence they are not distinct – a\n   contradiction.  \n   Consequently no configuration with \\(k=\\frac{n}{2}\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining Lemma 2 with the explicit constructions and the impossibility of the\n   exceptional even case yields the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \nN_{n}=|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points on a sunny line)\n\nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with reduced rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) (\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) lies on the line then  \n\n\\[\nb=ma+c\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;qb=pa+qc .\n\\]\n\nHence the integer points on the line form an arithmetic progression with step\n\\((q,p)\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the progression can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints; otherwise two consecutive points would already violate th