xis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have a complete solution.  \n\n**b. Method sketch**  \n\n1. **The point set.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=N_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   It is the set of lattice points of a right‑isosceles triangle with legs of\n   length \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Maximum number of points on a sunny line (Lemma 1).**  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with reduced rational slope\n   \\(m=p/q\\;(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0)\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).\n   All integer points on the line are\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=(a_0+qt,\\;b_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n   \\]\n   where \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is any integer point on the line.\n   The sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(q+p\\) when \\(t\\) is increased by one:\n   \\[\n   (a+b)(t)= (a_0+b_0)+(q+p)t .\n   \\]\n   Because every point of \\(S_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\), the number of\n   admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q+p|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(q\\) and \\(p\\) have the same sign*, then \\(|q+p|\\ge 2\\) and (1) gives\n   at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n   *If \\(q\\) and \\(p\\) have opposite signs*, then one of the coordinates\n   decreases when \\(t\\) grows.  From the positivity conditions\n   \\(a_0+qt\\ge1,\\;b_0+pt\\ge1\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   t\\le\\frac{a_0-1}{|q|}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   t\\le\\frac{b_0-1}{|p|}.\n   \\]\n   Adding these two inequalities yields\n   \\[\n   2t\\le\\frac{a_0-1}{|q|}+\\frac{b_0-1}{|p|}\n          \\le\\frac{a_0+b_0-2}{\\min\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\n          \\le\\frac{n-1}{1}=n-1 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and again the line\n   contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Consequently every sunny line meets at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M(n)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\).  The bound is sharp:\n   * for odd \\(n\\) the line \\(y=x\\) contains \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}=M(n)\\) points;\n   * for even \\(n\\) the line \\(y=-2x+n+2\\) contains \\(\\frac{n}{2}=M(n)\\) points.\n\n3. **Counting bound (Lemma 2).**  \n   A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=c\\))\n   contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n   If a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\n   then by Lemma 1 and the above observation  \n\n   \\[\n   kM(n)+(n-k)n\\ge N_n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(M(n)=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and\n   \\(N_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) gives the *necessary* condition  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (4).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\n   them sunny, covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Use the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny and together cover  \n\n   \\[\n   C_{n-k}= \\sum_{c=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-c)\n          =\\frac{(n-k)(2n+2-(n-k+1))}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining triangle.*  \n   The uncovered points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   U_k=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a right‑isosceles triangle of size  \n\n   \\[\n   R_k=|U_k|=N_n-C_{n-k}= \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\