nct\n     \\(a\\)-coordinates and distinct \\(b\\)-coordinates; the differences of the coordinates would force the slope to be either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  \n   * Consequently the \\(n-3\\) remaining lines can cover at most \\(2(n-3)\\) interior points.  \n     Since \\(|I_n|=\\dfrac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\), we have\n     \\[\n       2(n-3)\\ge \\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\le 4 .\n     \\]\n     For \\(n\\ge5\\) the inequality fails, so the only way to cover all interior points with \\(n-3\\) lines is that **every** remaining line is non‑sunny (horizontal, vertical or diagonal).  \n   * For \\(n=4\\) we have \\(|I_4|=1\\); the single interior point \\((2,2)\\) can be covered by a non‑sunny line (for instance the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\)).  \n     Hence also for \\(n=4\\) no sunny line is needed among the remaining \\(n-3=1\\) line.  \n\n   Therefore for every \\(n\\ge4\\) an admissible configuration can contain at most one sunny line.  \n\n3. Combining the constructions of step 1 with the impossibility proved in step 2 yields the complete list of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(i)  \\(k=0\\) (all \\(n\\) lines non‑sunny).**  \nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) has second coordinate \\(b\\le n\\), hence lies on exactly one of these lines.\nAll lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore none is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(ii)  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line).**  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\ntogether with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_n:\\; \\text{the unique line through }(1,n).\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(b\\le n-1\\) are covered by the first \\(n-1\\) lines; the only point with\n\\(b=n\\) is \\((1,n)\\), which lies on \\(L_n\\).  Hence the condition is satisfied and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(iii)  \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\).**  \nThe six points of \\(S_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:\\;y=x      &&\\text{(sunny, contains }(1,1),(2,2)),\\\\\nL_2&:\\;y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac52 &&\\text{(sunny, contains }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\nL_3&:\\;x=1      &&\\text{(non‑sunny, contains }(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll six points belong to at least one of the three lines and exactly two of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(iv)  \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).**  \nAgain with the six points of \\(S_3\\) choose  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:\\;y=x      &&\\text{(sunny, contains }(1,1),(2,2)),\\\\\nL_2&:\\;y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac52 &&\\text{(sunny, contains }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\nL_3&:\\;y=-2x+5  &&\\text{(sunny, contains }(1,3),(2,1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach of the six points lies on one of these three sunny lines, so \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(v)  \\(n=3,\\;k=0\\).**  \nTake the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3\\); all are non‑sunny and together they cover \\(S_3\\).\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) can be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe prove that any admissible family of \\(n\\) distinct lines contains **at most one** sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nThe vertical line \\(x=1\\), the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) must belong to every admissible family.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) all have first coordinate \\(1\\).  \nIf a line contains two of them it is vertical, i.e. \\(x=1\\); otherwise each of those points would require a distinct line, exhausting all \\(n\\) lines and leaving no line for the remaining points.  \nHence \\(x=1\\) is forced.  \n\nThe same argument applied to the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) forces the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n\nThe points on the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) are \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nAny line containing two of them has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); such a line is non‑sunny.  \nIf we omitted the diagonal, each anti‑diagonal point would need its own line, again exceeding the available number.  \nThus the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is also forced. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Definition.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of *interior* points, i.e. the points of \\(S_n\\) that are not on the three forced lines.\nA straightforward count gives  \n\n\\[\n|I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAfter removing the three forced lines we have exactly \\(n-3\\) lines left, and they must cover all points of \\(I_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.**  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(I_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct interior points\n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) with \\(a_i,b_i\\ge2\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n\\).\nBecause the points are collinear, the slopes between any two of them are equal:\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\nAll differences \\(a_j-a_i\\) and \\(b_j-b_i\\) are non‑zero integers.\nIf the common slope were \\(0\\) the line would be horizontal; if it were undefined the line would be vertical; if it were \\(-1\\) the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nAll three possibilities are excluded by the definition of a sunny line.  \nHence a sunny line cannot pass through three interior points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 3.**  \nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) the \\(n-3\\) remaining lines can cover the set \\(I_n\\) only if **none** of them is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 2 each sunny line covers at most two interior points, whereas any non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) can cover at most \\(\\displaystyle\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\) interior points; in particular it can cover at least two when \\(n\\ge5\\).  \nIf among the \\(n-3\\) lines there were \\(s\\ge1\\) sunny lines, the total number of interior points they could cover would be at most  \n\n\\[\n2s+(n-3-s)\\cdot\\frac{n-2}{2}= \\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}2 -s\\Bigl(\\frac{n-2}{2}-2\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge5\\) we have \\(\\frac{n-2}{2}-2>0\\), so the right‑hand side is **strictly smaller** than \\(\\dfrac{(n-2)(n-3)}2=|I_n|\\) (by (2)).  \nThus the interior points could not all be covered – a contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 4.**  For \\(n=4\\) the interior set consists of the single point \\((2,2)\\); it can be covered by a non‑sunny line (e.g. the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\)).  Hence no sunny line is needed among the remaining \\(n-3=1\\) line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion of the impossibility part.**  \nBy Lemma 1 every admissible configuration contains the three forced non‑sunny lines.\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) Lemma 3 shows that the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines must all be non‑sunny; for \\(n=4\\) Lemma 4 gives the same conclusion.\nConsequently **at most one** of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny when \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nTherefore \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final answer\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the constructions of §1 give \\(k=0,1,2,3\\); larger \\(k\\) are impossible because we have only three lines.  \n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the constructions of §1 provide configurations with \\(k=0\\) and with \\(k=1\\); Lemma 1–4 prove that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the summary. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
