ose  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:\\;y=x      &&\\text{(sunny, contains }(1,1),(2,2)),\\\\\nL_2&:\\;y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac52 &&\\text{(sunny, contains }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\nL_3&:\\;y=-2x+5  &&\\text{(sunny, contains }(1,3),(2,1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach of the six points lies on one of these three sunny lines, so \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(iv)  \\(n=3,\\;k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).**  \nThe constructions of (i) and (ii) work for \\(n=3\\) as well, giving \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Forced non‑sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nThe vertical line \\(x=1\\), the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) belong to every admissible family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) all have first coordinate \\(1\\).  \nIf a line contains two of them it must be the vertical line \\(x=1\\); otherwise each of those \\(n\\) points would need a distinct line, exhausting the whole set of \\(n\\) lines and leaving no line for the remaining points. Hence \\(x=1\\) is forced.  \n\nThe same argument applied to the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) forces the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n\nThe points on the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) are \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nAny line containing two of them has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); such a line is non‑sunny. If we omitted the diagonal, each anti‑diagonal point would need its own line, again exceeding the available number. Hence the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) is also forced. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Interior points  \n\nAfter removing the three forced lines we are left with  \n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines must cover all points of \\(I_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.**  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(I_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct interior points  \n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) with \\(a_i,b_i\\ge2\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n\\).  \nBecause the points are collinear, the slopes between any two of them are equal:\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\nAll differences \\(a_j-a_i\\) and \\(b_j-b_i\\) are non‑zero integers.  \nIf the common slope were \\(0\\) the line would be horizontal; if it were undefined the line would be vertical; if it were \\(-1\\) the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). All three possibilities are excluded by the definition of a sunny line. Hence a sunny line cannot pass through three interior points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of many sunny lines for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n**Lemma 3.** For \\(n\\ge4\\) the \\(n-3\\) lines remaining after the forced three cannot contain any sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf among the \\(n-3\\) lines there were \\(s\\ge1\\) sunny lines, by Lemma 2 they would cover at most \\(2s\\) interior points. The other \\(n-3-s\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them can cover at most \\(\\displaystyle\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\) interior points, which is at most \\(\\frac{n-2}{2}\\). Consequently the total number of interior points that could be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2s+(n-3-s)\\frac{n-2}{2}\n   =\\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}2 -s\\Bigl(\\frac{n-2}{2}-2\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) the factor \\(\\frac{n-2}{2}-2\\) is positive, so the right‑hand side is **strictly smaller** than \\(|I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\) from (2). Hence the interior points could not all be covered – a contradiction.  \n\nFor \\(n=4\\) we have \\(|I_4|=1\\); the single interior point \\((2,2)\\) can be covered by a non‑sunny line (e.g. the diagonal \\(x+y=5\\)), so again no sunny line is needed among the remaining \\(n-3=1\\) line. ∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 1 and Lemma 3 we conclude that for every \\(n\\ge4\\) **at most one** of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny. The constructions of §1 give both possibilities \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\). Hence for \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values are exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) the forced lines of Lemma 1 are \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=4\\).  \nAny admissible family must contain exactly one of these three (otherwise we would already have three lines and could not add a fourth).  \n\n*If the non‑sunny line is \\(x=1\\):* the uncovered points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\). Any line through two of them is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); therefore the two remaining lines cannot both be sunny.  \n\n*If the non‑sunny line is \\(y=1\\):* the uncovered points are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\); the same argument shows that two sunny lines are impossible.  \n\n*If the non‑sunny line is \\(x+y=4\\):* the uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); again any line joining two of them is non‑sunny.  \n\nThus **exactly two** sunny lines cannot occur for \\(n=3\\).  \n\nThe constructions of §1 already provide examples for \\(k=0,1,3\\). Hence for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]  \n\nAll listed values are attainable, and no other value of \\(k\\) can be realised. ∎"
