n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemmas on sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of each of the families \\(C,R,D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Two points of \\(C\\) have the same first coordinate, so a line through\nthem is vertical; similarly two points of \\(R\\) give a horizontal line and two\npoints of \\(D\\) give a line of slope \\(-1\\).  All three possibilities are\nexcluded for a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(I_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a sunny line contained three distinct interior points.\nBecause the points are collinear, the three slopes formed by the pairs are\nequal.  All differences of the coordinates are non‑zero integers; the only\npossibilities for an integer slope that repeats three times are \\(0\\),\n\\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), each of which contradicts the definition of a sunny\nline. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Bounding the number of sunny lines for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines in a feasible configuration.\n\n*Boundary points.*  \nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(3s\\) boundary points.\nThe remaining \\(n-s\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them can cover **all**\npoints of at most one of the families \\(C,R,D\\) (by taking the vertical,\nhorizontal or diagonal line).  Hence the non‑sunny lines can cover at most\n\\((n-s)n\\) boundary points.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n3s+(n-s)n\\ge |C\\cup R\\cup D|=3n-3. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Interior points.*  \nBy Lemma 2 the sunny lines cover at most \\(2s\\) interior points.\nEvery non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n-2\\) interior points (the maximal\npossible occurs for a horizontal or vertical line that stays inside the\ntriangle).  Thus the non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-s)(n-2)\\) interior\npoints, and we must have  \n\n\\[\n2s+(n-s)(n-2)\\ge |I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)