ng all points of \\(I_n\\) except those on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \n   The missing diagonal consists of exactly \\(n-3\\) interior points; it is covered by the single non‑sunny diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\).  \n   Consequently we obtain a configuration with \\(k=n-4\\) sunny lines.  \n   Removing any further sunny line and replacing it by a non‑sunny interior line reduces \\(k\\) by one, so every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-4\\) is attainable.\n\n   *Small values.*  \n   - \\(n=3\\): the interior set is empty; the three forced lines are the only ones needed.  Adding extra lines we can obtain \\(k=0,1,3\\) and no other values.  \n   - \\(n=4\\): \\(I_4=\\{(2,2)\\}\\).  Either the line \\(x=2\\) (non‑sunny) or the line \\(y=x\\) (sunny) is used, giving \\(k=0,1\\).  \n   - \\(n=5\\): \\(I_5=\\{(2,2),(2,3),(3,2)\\}\\).  Two interior lines are required; at most one of them can be sunny, so \\(k=0,1\\).  \n\n   These constructions together realise every value allowed by the upper bound (4).\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   Combining the upper bound (4) with the explicit constructions yields the complete description of admissible numbers of sunny lines stated at the beginning of the summary.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad   \nB=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad   \nC=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\;a,b\\ge1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\cup I_n\\), where  \n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Forced non‑sunny lines  \n\nThe three sets \\(A,B,C\\) lie respectively on the lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:y=1,\\qquad \\ell_3:x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf a line contains two distinct points of \\(A\\) (or of \\(B\\) or of \\(C\\)) it must be vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\); hence it is **non‑sunny**.  \nConsequently any family of lines covering all required points must contain the three lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\).  \nThese three lines are distinct and non‑sunny; they will be present in every admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Interior points  \n\nFrom (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines (the *interior lines*) have to cover the set \\(I_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  How many interior points can a sunny line contain?  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nWrite \\(L\\) in the form \\(y=mx+b\\) with rational \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nTwo distinct integer points \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\) on \\(L\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=m .\n\\]\n\nHence the difference \\(x_2-x_1\\) must be a multiple of the denominator of \\(m\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\) the horizontal width is at most \\(n-2\\); therefore the number of integer points that can lie on a sunny line is bounded by the largest integer \\(q\\) such that \\(q\\le\\frac{n-2}{|\\,\\text{denominator of }m\\,|}\\).  \nThe smallest possible denominator (different from \\(1\\)) is \\(2\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,r_n:=\\max_{L\\text{ sunny}}|L\\cap I_n|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is attained by the line \\(y=x\\) when \\(n\\) is even (it contains \\(\\frac{n-2}{2}\\) points) and by the same line when \\(n\\) is odd (it contains \\(\\frac{n-3}{2}\\) points).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Upper bound for the number of sunny interior lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns=\\#\\{\\text{sunny interior lines}\\},\\qquad\nt=n-3-s=\\#\\{\\text{non‑sunny interior lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny interior line is vertical, horizontal or diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(4\\le c\\le n\\); such a line meets \\(I_n\\) in at most \\(n-3\\) points.  \nUsing (3) and (4),\n\n\\[\nr_n\\,s+(n-3)\\,t\\;\\ge\\;|I_n|\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ns\\le\\frac{(n-3)^2-|I_n|}{\\,n-3-r_n\\,}\n=\\frac{(n-3)(n-4)}{2\\bigl(n-3-r_n\\bigr)} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInsert the value of \\(r_n\\) from (4).\n\n*If \\(n=2p\\) is even*, then \\(r_n=p-1\\) and \\(n-3-r_n=p-2\\).  \nFormula (5) gives  \n\n\\[\ns\\le\\frac{(2p-3)(2p-4)}{2(p-2)}=2p-3=n-3 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2p+1\\) is odd (\\(n\\ge5\\))*,\nthen \\(r_n=p-1\\) and \\(n-3-r_n=p-1\\).  \nFormula (5) yields  \n\n\\[\ns\\le\\frac{(2p-2)(2p-3)}{2(p-1)}=2p-3=n-4 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can contain more than the numbers of sunny lines displayed in (6)–(7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions attaining every admissible value  \n\n#### 5.1  Even \\(n\\ge 6\\)\n\nKeep the three forced lines (2).  \nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\Bigl\\{-\\frac{n-4}{2},-\\frac{n-4}{2}+1,\\dots ,\\frac{n-4}{2}\\Bigr\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ndefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=x+c .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|c|\\le\\frac{n-4}{2}\\), the line \\(L_c\\) meets the interior set \\(I_n\\) in  \n\n\\[\n|L_c\\cap I_n|=\n\\begin{cases}\n3, & c=0,\\\\[1mm]\n2, & c\\neq0 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c\\text{ as in }(8)}\\) consists of exactly \\(n-3\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \\(I_n\\).  \n\nTo obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny interior lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)) we proceed as follows:\n\n* keep the three forced lines;\n* select any \\(k\\) of the lines \\(L_c\\);\n* replace the remaining \\(n-3-k\\) lines \\(L_c\\) by any \\(n-3-k\\) distinct non‑sunny interior lines (for instance vertical lines \\(x=2,3,\\dots\\)).\n\nThe resulting set contains \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and all required points are covered. Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) is attainable.\n\n#### 5.2  Odd \\(n\\ge 7\\)\n\nAgain keep the three forced lines.  \nConsider the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=x+c ,\\qquad\nc\\in\\Bigl\\{-\\frac{n-5}{2},-\\frac{n-5}{2}+1,\\dots ,\\frac{n-5}{2}\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(n-4\\) such lines; together they cover all points of \\(I_n\\) **except** those lying on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \nThe diagonal \\(x+y=n\\) consists of exactly \\(n-3\\) interior points and is covered by the non‑sunny diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_n:\\;x+y=n .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus we have a configuration with \\(k=n-4\\) sunny lines.  \nRemoving any sunny line \\(L_c\\) and inserting any other non‑sunny interior line reduces the number of sunny lines by one while still covering all points. Consequently every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-4\\}\n\\]\n\nis realizable for odd \\(n\\ge7\\).\n\n#### 5.3  Small values  \n\n* \\(n=3\\): the interior set \\(I_3\\) is empty.  The three forced lines already give a covering.  Adding extra lines we can obtain \\(k=0\\) (all extra lines non‑sunny), \\(k=1\\) (one extra sunny line, e.g. \\(y=x\\)), or \\(k=3\\) (three extra sunny lines, e.g. the three lines joining the three pairs of points of the triangle).  No other value of \\(k\\) is possible because the three forced lines are non‑sunny.\n\n* \\(n=4\\): \\(I_4=\\{(2,2)\\}\\).  The interior point can be covered either by the non‑sunny line \\(x=2\\) or by the sunny line \\(y=x\\); hence \\(k=0,1\\) are possible and, by the upper bound (6), no larger \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n* \\(n=5\\): \\(I_5=\\{(2,2),(2,3),(3,2)\\}\\).  Two interior lines are needed.  At most one of them can be sunny (a sunny line cannot contain both \\((2,3)\\) and \\((3,2)\\) because that would give slope \\(-1\\)).  Hence \\(k=0,1\\) are possible, and the bound (7) shows that no larger \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer 