ven},\\\\[2mm]\n   \\dfrac{n-3}{2}, & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, for even \\(n\\) the unique line attaining \\(r_{n}\\) is \\(y=x\\); for odd \\(n\\) the line \\(y=x\\) attains \\(r_{n}\\) as well, but every other sunny line meets \\(I_{n}\\) in at most two points.\n\n4. **A lower bound on the number of non‑sunny interior lines.**  \n   Let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny interior lines and \\(t=n-3-s\\) the number of non‑sunny interior lines.  \n   Consider the two “border” sets  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{x}= \\{(2,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-2\\},\\qquad   \n   S_{y}= \\{(a,2)\\mid 2\\le a\\le n-2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Every sunny line contains at most one point of \\(S_{x}\\cup S_{y}\\) (except possibly the common point \\((2,2)\\)).*  \n   Indeed, two distinct points of \\(S_{x}\\) have the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate, forcing a vertical line; two distinct points of \\(S_{y}\\) have the same \\(y\\)‑coordinate, forcing a horizontal line; a sunny line cannot contain a point of \\(S_{x}\\) and a different point of \\(S_{y}\\) because that would give slope \\(-1\\) (the only non‑sunny slope with that property).  \n\n   Hence the \\(s\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(s+1\\) points of \\(S_{x}\\cup S_{y}\\) (the extra “+1’’ accounts for the possible use of \\((2,2)\\)).  \n   The set \\(S_{x}\\cup S_{y}\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_{x}\\cup S_{y}| = 2(n-3)-1 = 2n-7\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  \n   The remaining points of \\(S_{x}\\cup S_{y}\\) must be covered by the \\(t\\) non‑sunny interior lines.  \n   A non‑sunny interior line is either vertical, horizontal or diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(4\\le c\\le n\\); such a line can contain at most \\(n-3\\) points of \\(S_{x}\\cup S_{y}\\).\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   (s+1) + t\\,(n-3) \\;\\ge\\; 2n-7 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(t=n-3-s\\) and simplifying gives\n\n   \\[\n   s\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-5, & n\\text{ even and }n\\ge 8,\\\\[2mm]\n   n-4, & n\\text{ odd and }n\\ge 7,\\\\[2mm]\n   n-4, & n=6\\ (\\text{the inequality is not tight here}).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus at least two non‑sunny interior lines are necessary when \\(n\\) is even and \\(n\\ge 8\\); at least one non‑sunny interior line is necessary when \\(n\\) is odd and \\(n\\ge 7\\); for \\(n=6\\) a single non‑sunny line already suffices.\n\n5. **Realising every admissible value.**  \n\n   *Even \\(n\\ge 8\\).*  \n   Keep the three forced non‑sunny lines.  \n   Use the two non‑sunny interior lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=2,\\qquad y=2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(S_{x}\\cup S_{y}\\).  \n   The remaining interior points are those with \\(a,b\\ge3\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\); they form the smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   I'_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge3,\\;b\\ge3,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\qquad |I'_{n}|=\\frac{(n-4)(n-5)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(I'_{n}\\) is covered by the family of sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{c}:\\;y=x+c,\\qquad \n   c\\in\\Bigl\\{-\\frac{n-6}{2},-\\frac{n-6}{2}+1,\\dots ,\\frac{n-6}{2}\\Bigr\\},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   which consists of exactly \\(n-5\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n   Each of these lines meets \\(I'_{n}\\) in either two points (if \\(|c|<\\frac{n-6}{2}\\)) or three points (if \\(c=0\\)).  Hence together they cover all points of \\(I'_{n}\\).  \n\n   By deleting any \\(s\\) of the lines in (7) and inserting any \\(s\\) distinct non‑sunny interior lines (for instance vertical lines \\(x=3,4,\\dots\\)), we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k=s\\) sunny interior lines, for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le s\\le n-5 .\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the three forced non‑sunny lines gives the same values for the total number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n   *Odd \\(n\\ge 7\\).*  \n   Keep the three forced lines.  \n   Use the single non‑sunny interior line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{n}:\\;x+y=n .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   It covers all points of \\(S_{x}\\cup S_{y}\\) (there are \\(n-3\\) of them).  \