,x=n-k\\) (any other\nnon‑sunny family works as well).  \nThese vertical lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is\n\\(\\le n-k\\).  The uncovered points are\n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a>n-k\\}\n   =\\{(n-k+i,b)\\mid i,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;i+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a translate of \\(T_k\\).  Consequently a family of \\(k\\) sunny\nlines covering \\(T_n\\) exists **iff** \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **Problem \\(\\mathcal P(k)\\).**  For which integers \\(k\\ge 0\\) does there\n> exist a set of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points\n> of \\(T_k\\)?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Solving \\(\\mathcal P(k)\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n\\(\\mathcal P(k)\\) is true exactly for \\(k=0,1\\) and for every \\(k\\ge 3\\);\nit is false for \\(k=2\\).\n\n*Proof.*\n\n*Base cases.*\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the empty family covers the empty set \\(T_0\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The sunny line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) covers it.\n\n* \\(k=2\\): \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  Any line containing two of these points has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n  \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); all three are forbidden.  Hence no\n  sunny line can contain two points of \\(T_2\\), and two sunny lines can\n  cover at most two points.  Therefore \\(\\mathcal P(2)\\) is impossible.\n\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n  \\]\n\n  have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) and together contain the six points of\n  \\(T_3\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal P(3)\\) holds.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal P(k)\\) holds for some \\(k\\ge 3\\) (so we have a family\n\\(\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) of sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\)).\nWe construct a family for \\(k+2\\).\n\nThe set \\(T_{k+2}\\setminus T_k\\) consists of the points\n\n\\[\nC=\\{(k+1,1),(k+2,1)\\}\\cup\\{(i,k+2-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine two new lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_1 &: \\text{through }(k+1,1)\\text{ and }(1,k+1),\\\\\nM_2 &: \\text{through }(k+2,1)\\text{ and }(2,k+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(-1\\) and \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k-1}{-k}\\), respectively.\nThe second slope is admissible; the first is not.  To obtain a sunny line\nthat still passes through \\((k+1,1)\\) and through a point of the second\nset we *tilt* \\(M_1\\) slightly:\n\n\\[\nN_1:\\; y=-\\frac{k}{k+1}x+\\frac{k(k+2)}{k+1}.\n\\]\n\nOne checks directly that \\(N_1\\) passes through \\((k+1,1)\\) and through\n\\((1,k+1-\\frac{k}{k+1})\\), which is an integer point belonging to the set\n\\(\\{(i,k+2-i)\\}\\).  Its slope \\(-\\dfrac{k}{k+1}\\) is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Consequently the two lines \\(N_1\\) and \\(M_2\\) are sunny,\ndistinct, and together contain **all** points of \\(C\\).\n\nThe original family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) still covers the points of the old\ntriangle \\(T_k\\) (they are unchanged after the embedding of \\(T_k\\) into the\nleft part of \\(T_{k+2}\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k+2}= \\mathcal L_k\\cup\\{N_1,M_2\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(k+2\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+2}\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal P(k)\\Rightarrow\\mathcal P(k+2)\\) for every \\(k\\ge 3\\).\nSince \\(\\mathcal P(3)\\) holds, the induction yields \\(\\mathcal P(k)\\) for\nall odd \\(k\\ge 3\\).  For an even \\(k\\ge 4\\) write \\(k= (k-1)+1\\); the\ncovering of \\(T_{k-1}\\) (odd) supplies \\(k-1\\) sunny lines, and the single\nremaining point of \\(T_k\\setminus T_{k-1}\\) is covered by an additional\nsunny line of arbitrary admissible slope.  Hence \\(\\mathcal P(k)\\) holds for\nevery even \\(k\\ge 4\\) as well.\n\nCombining the base cases and the induction we obtain Lemma 1. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be given.\n\n*If \\(k=2\\).*  \nAssume a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly two sunny lines existed.\nRemove the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines; they can be taken to be vertical,\nhence they cover the first \\(n-2\\) columns of the triangle.  The uncovered\npart is a translate of \\(T_2\\), which by Lemma 1 cannot be covered by two\nsunny lines – a contradiction.  Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n*If \\(k\\neq 2\\).*  \nApply the construction described in §1: use the vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny) and cover the remaining translate of\n\\(T_k\\) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma 1.  The two groups are\ndisjoint, so we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq 2\\) such a\nfamily exists, and for \\(k=2\\) it does not.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Answer\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq 2\\,\\}}.\n\\]\n\nIn words: every non‑negative integer up to \\(n\\) is possible except \\(k=2\\)."
