amily \\(\\{D_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains no sunny\nline.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_i\\) are non\u2011sunny.  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with\n\\(x\\le n-1\\) lies on one of them; the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).\nChoose any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},S\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly one of which is sunny, and it covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the\nrequirements and contains exactly two sunny lines.\nConsequently the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are all non\u2011sunny; each of them is\nparallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nAll belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\) and \\(n+1\\le n+1\\).\nThe slopes of the lines joining any two of these points are respectively\nvertical, horizontal and \\(-1\\); each of those slopes is forbidden for a\nsunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three\npoints.  With only two sunny lines, at least one of \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) would\nremain uncovered, contradicting the covering condition.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq 2\\text{ for any }n\\ge 3 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently\nevery \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes\nare distinct.\n\n*Claim.* Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the\nlines \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nFix \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  Since \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a\\le n\\), set\n\\(i=a\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (3.1) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a+\\frac{1}{a+1}=a-\\frac{a-1}{a+1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\) is an integer and \\(0<b\\le n+1-a\\), a straightforward case\nanalysis shows that the equality \\(b=\\frac{a}{a+1}a+\\frac{1}{a+1}\\) holds\nexactly when \\(b=a\\) or \\(b=a-1\\).  Both possibilities occur for points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\):  \n\n* if \\(b=a\\) then \\(2a\\le n+1\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\);  \n* if \\(b=a-1\\) then \\(2a-1\\le n+1\\) and again \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to some \\(L_i\\), completing the\nclaim.  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\) satisfy the required\nconditions for \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Remarks on the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe arguments above settle the cases \\(k=0,1,2,n\\).  \nFor intermediate values \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) a construction that uses exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines while covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) has not been\nfound.  The main obstacle is to arrange the sunny lines so that they together\ncover the whole triangular lattice without exceeding the prescribed number\nof sunny lines.  This part of the problem remains open."
