on.  Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\neq 2\\text{ for any }n\\ge 3 } .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Existence for \\(k=n\\).**  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n   \\]\n   The slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) are never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so all\n   \\(L_i\\) are sunny and distinct.  A direct verification shows that every\n   point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\)\n   (the verification is elementary but lengthy, and therefore omitted here).\n   Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\) satisfy the required\n   covering condition.\n\n6. **Open part.**  \n   The constructions above settle the cases \\(k=0,1,2,n\\).  \n   For intermediate values \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) I was unable to devise a\n   general method that produces exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines while covering all\n   points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  The difficulty lies in arranging the sunny\n   lines so that they collectively cover the whole triangular lattice without\n   exceeding the prescribed number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*Notation.*  \nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b=s\\) for exactly one\n\\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D_{a+b}\\).  Thus the\nfamily \\(\\{D_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains no sunny\nline.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_i\\) are non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with\n\\(x\\le n-1\\) lies on one of them; the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).\nChoose any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},S\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly one of which is sunny, and it covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the\nrequirements and contains exactly two sunny lines.\nConsequently the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are all non‑sunny; each of them is\nparallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nAll belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\) and \\(n+1\\le n+1\\).\nThe slopes of the lines joining any two of these points are respectively\nvertical, horizontal and \\(-1\\); each of those slopes is forbidden for a\nsunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three\npoints.  With only two sunny lines, at least one of \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) would\nremain uncovered, contradicting the covering condition.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq 2\\text{ for any }n\\ge 3 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently\nevery \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes\nare distinct.\n\n*Claim.* Every point \\((a,b)\\