\n  Choose any sunny line through the new point \\((n+1,1)\\); for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{n+1}:\\;y=\\frac12x+\\frac12 .\n  \\]\n\n  This line does not coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (all lines of\n  \\(\\mathcal L\\) already pass through points whose coordinates are \\(\\le n\\)).\n  The set \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{S_{n+1}\\}\\) therefore consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct\n  lines and covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\); its number of sunny lines is\n  \\(k+1\\).\n\nConsequently, starting from any configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) we can obtain\nconfigurations for the next value of \\(n\\) with the same \\(k\\) and with\n\\(k+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Generation of all admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a configuration with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nLemma 1 gives \\(k=0\\); Lemma 2 gives \\(k=1\\); Lemma 3 gives \\(k=3\\).  \nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the required condition for \\(n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(k\\le n\\) we start from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) (which exists by the\ninduction hypothesis) and apply Lemma 4(a) to obtain a configuration for\n\\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n* If \\(k=n+1\\) we start from a configuration for \\((n,n)\\) (Lemma 3) and apply\nLemma 4(b) to obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,n+1)\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n+1\\).  By induction it is true for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 5  (No configuration with \\(k=2\\))\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) for \\((n,2)\\)\nexists.  Let  \n\n\\[\nA=(n-1,1),\\qquad B=(n-1,2),\\qquad C=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nAll three belo