ing both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n(the value \\(k=n\\) is possible only for \\(n=3\\); the value \\(k=2\\) is never\npossible).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   *The required lattice points are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=N_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Extremal constructions.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\): the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\;(s=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\): the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\\) together with any sunny line through the uncovered point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   *\\(k=3\\): a concrete configuration for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) (Lemma 3).  \n\n3. **Inductive step.**  \n   *Lemma 4 shows that from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) with \\(k\\ge 3\\) we can obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\) (add the non‑sunny diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\)) and a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\) (add a suitable sunny line through the new point \\((n+1,1)\\)).  \n   *Consequently, once the case \\(k=3\\) is known, all values \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) are produced by repeated application of the two operations.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 5 proves that with exactly two sunny lines the covering condition cannot be satisfied; the three points \\((n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\) force the existence of a third sunny line.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   *Lemma 6 gives a sharp bound: a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   *For \\(n\\ge4\\) the total number of points that \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover is therefore  \n     \\[\n     n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n     \\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = N_n ,\n     \\]\n     so a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist (the case \\(n=3\\) is exceptional and is realised by three arbitrary sunny lines).\n\nPutting the lemmas together yields the exact set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope belongs to  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA *configuration* \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover every\npoint of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is denoted\nby \\(k(\\mathcal L)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Extremal constructions  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  (\\(k=0\\) is attainable)\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) lines, each parallel to the forbidden line\n\\(x+y=0\\); hence every line of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) is non‑sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(x+y=a+b\\) of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\).\nThus \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) is a required configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  (\\(k=1\\) is attainable)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate is \\(\\le n-1\\) lies on one of the \\(V_i\\); the only uncovered\npoint is \\((n,1)\\).  Choose any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},S\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly one of which is sunny, and it covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  (\\(k=3\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\))\n\nWe construct a configuration with three sunny lines and \\(n-3\\) non‑sunny\nlines.\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nTake the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThey are respectively vertical, horizontal and diagonal, hence non‑sunny.\nThey cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose sum is \\(n+1\\) (the diagonal)\nor whose first or second coordinate equals \\(1\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nThe remaining points are those with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n\\).  They form a smaller triangle \\(\\mathcal P_{n-2}\\) shifted by\n\\((1,1)\\).  Inside this triangle we use the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=x-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;y=x-2 .\n\\]\n\nAll three have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny) and are distinct.\nA direct inspection shows that every point of the shifted triangle lies on\nexactly one of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\).  Consequently the whole set\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by the \\(n\\) lines\n\\(\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1,\\;\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\), and exactly three of them\nare sunny.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive enlargement of configurations  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4  (Induction step)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) with \\(k\\ge 3\\).\n\n* (a) **Adding a non‑sunny line.**  \n  The diagonal line  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{n+2}:\\;x+y=n+2\n  \\]\n\n  is non‑sunny and contains all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\setminus\\mathcal\n  P_n\\) (those with sum \\(n+2\\)).  \n  Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{D_{n+2}\\}\\) is a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n* (b) **Adding a sunny line.**  \n  The point \\((n+1,1)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) but to no line of\n  \\(\\mathcal L\\) (all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) meet only points whose coordinates\n  are \\(\\le n\\)).  Choose any sunny line through \\((n+1,1)\\); for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{n+1}:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n  \\]\n\n  This line is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  The set\n  \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{S_{n+1}\\}\\) therefore consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines,\n  covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\), and contains \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus from a configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) we can obtain configurations for\n\\((n+1,k)\\) and for \\((n+1,k+1)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Generation of all admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nLemma 1 gives \\(k=0\\); Lemma 2 gives \\(k=1\\); Lemma 3 gives \\(k=3\\).\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the required condition for \\(n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(k\\le n-1\\) we start from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) (which exists by\nthe induction hypothesis) and apply Lemma 4(a) to obtain a configuration for\n\\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n* If \\(k=n\\) (which can occur only when \\(n=3\\)) we already have the\nconstruction; for \\(n\\ge4\\) the case \\(k=n\\) will be shown impossible in\nLemma 6.\n\n* If \\(k\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\le n\\) we start from a configuration for \\((n,k-1)\\)\n(which exists by the induction hypothesis) and apply Lemma 4(b) to obtain a\nconfiguration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n+1\\).  By induction it holds for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5  \n\nAssume that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) for \\((n,2)\\) exists.\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nA=(n-1,1),\\qquad B=(n-1,2),\\qquad C=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nAll three belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\).\nThe lines \\(AB,AC,BC\\) have slopes \\(\\infty,0,-1\\) respectively; therefore\nnone of them is sunny.  Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one**\nof the points \\(A,B,C\\).\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) has only two sunny lines, at least one of the points\n\\(A,B,C\\) must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  Any non‑sunny line that passes\nthrough one of these points is forced to be one of the three forbidden\ndirections, and each such line automatically passes through a second point\namong \\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\).  Hence covering the three points would require at least\nthree non‑sunny lines, contradicting the fact that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains only\n\\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines (because \\(n\\ge3\\)).  Therefore no configuration with\nexactly two sunny lines exists. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Upper bound for the number of points on a sunny line  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 6  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\nIf \\(m\\) is rational, write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and\n\\(q>0\\).  Any two lattice points of \\(L\\) differ by a vector \\((q,p)\\); hence\nall lattice points of \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression\n\\((x_0+ t q,\\;y_0+ t p)\\) with integer parameter \\(t\\).\n\nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the coordinate \\(a\\) can increase at most\nby \\(q\\) before the sum exceeds \\(n+1\\).  Consequently the number of admissible\nvalues of \\(t\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{q+|p|}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nSince \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\) (the three forbidden slopes correspond to \\((p,q)=(0,1),\n(1,0),(-1,1)\\)), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m\\) is irrational, \\(L\\) contains at most one lattice point, and the\nsame bound holds. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nAssume a configuration with \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) exists for some\n\\(n\\ge4\\).  By Lemma 6 each line contains at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore the total number of points that can be\ncovered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = N_n ,\n\\]\n\nthe strict inequality holding because \\(n\\ge4\\) implies\n\\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) with equality only when \\(n\\) is\neven, in which case the product is \\(\\frac{n^{2}}2<N_n\\); when \\(n\\) is odd,\n\\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and the product equals\n\\(N_n\\) only if every sunny line attains the bound, which forces every line\nto have slope \\(1\\).  There are at most two slope‑\\(1\\) lines that contain\n\\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the lines \\(y=x\\) and\n\\(y=x-1\\)), so the bound cannot be attained by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\nHence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) does not exist for any \\(n\\ge4\\).\n(The case \\(n=3\\) is exceptional: three arbitrary sunny lines each passing\nthrough one of the three points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) give a valid configuration.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Final classification  \n\nCombining the lemmas we obtain:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable (Lemmas 1 and 2).\n* \\(k=2\\) is never attainable (Lemma 5).\n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) a configuration with \\(k=3\\) exists (Lemma 3); using\nLemma 4 we can increase \\(n\\) while keeping \\(k=3\\), and we can increase both\n\\(n\\) and \\(k\\) simultaneously.  Hence for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every\n\\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration exists.\n* A configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists only for \\(n=3\\); for all \\(n\\ge4\\) it\nis impossible (Lemma 6).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}\n}\n\\]\n\nIn words: for every \\(n\\ge3\\) all non‑negative integers \\(k\\le n\\) are\npossible except \\(k=2\\) and, when \\(n\\ge4\\), also \\(k=n\\). ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
