t cover\nevery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is\ndenoted by \\(k(\\mathcal L)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for the extreme values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1  (\\(k=0\\) is attainable)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) lines, each parallel to the forbidden line\n\\(x+y=0\\); hence every line of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) is non‑sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(x+y=a+b\\) of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\).\nThus \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) is a required configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2  (\\(k=1\\) is attainable)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate is \\(\\le n-1\\) lies on one of the \\(V_i\\); the only uncovered\npoint is \\((n,1)\\).  Choose any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},S\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly one of which is sunny, and it covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 3  (\\(k=3\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-3)\n\\]\n\nbe the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines; they are non‑sunny.\nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not lying on any \\(V_i\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_n=\n\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nsix points forming a small “corner’’ of the triangle.\n\nDefine three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-1=-\\tfrac12\\,(x-n)      &&\\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: y-x=1                    &&\\text{through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(n-2,1),\\\\\nL_3 &: y+2x=n+2                 &&\\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12,\\,1,\\,-2\\), none of which belongs to\n\\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence they are sunny and pairwise distinct.\nA direct inspection shows that each point of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) lies on exactly\none of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  Consequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-3},L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and has exactly\nthree sunny lines.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Inductive enlargement of configurations  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 4  (Induction step)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) with \\(k\\ge3\\).\n\n* (a) **Adding a non‑sunny line.**  \n  The diagonal line  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{n+2}:\\;x+y=n+2\n  \\]\n\n  is non‑sunny and contains precisely the points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\setminus\n  \\mathcal P_n\\) (those with sum \\(n+2\\)).  Hence\n  \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{D_{n+2}\\}\\) is a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n* (b) **Adding a sunny line.**  \n  The point \\((n+1,1)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) but to no line of\n  \\(\\mathcal L\\) (all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) meet only points whose\n  coordinates are \\(\\le n\\)).  Choose any sunny line through \\((n+1,1)\\); for\n  instance  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{n+1}:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n  \\]\n\n  This line is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Thus\n  \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{S_{n+1}\\}\\) is a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Generation of all admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nLemma 1 gives \\(k=0\\); Lemma 2 gives \\(k=1\\); Lemma 3 (applied with \\(n=3\\))\ngives \\(k=3\\); and a configuration with \\(k=2\\) is obtained by taking the\nthree lines of Lemma 1 and replacing one of them by a sunny line that does not\npass through any point of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).  Hence the statement holds for\n\\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the required condition for \\(n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(k\\le n-1\\) we start from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) (exists by the\ninduction hypothesis) and apply Lemma 4(a) to obtain a configuration for\n\\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n* If \\(k\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\le n\\) we start from a configuration for \\((n,k-1)\\)\n(which exists by the induction hypothesis) and apply Lemma 4(b) to obtain a\nconfiguration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n+1\\).  By induction it holds for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 5  \n\nAssume \\(n\\ge4\\) and that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) with exactly two sunny\nlines exists.  Remove from \\(\\mathcal L\\) all non‑sunny vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\); the remaining lines still cover the six points  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_n=\n\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line whose slope is sunny can contain **at most two** points of\n\\(\\mathcal S_n\\): a direct check of the \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) pairs shows that the\nthird point, if any, would force the slope to be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\nConsequently covering the six points requires at least three sunny lines,\ncontradicting the assumption that only two sunny lines are present.\nHence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for \\(n\\ge4\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 6  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(m\\) is irrational*, \\(L\\) contains at most one lattice point, and the\ninequality holds.\n\n*If \\(m\\) is rational*, write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and\n\\(q>0\\).  Any two lattice points of \\(L\\) differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\); thus\nthe lattice points of \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression\n\\((x_0+tq,\\;y_0+tp)\\) with integer parameter \\(t\\).\n\nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the sum \\(a+b\\) increases by \\(|p|+|q|\\) when\n\\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\).  Since \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\) (the three forbidden slopes\ncorrespond to \\((p,q)=(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\)), the number of admissible values\nof \\(t\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, Lemma 6 would give\n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=N_n\\qquad (n\\ge4),\n\\]\n\nso the \\(n\\) sunny lines could not cover all \\(N_n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) does not exist for any \\(n\\ge4\\).\nFor \\(n=3\\) the bound is sharp and a configuration with three sunny lines\nexists (take three distinct sunny lines, each passing through one of the three\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Final classification  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining the lemmas we obtain:\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is attainable.\n* For \\(n\\ge4\\):\n  * \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable (Lemmas 1 and 2).\n  * \\(k=2\\) is impossible (Lemma 5).\n  * For each \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration exists\n    (Lemma 3 provides the base case \\(k=3\\); Lemma 4 yields all larger\n    values).\n  * \\(k=n\\) is impossible (Lemma 7), while \\(k=n\\) does occur for the\n    exceptional case \\(n=3\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\] ∎"
