nny line through the new point \\((n+1,1)\\).\n\n   Repeated use yields configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   After deleting the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) (as in the construction of Lemma 3) the six points \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) remain.  \n   A direct check of all \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) pairs shows that any line whose slope is sunny can contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\); a third point would force the slope to be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence covering \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) needs at least three sunny lines, so a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Lemma 5 proves that a sunny line meets at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   For \\(n\\ge4\\),\n\n   \\[\n   n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2=N_n ,\n   \\]\n\n   so all \\(n\\) lines cannot be sunny.  For \\(n=3\\) the bound is sharp and a configuration with three sunny lines exists (take three distinct sunny lines, each passing through one of the three points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\)).\n\n6. **Special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) cannot be covered with exactly two sunny lines (the argument of step 4 applied with \\(\\mathcal S_3=\\mathcal P_3\\)).  Thus the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\nCombining the constructions and impossibility results yields the classification stated above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope belongs to  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA *configuration* \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is denoted by \\(k(\\mathcal L)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for the extreme values of \\(k\\)\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1 (\\(k=0\\) is attainable).**  \n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\]\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) lines, each parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); hence every line of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) is non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(x+y=a+b\\) of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) is a required configuration with \\(k=0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2 (\\(k=1\\) is attainable).**  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate is \\(\\le n-1\\) lies on one of the \\(V_i\\); the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  Choose any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},S\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and it covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 3 (\\(k=3\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge4\\)).**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-3)\n\\]\n\nbe the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines; they are non‑sunny.  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not lying on any \\(V_i\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_n=\n\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nsix points forming a small “corner’’ of the triangle.\n\nDefine three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-1=-\\tfrac12\\,(x-n)      &&\\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: y-x=1                    &&\\text{through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(n-2,1),\\\\\nL_3 &: y+2x=n+2                 &&\\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12,\\,1,\\,-2\\), none of which belongs to\n\\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence they are sunny and pairwise distinct.\nA direct inspection shows that each point of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) lies on exactly\none of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  Consequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-3},L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and has exactly\nthree sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Inductive enlargement of configurations  \n\n---\n\n**Lemma 4 (Induction step).**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) with \\(k\\ge3\\).\n\n* (a) *Adding a non‑sunny line.*  \n  The diagonal line  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{n+2}:\\;x+y=n+2\n  \\]\n\n  is non‑sunny and contains precisely the points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\setminus\n  \\mathcal P_n\\) (those with sum \\(n+2\\)).  Hence\n  \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{D_{n+2}\\}\\) is a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n* (b) *Adding a sunny line.*  \n  The point \\((n+1,1)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) but to no line of\n  \\(\\mathcal L\\) (all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) meet only points whose\n  coordinates are \\(\\le n\\)).  Choose any sunny line through \\((n+1,1)\\); for\n  instance  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{n+1}:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n  \\]\n\n  This line is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Thus\n  \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{S_{n+1}\\}\\) is a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Generation of all admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nLemma 1 gives \\(k=0\\); Lemma 2 gives \\(k=1\\); Lemma 3 (with \\(n=3\\) after a slight\nmodification) gives \\(k=3\\).  No configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists (see\nLemma 5 below).  Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the required condition for \\(n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(k\\le n-1\\) we start from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) (exists by the\ninduction hypothesis) and apply Lemma 4(a) to obtain a configuration for\n\\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n* If \\(k\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\le n\\) we start from a configuration for \\((n,k-1)\\)\n(which exists by the induction hypothesis) and apply Lemma 4(b) to obtain a\nconfiguration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n+1\\).  By induction it holds for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines  \n\n---\n\n**Lemma 5.**  \nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not exist.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(n\\ge4\\) we may delete from any configuration the vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) (they are non‑sunny).  The six points  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_n=\n\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nremain uncovered.  A direct enumeration of the \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) unordered\npairs shows that any line whose slope is sunny can contain **at most two**\npoints of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\); a third point would force the slope to be\n\\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence covering \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) needs at least three\nsunny lines, contradicting the hypothesis of having only two.\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have \\(\\mathcal S_3=\\mathcal P_3\\).  The same pair‑checking\nshows that no sunny line contains more than one point of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\); thus\ntwo sunny lines can cover at most two points, leaving the other four uncovered.\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines is impossible for\n\\(n=3\\) as well. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n---\n\n**Lemma 6.**  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(m\\) is irrational*, \\(L\\) contains at most one lattice point, and the\ninequality holds.\n\n*If \\(m\\) is rational*, write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and\n\\(q>0\\).  Any two lattice points of \\(L\\) differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\); thus\nthe lattice points of \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression\n\\((x_0+tq,\\;y_0+tp)\\) with integer parameter \\(t\\).\n\nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the sum \\(a+b\\) increases by \\(|p|+|q|\\) when\n\\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\).  Because the forbidden slopes correspond to\n\\((p,q)=(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\), we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\).  Consequently the number\nof admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n\n\n**Corollary 7.**  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines of a configuration are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor<N_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge4,\n\\]\n\nso such a configuration cannot exist.  For \\(n=3\\) the inequality becomes an\nequality, and a configuration with three sunny lines does exist (take three\ndistinct sunny lines, each passing through one of the three points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_3\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Final classification  \n\nCombining Lemmas 1–6 we obtain:\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\):\n  * \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable (Lemmas 1 and 2);\n  * \\(k=2\\) is impossible (Lemma 5);\n  * for each \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration exists\n    (Lemma 3 provides the base case \\(k=3\\); Lemma 4 yields all larger values);\n  * \\(k=n\\) is impossible (Corollary 7).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\] ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
