\) the constructions of Lemmas 1, 2 and the special case of Lemma 3\ngive configurations with \\(k=0,1,3\\); Lemma 5 excludes \\(k=2\\).  \nHence the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n* Let \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n  – Lemma 1 provides \\(k=0\\).  \n  – Lemma 2 provides \\(k=1\\).  \n  – Lemma 3 gives \\(k=3\\).  \n  – Lemma 4 shows that from the configuration with three sunny lines we can\n    obtain configurations with any larger number of sunny lines up to \\(n-1\\).  \n  – Lemma 5 rules out \\(k=2\\).  \n  – Corollary 7 rules out \\(k=n\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\] ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
