1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\]\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) lines, each parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\);\nhence every line of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) is non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(x+y=a+b\\) of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) is a required configuration with \\(k=0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  (\\(k=1\\) is attainable).  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate is \\(\\le n-1\\) lies on one of the \\(V_i\\); the only uncovered point\nis \\((n,1)\\).  Choose the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y-1=-\\tfrac12\\,(x-n)      \\qquad\\text{(through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2)).\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is neither \\(0,\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  The family\n\\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},S\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of\nwhich is sunny, and it covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3  (\\(k=3\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge4\\)).  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-3)\n\\]\n\nbe the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines; they are non‑sunny.  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not lying on any \\(V_i\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_n=\n\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nsix points forming a small “corner’’ of the triangle.\n\nDefine three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-1=-\\tfrac12\\,(x-n)      &&\\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: y-1=-2\\,(x-(n-1))        &&\\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3),\\\\\nL_3 &: y-2 = 1\\,(x-(n-1))       &&\\text{through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(n-2,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12,\\,-2,\\;1\\), none of which belongs to\n\\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence they are sunny and pairwise distinct.\nA direct verification shows that each point of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) lies on exactly\none of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  Consequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-3},L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and has exactly\nthree sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  From three sunny lines to any larger admissible number\n\n#### Lemma 4  (Increasing the number of sunny lines).  \nLet \\(n\\ge4\\) and let \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) be the configuration of Lemma 3.\nFor any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-4\\) replace the vertical line\n\\(x=i\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\)) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y = x-i+1 \\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,t).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll the lines \\(S_i\\) have slope \\(1\\), hence are sunny and are pairwise\ndistinct.  Consider a point \\((x,y)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le t\\).\nSet \\(a:=x-y+1\\); because \\(y\\ge1\\) we have \\(a\\le x\\le t\\), and because\n\\(y\\le n+1-x\\) we have \\(a\\ge1\\).  Thus \\(a\\) is an integer in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\)\nand\n\n\\[\ny = x-a+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((x,y)\\) lies on the line \\(S_a\\).  Hence the collection\n\\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_t\\}\\) covers **all** points that were previously covered by the\nremoved vertical lines.  The other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remain covered\neither by the untouched vertical lines \\(x=t+1,\\dots ,n-3\\) or by the three\noriginal sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  Each replacement removes one\nnon‑sunny line and adds one sunny line, while the total number of lines stays\nequal to \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n\nStarting from the configuration of Lemma 3 (which has three sunny lines) and\nperforming \\(k-3\\) successive replacements described in Lemma 4 we obtain a\nconfiguration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 5  \nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not\nexist.\n\n*Proof.*  \nRemove from any configuration the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\); they are\nnon‑sunny.  The six points \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) defined in Lemma 3 remain\nuncovered.  Any line that contains two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) is\nnecessarily horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); consequently it is **not**\nsunny.  Hence a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\).\nWith only two sunny lines at most two points of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) can be covered,\nleaving at least four points uncovered – a contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 6  (Points on a sunny line).  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(m\\) is irrational*, the line contains at most one lattice point, and the\ninequality holds.\n\n*If \\(m\\) is rational*, write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and\n\\(q>0\\).  Any two lattice points of \\(L\\) differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\); thus\nthe lattice points of \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression\n\\((x_0+tq,\\;y_0+tp)\\) with integer parameter \\(t\\).\n\nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the sum \\(a+b\\) increases by\n\\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\) when \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\).  Hence at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) values of \\(t\\) are admissible. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Corollary 7.  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines of a configuration are sunny, then for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=N_n ,\n\\]\n\nso such a configuration cannot exist.  For \\(n=3\\) the inequality becomes an\nequality, and a configuration with three sunny lines does exist (take three\ndistinct sunny lines, each passing through one of the three points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_3\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final classification  \n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the constructions of Lemmas 1, 2 and the special case of Lemma 3\ngive configurations with \\(k=0,1,3\\); Lemma 5 excludes \\(k=2\\).  \nHence the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n* Let \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n  – Lemma 1 provides \\(k=0\\).  \n  – Lemma 2 provides \\(k=1\\).  \n  – Lemma 3 gives \\(k=3\\).  \n  – Lemma 4 shows that from the configuration with three sunny lines we can\n    obtain configurations with any larger number of sunny lines up to \\(n-1\\).  \n  – Lemma 5 rules out \\(k=2\\).  \n  – Corollary 7 rules out \\(k=n\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\] ∎"
