,L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and has exactly\nthree sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  From three sunny lines to any larger admissible number\n\n#### Lemma 4  (Increasing the number of sunny lines).  \nLet \\(n\\ge4\\) and let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be the configuration of Lemma 3.\nFor any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-4\\) replace the vertical line\n\\(x=n-3-t\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_{n-3-t}:\\;y = x-(n-3-t)+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(S_{n-3-t}\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny) and passes through the\npoint \\((n-3-t,1)\\).  For every integer \\(u\\ge0\\) with\n\\((n-3-t)+u+1+u\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl((n-3-t)+u,\\;1+u\\bigr)\\in\\mathcal P_n,\n\\]\n\nso the whole set of points of the removed vertical line is contained in\n\\(S_{n-3-t}\\).  All other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remain covered because\nthey are either on one of the unchanged vertical lines or on one of the three\noriginal sunny lines.  The replacement reduces the number of non‑sunny lines by\n\\(1\\) and increases the number of sunny lines by \\(1\\), while the total number\nof lines stays equal to \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n\nStarting from the configuration of Lemma 3 (which has three sunny lines) and\nperforming \\(k-3\\) successive replacements described in Lemma 4 we obtain a\nconfiguration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 5  \nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not\nexist.\n\n*Proof.*  \nRemove from any configuration the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\); they are\nnon‑sunny.  The six points \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) defined in Lemma 3 remain\nuncovered.  A straightforward enumeration of the \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) unordered\npairs of points shows that any line whose slope is sunny can contain at most\ntwo points of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\); a third point would force the slope to be\n\\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence at least three sunny lines are necessary to\ncover \\(\\mathcal S_n\\), contradicting the hypothesis of having only two. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 6  (Points on a sunny line).  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(m\\) is irrational*, the line contains at most one lattice point, and the\ninequality holds.\n\n*If \\(m\\) is rational*, write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and\n\\(q>0\\).  Any two lattice points of \\(L\\) differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\); thus\nthe lattice points of \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression\n\\((x_0+tq,\\;y_0+tp)\\) with integer parameter \\(t\\).\n\nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the sum \\(a+b\\) increases by\n\\(|p|+|q|\\) when \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\).  Because the forbidden slopes\ncorrespond to \\((p,q)=(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\), we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\).  Hence the\nnumber of admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Corollary 7.  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines of a configuration are sunny, then for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=N_n ,\n\\]\n\nso such a configuration cannot exist.  For \\(n=3\\) the inequality becomes an\nequality, and a configuration with three sunny lines does exist (take three\ndistinct sunny lines, each passing through one of the three points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_3\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final classification  \n\n* For \\(n=3