d \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n   \\]\n\n   Since all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   When \\(n\\) is odd, the right–hand side of (3) equals \\(n\\), but a configuration with \\(k=n\\) would require every line to contain exactly \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points, i.e. the points of \\(S_n\\) would have to be partitioned into \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n   This is impossible because the line \\(y=x\\) is the **only** sunny line that attains the maximal size \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\); all other sunny lines contain at most \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) points. Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n+1}{2}+ (n-1)\\frac{n-1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}2- \\frac{n-1}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Combining the two cases we obtain the universal upper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   Write  \n\n   \\[\n   f:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 1.*  Use the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_0:\\;x=1\n   \\]\n\n   (non‑sunny).  It contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\).\n\n   *Step 2.*  For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) introduce the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = x-i+1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The direction vector of each \\(L_i\\) is \\((1,1)\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Because the smallest point of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L_i\\) is \\((i,1)\\) and the sum of its coordinates is \\(i+1\\le n\