are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nWe claim that \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nConsider the integer  \n\n\\[\ni:=a+b-1\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nA short computation shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nb\n&= \\frac{i-1}{i}\\,a+1-\\frac{i-1}{i}\n   =\\frac{(a+b-2)}{a+b-1}\\,a+1-\\frac{a+b-2}{a+b-1}\\\\[2mm]\n&= \\frac{a(a+b-2)+(a+b-1)-(a+b-2)}{a+b-1}\n   =\\frac{a(a+b-2)+1}{a+b-1}=b .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  Since every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\), the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(0<k<n\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nC_i:=\\{(i,b)\\in T_n\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the **\\(i\\)-th column** of points.  Its cardinality is \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\).\n\nA sunny line cannot be vertical; consequently it can contain **at most one** point of any column \\(C_i\\).  \nIf a column \\(C_i\\) is not covered by a vertical line, then every point of \\(C_i\\) must be taken by a distinct sunny line.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in C}(n+1-i)\\le k, \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) denotes the set of indices of columns that are **not** covered by a vertical line.\n\nLet \\(t:=|C|\\).  To make the left–hand side of (5) as small as possible we choose the rightmost columns, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{n,n-1,\\dots ,n-t+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor this choice  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in C}(n+1-i)=1+2+\\dots +t=\\frac{t(t+1)}2 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore (5) implies  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{t(t+1)}2 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause the remaining \\(n-t\\) columns are covered by vertical lines, we have \\(t\\le n-k\\).  Substituting this bound for \\(t\\) into (7) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2 . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(f(k)=\\dfrac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2\\).  For \\(k\\in[0,n]\\) the function \\(f\\) is strictly decreasing, and a direct verification shows\n\n\\[\nf(0)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>0,\\qquad\nf(n-1)=1,\\qquad\nf(n)=0 .\n\\]\n\nInequality (8) can therefore hold only when \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=n\\).  Since we assumed \\(0<k<n\\), we obtain a contradiction.  Hence no configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe discussion above shows:\n\n* a configuration with **no** sunny line (\\(k=0\\)) exists (vertical lines);\n* a configuration with **all** lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)) exists (the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) defined in (4));\n* any configuration with a number of sunny lines strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is impossible.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the required configuration exists precisely for }k=0\\text{ or }k=n.}\n\\]"
