            "content": "\n\n### RLAC Round 1 of 15 ###\n\n**Current Attack Intensity**: BASIC\n\n**Scoring System**:\n- Critical flaw (counterexample): -10 points\n- Major issue (logical gap): -5 points\n- Minor issue (clarity): -2 points\n- Robust solution (passes attack): +10 points\n\n**Previous Rounds**:\nNo previous attacks (first round)\n\n**Your Goal**: Find flaws the previous rounds missed, or confirm the solution is robust.\n\n\n\n### Attack Intensity: BASIC ###\n\nFocus on obvious flaws:\n1. Check if the base case is handled correctly\n2. Test with small values (n=0, n=1, n=2)\n3. Look for simple algebraic errors\n4. Verify the conclusion matches the problem statement\n\n\n\n### MANDATORY SMALL-CASE CHECK ###\n\nBEFORE providing any verdict on this solution:\n\n1. **STOP** and pick 3-5 small concrete values\n2. **COMPUTE** what the solution predicts for each case\n3. **VERIFY** by direct calculation (not reasoning about the solution)\n4. **REPORT** any discrepancy as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nThis is MANDATORY. Do not skip small-case verification.\n\n**Example for \"Prove sum of first n integers = n(n+1)/2\"**:\n- n=1: Formula gives 1(2)/2=1, actual sum=1 ✓\n- n=2: Formula gives 2(3)/2=3, actual sum=1+2=3 ✓\n- n=3: Formula gives 3(4)/2=6, actual sum=1+2+3=6 ✓\n- n=4: Formula gives 4(5)/2=10, actual sum=1+2+3+4=10 ✓\nResult: ALL_PASS\n\n**If you find a mismatch, this is a COUNTEREXAMPLE - report it immediately.**\n\n\n### Problem ###\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k=n } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   Every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n   All these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=n\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+1-\\frac{i-1}{i}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\); it is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), so every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) are distinct because their slopes are distinct.  \n   For any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) set \\(i:=a+b\\) (note that \\(2\\le i\\le n+1\\)).  \n   Substituting \\(i\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,a+1-\\frac{i-1}{i},\n   \\]\n\n   which is exactly the equation satisfied by \\((a,b)\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  \n   Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all required points.\n\n3. **Impossibility of intermediate values \\(0<k<n\\).**  \n   *Column argument.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   C_i:=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of points in the \\(i\\)-th vertical column; \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\).  \n   A sunny line is not vertical, so it can contain **at most one** point of any column \\(C_i\\).  \n   If a column is not covered by a vertical (non‑sunny) line, then every point of that column must be taken by a distinct sunny line.  \n\n   Let \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of column indices that are **not** covered by a vertical line and put \\(t:=|C|\\).  \n   Ordering the columns so that the rightmost ones are in \\(C\\) minimises the total number of points that must be covered by sunny lines; in that case  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i\\in C}(n+1-i)=1+2+\\dots +t=\\frac{t(t+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because each sunny line can cover at most one point of each column, we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{t(t+1)}2 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(n-t\\) columns are covered by vertical lines, so \\(t\\le n-k\\).  \n   Substituting \\(t\\le n-k\\) into (3) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2 . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Define \\(f(k)=\\dfrac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2\\) for \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   The function \\(f\\) is strictly decreasing on this interval; a direct check shows  \n\n   \\[\n   f(0)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>0,\\qquad\n   f(n-1)=1,\\qquad\n   f(n)=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (4) can therefore hold only when \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=n\\).  \n   Hence no configuration with a number of sunny lines strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) exists.\n\nThe three steps together give a complete solution.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has abscissa \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+1-\\frac{i-1}{i}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*The lines are sunny.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\).  \nSince \\(i\\ge2\\), \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\neq0\\); it is finite, so the line is not vertical; and \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\neq-1\\).  \nHence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*The lines are distinct.*  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\neq\\dfrac{j-1}{j}\\); therefore the lines have different slopes and cannot coincide.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=a+b .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(2\\le i\\le n+1\\), so \\(L_i\\) is defined.  \nSubstituting \\(i=a+b\\) into (6) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{a+b-1}{a+b}\\,x+1-\\frac{a+b-1}{a+b}.\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{a+b-1}{a+b}\\,a+1-\\frac{a+b-1}{a+b}\n   =\\frac{a(a+b-1)+(a+b)-(a+b-1)}{a+b}\n   =\\frac{a^2+ab-a+b}{a+b}=b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  \nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\).\n\nHence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(0<k<n\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nC_i:=\\{(i,b)\\in T_n\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the **\\(i\\)-th column** of points; \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\).\n\nA sunny line is not vertical, therefore it can contain **at most one** point of any column \\(C_i\\).  \nIf a column \\(C_i\\) is not covered by a vertical (non‑sunny) line, then every point of that column must be taken by a distinct sunny line.  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nC:=\\{\\,i\\mid C_i\\text{ is not covered by a vertical line}\\,\\},\\qquad\nt:=|C|.\n\\]\n\nBecause each sunny line can contribute at most one point to each column, we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{i\\in C}|C_i|\n   =\\sum_{i\\in C}(n+1-i). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nTo make the right‑hand side as small as possible we place the columns of \\(C\\) as far to the right as possible, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{n,n-1,\\dots ,n-t+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor this choice  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in C}(n+1-i)=1+2+\\dots +t=\\frac{t(t+1)}2 . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence (7) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{t(t+1)}2 . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n-t\\) columns are covered by vertical lines, so \\(t\\le n-k\\).  \nUsing \\(t\\le n-k\\) in (9) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2 . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(f(k)=\\dfrac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2\\) for \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nA short derivative computation shows that \\(f\\) is strictly decreasing on this interval.  \nEvaluating at the endpoints we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(0)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>0,\\qquad\nf(n-1)=1,\\qquad\nf(n)=0 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, inequality (10) can hold only when \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=n\\).  \nOur assumption \\(0<k<n\\) therefore leads to a contradiction.  \n\nThus no configuration with a number of sunny lines strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* a configuration with **no** sunny line (\\(k=0\\)) exists;\n* a configuration with **all** lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)) exists;\n* any configuration with a number of sunny lines strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is impossible.\n\nTherefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=n } .\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
