refore all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, i.e. \\(k=n\\).\n\n   The case \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) would correspond to a value of \\(t\\) different from \\(0,1,n\\), contradicting (4).  Hence no configuration with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n  \\]\n\n  are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only point of \\(T_n\\) not lying on one of these lines is \\((n,1)\\).  \n  Choose any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  y=\\frac12\\,(x-n)+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  This yields a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n  The slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Put \\(s=a+b\\) (so \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  Choosing \\(i=s-1\\) in (1) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  y=\\frac{s-1}{s}\\,a+\\frac1s\n   =\\frac{(s-1)a+1}{s}\n   =\\frac{sa-b}{s}=b,\n  \\]\n\n  where the last equality uses the relation \\(a+b=s\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{s-1}\\).  Therefore the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where  \n\n\\[\n2\\le k\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nC_i:=\\{(i,b)\\in T_n\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\n\\]\n\n(the points of the \\(i\\)-th vertical column).  Clearly \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\).\n\nA line that is **not** vertical meets each column in at most one point; this holds for horizontal lines, for lines of slope \\(-1\\), and for all sunny lines.  Consequently, if a column \\(C_i\\) is **not** covered by a vertical line, every point of \\(C_i\\) must be supplied by a distinct non‑vertical line.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV:=\\{i\\mid C_i\\text{ is covered by a vertical line}\\},\\qquad\nt:=|\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus V|.\n\\]\n\nThus exactly \\(t\\) columns are not covered by a vertical line; the remaining \\(n-t\\) columns are covered completely by the \\(n-t\\) vertical lines.\n\nThe uncovered columns are the right‑most ones,\n\\[\nC_{n-t+1},C_{n-t+2},\\dots ,C_{n},\n\\]\nand their total number of points equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{t}j=\\frac{t(t+1)}2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery non‑vertical line can meet each of these \\(t\\) columns in at most one point, so a single non‑vertical line contributes at most \\(t\\) points to the set (2).  With \\(n\\) lines altogether we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n\\[\nn\\,t\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{t(t+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t>0\\), division by \\(t\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n2n\\;\\ge\\;t+1 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\le n\\), (3) forces  \n\n\\[\nt\\in\\{0,1,n\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWe now translate the three possibilities for \\(t\\) into statements about \\(k\\).\n\n* **\\(t=0\\).**  All columns are covered by vertical lines; therefore every line of the configuration is vertical, i.e. non‑sunny.