ring a triangle with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\ge 3\\))\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\forall k\\ge 3\\;,\\;S_k\\text{ can be covered by }k\\text{ distinct sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y-1=\\frac{t}{\\,k+1-t\\,}\\bigl(x-(n-k+t)\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{t}{k+1-t}\\); because\n\\(1\\le t\\le k\\) and \\(k\\ge 3\\) this slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or\n\\(-1\\).  Hence every \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct\n(their slopes are distinct).\n\nFix a column \\(x=n-k+i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  Substituting \\(x=n-k+i\\) in\n(5) gives  \n\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,(i-i)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((n-k+i,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Substituting\n\\(y=k-i+1\\) in (5) yields  \n\n\\[\nk-i+1-1=\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\bigl((n-k+i)-(n-k+i)\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((n-k+i,k-i+1)\\) also lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Consequently\neach line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains the two extremal points of the \\(i\\)-th\ncolumn of the triangle.\n\nNow take any point \\((n-k+i,j)\\in S_k\\).  Because the points of a column\nare collinear, the segment joining \\((n-k+i,1)\\) and\n\\((n-k+i,k-i+1)\\) contains every intermediate lattice point of that\ncolumn, and therefore the whole column is contained in the line\n\\(\\ell_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_k\\) belongs to at least one of the\nlines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\nThus \\(S_k\\) is covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.4  Completion of the construction\n\nFor a prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq 2\\) we\nproceed as follows.\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) we use the constructions of §1.1.\n* If \\(k\\ge 3\\) we take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (3) and the \\(k\\) sunny\n  lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) supplied by the Lemma.  \n\nThe two families are disjoint, they consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nevery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them, and exactly \\(k\\) of\nthe lines are sunny.  Hence for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a\nrequired configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a configuration with exactly two sunny\nlines exists.  Let the two sunny lines be \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).\n\nFor any integers \\(i,j\\ge 1\\) with \\(i+j\\le n-1\\) consider the three\npoints  \n\n\\[\nP=(i,j),\\qquad Q=(i,j+1),\\qquad R=(i+1,j). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nTheir pairwise slopes are respectively \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), \\(0\\)\n(horizontal) and \\(-1\\); consequently **no sunny line can contain two of\nthem**.  Hence each of the three points can be covered by at most one of\nthe sunny lines, and at most two of the three points are covered by the\nsunny lines.\n\nThe third point must therefore be covered by a non‑sunny line.  A\nnon‑sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  Such a line passing through the third point necessarily also\npasses through one of the remaining two points of the triple:\n* a vertical line through \\(P\\) also contains \\(Q\\);\n* a horizontal line through \\(P\\) also contains \\(R\\);\n* a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) through \\(Q\\) also contains \\(R\\).\n\nThus the non‑sunny line would cover **two** points of the triple, leaving\nthe third point uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nSince a triple of the form (6) exists for every pair \\((i,j)\\) with\n\\(i+j\\le n-1\\), the contradiction shows that a covering with exactly two\nsunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nCombining the constructions of §1 with the impossibility proved in §2 we\nobtain the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny\nlines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable except \\(k=2\\)."
