ying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, every non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) is possible except \\(k=2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Easy constructions.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* – the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover \\(T_n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* – take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); the single remaining point \\((n,1)\\) is covered by any sunny line through it.\n\n3. **A triangular sub‑problem.**  \n   After using \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) the uncovered points form the right‑angled triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(n-k+i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k-i+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the problem reduces to covering the triangle \\(S_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Lemma (covering a triangle).**  \n   For every integer \\(k\\ge 3\\) the set \\(S_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n   *Construction.*  \n   For \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t:\\; y-1=\\frac{t}{k+1-t}\\,(x-(n-k+t)).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{t}{k+1-t}\\); it is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) because \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) and \\(k\\ge 3\\).  \n   One checks directly that for each column \\(x=n-k+i\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\\) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains the point \\((n-k+i,\\,k-i+1)\\), i.e. the highest point of that column, and also the point \\((n-k+i,1)\\). Consequently every point of \\(S_k\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  \n   The \\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have distinct slopes. Hence \\(S_k\\) is covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   For a prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq 2\\) we take  \n\n   * the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (all non‑sunny);  \n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) given by the Lemma, which cover the remaining set \\(S_k\\).\n\n   The two families are disjoint, they consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of them, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume that a covering with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   Let the two sunny lines be \\(L_1,L_2\\).  \n   Because a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) cannot contain two of the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i,j}=(i,j),\\qquad Q_{i,j}=(i,j+1),\\qquad R_{i,j}=(i+1,j)\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (their pairwise slopes are \\(\\infty,0,-1\\)), each sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  \n   Hence at most two of the three points can be covered by the sunny lines; the third point must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  \n   Any non‑sunny line that passes through the third point necessarily passes through **another** point of the same triple (a vertical line through \\(P_{i,j}\\) also contains \\(Q_{i,j}\\), a horizontal line through \\(P_{i,j}\\) also contains \\(R_{i,j}\\), a diagonal line through \\(Q_{i,j}\\) also contains \\(R_{i,j}\\)).  \n   Consequently that non‑sunny line would already cover two points of the triple, leaving the third point uncovered – a contradiction.  \n\n   The triple \\((i,j),(i,j+1),(i+1,j)\\) exists for every pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i+j\\le n-1\\); therefore a covering with only two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n7. **Extremal values.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* – all vertical lines.  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* – \\(n-1\\) vertical lines plus one sunny line through \\((n,1)\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\)* – the construction of the Lemma with \\(n-k=0\\) gives a covering consisting solely of sunny lines.  \n\n   Together with the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) we obtain the complete list of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) and for \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\n#### 1.1  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n  \\]\n\n  are not sunny and each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line whose\n  abscissa equals its first coordinate.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); the only\n  uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  Any line through \\((n,1)\\) whose slope\n  is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) is sunny, giving a covering with exactly one\n  sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  Reduction to a triangular set\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\) are\ncovered.  The still uncovered points form the set  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(n-k+i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k-i+1\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_k\\) is a right‑angled triangular lattice of side length\n\\(k\\); it contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to covering the triangle\n\\(S_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  Lemma – covering a triangle with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\ge 3\\))\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\forall k\\ge 3\\;,\\;S_k\\text{ can be covered by }k\\text{ distinct sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y-1=\\frac{t}{\\,k+1-t\\,}\\bigl(x-(n-k+t)\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{t}{k+1-t}\\); because\n\\(1\\le t\\le k\\) and \\(k\\ge 3\\) this slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or\n\\(-1\\).  Hence every \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct\n(their slopes are distinct).\n\nFix a column \\(x=n-k+i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  Substituting \\(x=n-k+i\\) in\n(5) gives  \n\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,(i-i)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((n-k+i,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Substituting\n\\(y=k-i+1\\) in (5) yields  \n\n\\[\nk-i+1-1=\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\bigl((n-k+i)-(n-k+i)\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((n-k+i,k-i+1)\\) also lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Consequently\neach line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains the two extremal points of the \\(i\\)-th\ncolumn of the triangle.\n\nNow take any point \\((n-k+i,j)\\in S_k\\).  Because the points of a column\nare collinear, the segment joining \\((n-k+i,1)\\) and\n\\((n-k+i,k-i+1)\\) contains every intermediate lattice point of that\ncolumn, and therefore the whole column is contained in the line\n\\(\\ell_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_k\\) belongs to at least one of the\nlines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\nThus \\(S_k\\) is covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.4  Completion of the construction\n\nFor a prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq 2\\) we\nproceed as follows.\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) we use the constructions of §1.1.\n* If \\(k\\ge 3\\) we take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (3) and the \\(k\\) sunny\n  lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) supplied by the Lemma.  \n\nThe two families are disjoint, they consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nevery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them, and exactly \\(k\\) of\nthe lines are sunny.  Hence for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a\nrequired configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a configuration with exactly two sunny\nlines exists.  Let the two sunny lines be \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).\n\nFor any integers \\(i,j\\ge 1\\) with \\(i+j\\le n-1\\) consider the three\npoints  \n\n\\[\nP=(i,j),\\qquad Q=(i,j+1),\\qquad R=(i+1,j). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nTheir pairwise slopes are respectively \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), \\(0\\)\n(horizontal) and \\(-1\\); consequently **no sunny line can contain two of\nthem**.  Hence each of the three points can be covered by at most one of\nthe sunny lines, and at most two of the three points are covered by the\nsunny lines.\n\nThe third point must therefore be covered by a non‑sunny line.  A\nnon‑sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  Such a line passing through the third point necessarily also\npasses through one of the remaining two points of the triple:\n* a vertical line through \\(P\\) also contains \\(Q\\);\n* a horizontal line through \\(P\\) also contains \\(R\\);\n* a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) through \\(Q\\) also contains \\(R\\).\n\nThus the non‑sunny line would cover **two** points of the triple, leaving\nthe third point uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nSince a triple of the form (6) exists for every pair \\((i,j)\\) with\n\\(i+j\\le n-1\\), the contradiction shows that a covering with exactly two\nsunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nCombining the constructions of §1 with the impossibility proved in §2 we\nobtain the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny\nlines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable except \\(k=2\\).\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
