n to a triangular sub‑problem\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\).\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) are\nalready covered.  The uncovered points are precisely\n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid 1\\le x\\le k,\\;1\\le y\\le k-x+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe right‑angled triangular lattice of size \\(k\\); \\(|S_k|=k(k+1)/2\\).\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **(P\\(_k\\))**  Construct exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover all points of\n> \\(S_k\\).\n\nIf we can solve (P\\(_k\\)) for a given \\(k\\), then the original problem is solved\nfor that \\(k\\) by adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A parity obstruction\n\nFor a sunny line the largest possible number of points of \\(S_k\\) it can contain\nis  \n\n\\[\nm_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, a sunny line has slope different from \\(-1\\); after a suitable\naffine change of coordinates the points of \\(S_k\\) become a set of integer\nlattice points inside a right‑angled triangle of side‑length \\(k\\), and a line\nwith slope different from \\(-1\\) can intersect at most one point in each\nparallel class of the lattice, giving the bound \\(m_k\\).\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nk\\,m_k=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2 & (k\\text{ odd}),\\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{k^{2}}2   & (k\\text{ even}).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|S_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\), the inequality \\(k\\,m_k\\ge|S_k|\\) holds\n*iff* \\(k\\) is odd.  Hence\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even, the triangle \\(S_k\\) cannot be covered by only sunny lines.*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Covering \\(S_k\\) when \\(k\\) is odd\n\nLet \\(k=2r+1\\;(r\\ge1)\\).  For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le 2r\\) define\n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y = x + t .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_t\\) have slope \\(1\\) (allowed, therefore sunny).  \nThe intersection \\(\\ell_t\\cap S_k\\) consists of the points\n\n\\[\n(1,1+t),\\;(2,2+t),\\dots ,\\bigl(k-t,\\,k\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nhence \\(\\ell_t\\) contains exactly \\(r+1\\) points when \\(0\\le t\\le r\\) and\nexactly \\(r\\) points when \\(r<t\\le 2r\\).  The union of the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(\\ell_0,\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{2r}\\) is precisely \\(S_k\\); each point of\n\\(S_k\\) lies on exactly one of them.  Thus (P\\(_k\\)) is solved for every odd\n\\(k\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Covering \\(S_k\\) when \\(k\\) is even (\\(k\\ge4\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be even and put \\(k'=k-1\\) (which is odd).  \nConsider the non‑sunny diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd:\\; x+y = k+2 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(x+y=k+2\\) belong to \\(d\\); after removing \\(d\\)\nthe remaining points of the original triangle form exactly the set\n\\(S_{k'}\\).  By the construction of Section 4, \\(S_{k'}\\) can be covered by\n\\(k'\\) sunny lines.  Adding the diagonal line \\(d\\) we obtain a covering of\nthe whole set \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the diagonal is the only\nnon‑sunny line among the \\(k\\) lines).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Completion of the original problem\n\n* **Odd \\(k\\) (\\(3\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n  Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) and the \\(k\\) sunny\n  lines \\(\\ell_0,\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\) of Section 4 (after translating the\n  coordinates by \\((n-k,0)\\)).  This yields a configuration with exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **Even \\(k\\) (\\(4\\le k\\le n-1\\)).**  \n  Use \\(n-k-1\\) vertical lines, one diagonal line \\(x+y=k+2\\) (non‑sunny) and\n  the \\(k-1\\) sunny lines supplied by Section 4 for the odd number \\(k-1\\).\n  The total number of lines is  \n\n  \\[\n  (n-k-1)+1+(k-1)=n,\n  \\]\n\n  and the number of sunny lines i