\(\\mathcal D_c\\) are the “anti‑diagonals’’ of the lattice.  \nBecause the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is different from \\(-1\\), the function\n\\((x,y)\\mapsto x+y\\) is strictly monotone along \\(\\ell\\); consequently\n\\(\\ell\\) meets each \\(\\mathcal D_c\\) in **at most one** lattice point.\n\nInside \\(S_k\\) the possible values of \\(c=x+y\\) are the integers\n\\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\); there are \\(k\\) such values.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,|\\ell\\cap S_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Parity obstruction\n\nSumming (6) over the \\(k\\) sunny lines that we are allowed to use gives\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\begin{cases}\n=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2 =|S_k| &\\text{if }k\\text{ is odd},\\\\[8pt]\n=\\dfrac{k^{2}}2 <\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2 =|S_k| &\\text{if }k\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(k\\) is even the right‑hand side is strictly smaller than \\(|S_k|\\);\ntherefore **(P\\(_k\\)) is impossible for every even \\(k\\)**.  In particular\n\\(k=2\\) never occurs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=2r+1\\qquad (r\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit two families of sunny lines that together cover the whole\ntriangle \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Upper‑half family (slope \\(+1\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nU_i:\\; y = x + i . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(U_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Lower‑half family (slope \\(-2\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le r-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = -2x + (2r+3+2j). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_j\\) have slope \\(-2\\); consequently they are sunny as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  The family contains exactly \\(k\\) lines  \n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{U_i\\mid 0\\le i\\le r\\}\\cup\\{L_j\\mid 0\\le j\\le r-1\\}\n\\]\n\nhas \\((r+1)+r = 2r+1 = k\\) distinct members.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.4  Every point of \\(S_k\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((x,y)\\in S_k\\).\n\n*If \\(y\\ge x\\).*  \nPut \\(i = y-x\\).  Because \\(x+y\\le k+1 = 2r+2\\) and \\(y\\ge x\\), we have\n\\(0\\le i\\le r\\).  Then \\(y = x+i\\), i.e. \\((x,y)\\) satisfies the equation of\n\\(U_i\\); hence \\((x,y)\\in U_i\\).\n\n*If \\(y< x\\).*  \nWrite \\(x-y = j+1\\) with \\(j\\ge 0\\).  From the inequality \\(x+y\\le 2r+2\\) we obtain\n\\(j\\le r-1\\).  Solving the equation of \\(L_j\\) for \\(y\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = -2x + (2r+3+2j)\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nx+y = -x + (2r+3+2j).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x-y = j+1\\) we have \\(-x = -(y+j+1)\\), and a short substitution shows that\nthe right‑hand side equals \\(x+y\\).  Hence \\((x,y)\\) satisfies the equation of\n\\(L_j\\); therefore \\((x,y)\\in L_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_k\\) belongs to exactly one line of the family\n\\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.5  Summary of the construction\n\nThe \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are distinct, all are sunny, and together they\ncover all points of the triangle \\(S_k\\).  Hence **(P\\(_k\\)) is solvable for every\nodd \\(k\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the original problem\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover all points of\n  \\(T_n\\); none of them is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  The \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) leave only the\n  point \\((n,1)\\) uncovered; any sunny line through this point completes the\n  covering.\n\n* **\\(k\\ge 3\\) odd.**  \n  Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (4); the remaining points form the triangle\n  \\(S_k\\).  Translate each line of the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) (defined in\n  Sections 4.1–4.2) by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).  Translation does not change the\n  slope, so the lines stay sunny.  Together with the vertical lines we obtain a\n  configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n* **\\(k\\) even.**  \n  By Section 3 the triangle \\(S_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n  therefore no configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{and every odd }k\\text{ with }3\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is even the largest admissible odd integer is \\(n-1\\); when \\(n\\) is\nodd the largest admissible integer is \\(n\\) itself.  No other non‑negative\ninteger can occur. ∎"
