he $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nThe answer “\\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)” is still correct, but the existence‑construction given in the previous solution is wrong (the counter‑example for \\(n=3,\\;k=1\\) shows this).  \nBelow I give a *new* construction that works for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n\\]\n\ntogether with a rigorous proof that larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   *Each non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of the set*  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(m\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, then \\(mn\\ge|S_n|\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Longrightarrow k=n-m\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction for any admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Put  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil).\n   \\]\n\n   Use the \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n   \\]\n\n   They cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the uncovered region.*  \n   The points that are **not** covered by the horizontal family are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;\n          1\\le a\\le k,\\;\n          m+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\Bigr\\