al  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   is attainable.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly this\n   interval.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) (equation \\(y=-x+c\\)).  \nIn each case the line meets the integer lattice points of \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\)\npoints:  \n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains the points \\((a,c)\\) with\n  \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) of them;\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points by symmetry;\n* a line \\(y=-x+c\\) contains the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with\n  \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\ge1\\); again at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nHence if \\(m\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, the total number of points of\n\\(S_n\\) that can be covered is at most \\(mn\\).  Because every point of \\(S_n\\) must\nbe covered, we need  \n\n\\[\nmn\\ge|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\Longrightarrow\nm\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k=n-m\\),\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can contain more than\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad\\bigl(m\\ge\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### 2.1  The horizontal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,m\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(m\\) distinct horizontal lines, therefore non‑sunny.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2  Description of the points not covered by the horizontals  \n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) fails to lie on any \\(H_j\\) exactly when \\(b>m\\).\nBecause \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) (the defining inequality of \\(S_n\\)) and \\(m=n-k\\),\nthe condition \\(b>m\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nn-k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the uncovered set is precisely  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;\n          1\\le a\\le k,\\;\n          n-k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(U\\) is a right‑justified triangle of side length \\(k\\); a direct\ncount gives  \n\n\\[\n|U|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### 2.3  The sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-i\\,x = n-k+1-i .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), every \\(L_i\\) is\nsunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct (different slopes).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.4  Every point of \\(U\\) lies on one of the \\(L_i\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in U\\).  Since \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) we can write  \n\n\\[\nb-(n-k+1)=i\\,(a-1)\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nfor a unique integer \\(i\\).  Indeed,\n\\[\n0\\le b-(n-k+1)\\le (n+1-a)-(n-k+1)=k-a,\n\\]\nand \\(a-1\\) ranges from \\(0\\) to \\(k-1\\); therefore the quotient\n\\(i:=\\dfrac{b-(n-k+1)}{a-1}\\) is an integer satisfying \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)\n(the case \\(a=1\\) gives \\(b=n-k+1\\) and any \\(i\\) works; we may choose \\(i=1\\)).\nRe‑arranging (12) yields  \n\n\\[\nb-i\\,a = n-k+1-i ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the equation of the line \\(L_i\\) in (11).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).