ith integer intercepts \\(c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (capacity of a line)\n\n*Any line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nMoving from one lattice point of \\(T_n\\) to another along a straight line changes the sum\n\\(a+b\\) by at least \\(1\\).  Since the smallest possible sum is \\(2\\) and the largest is \\(n+1\\),\nat most \\(n\\) different values of the sum can occur on a single line; consequently the line can\nmeet at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (covering the right‑hand triangle)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \nk=n-s\\ge 1,\n\\qquad \nR_{k}=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge s+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(R_{k}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the columns of \\(R_{k}\\) as \\(a=s+1,s+2,\\dots ,n\\); column \\(a\\) contains exactly\n\\(n+1-a\\) points, and therefore  \n\n\\[\n|R_{k}|=\\sum_{a=s+1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(s+i,1)\\text{ and }(n,i+1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(i+1)-1}{\\,n-(s+i)\\,}\n      =\\frac{i}{k-i},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rational number different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(1\\le i\\le k-1\\) implies\n\\(k-i\\ge 1\\) and \\(i\\neq k-i\\).  Hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The lines are distinct because they\nhave different pairs of intercepts.\n\nFor the last line we take  \n\n\\[\nL_k:\\;(s+k,1)\\text{ and }(n,1).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k=1\\) this line would be horizontal (slope \\(0\\)), which is not sunny.  In the special case\n\\(k=1\\) we replace \\(L_1\\) by the line through \\((s+1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); its slope is\n\\(-\\dfrac{1}{k-1}=-1\\) only when \\(k=2\\), so for \\(k=1\\) the slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{0}\\), i.e. a\nvertical line, which is also non‑sunny; therefore we treat \\(k=1\\) separately by taking the single\nvertical line \\(x=s+1\\) (which is non‑sunny) together with the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=s\\);\nthis yields the already‑known construction for \\(k=1\\).  Hence we may assume \\(k\\ge 2\\), and the\ndefinition of \\(L_k\\) above is valid: its slope is \\(0\\) only when \\(k=n\\) and \\(s=0\\); in that\ncase we replace \\(L_k\\) by the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\), whose slope is\n\\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  Consequently all \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\nNow we verify that the union \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\\) contains every point of \\(R_{k}\\).\nFix a column \\(a=s+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  The points of this column are\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a = k+1-j\\).  For each \\(b\\) we choose the line\n\n\\[\nL_{i}\\quad\\text{with }i=j+b-1\\;( \\le k).\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\nL_{i}\\text{ passes through }(s+i,1)\\text{ and }(n,i+1),\n\\]\nand a simple computation shows that the point \\((s+j,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{j}= \\frac{(i+1)-1}{\\,n-(s+i)\\,}\n            =\\frac{i}{k-i},\n\\qquad\\text{with }i=j+b-1.\n\\]\nThus every point of column \\(a\\) belongs to some \\(L_i\\), and no two points of the same column\nare assigned to the same line (the index \\(i\\) is different because \\(b\\) is different).  Hence all\npoints of \\(R_{k}\\) are covered. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (all‑sunny configuration)\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply Lemma 2 with \\(s=0\\) (so \\(k=n\\)).  The construction described there yields \\(n\\) sunny\nlines covering the whole set \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(s=n-k\\).\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) we take the \\(n\\) v