allest possible sum is \\(2\\) and the largest is \\(n+1\\), at most \\(n\\) different values of the sum can occur on a single line; consequently the line can meet at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (covering the right‑hand triangle)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad \nk=n-s\\ge 1,\n\\qquad \nR_{k}=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge s+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine, for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(s+i,1)\\text{ and }(n,i+1). \\tag{★}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n* the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct;  \n* each \\(L_i\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(\\displaystyle m_i=\\frac{i}{k-i}\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\));  \n* \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i = R_{k}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Distinctness.**  \n   The two defining points of \\(L_i\\) are different from those of \\(L_j\\) when \\(i\\neq j\\); hence the lines are distinct.\n\n2. **Sunny property.**  \n   For \\(1\\le i\\le k-1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{(i+1)-1}{\\,n-(s+i)\\,}\n        =\\frac{i}{k-i},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a rational number different from \\(0\\) (because \\(i>0\\)), different from \\(\\infty\\) (because the denominator \\(k-i>0\\)), and different from \\(-1\\) (because \\(i\\neq k-i\\)).  \n   For \\(i=k\\) the line joins \\((s+k,1)=(n,1)\\) and \\((n,k+1)=(n,n+1-s)\\); its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_k=\\frac{(k+1)-1}{\\,n-(s+k)\\,}\n        =\\frac{k}{0},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the line is vertical.  A vertical line is **non‑sunny**, but this situation occurs only when \\(s=0\\) (the case \\(k=n\\)).  \n   In the special case \\(s=0\\) we replace the last line by the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence for every \\(s\\) we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Coverage of \\(R_{k}\\).**  \n   Fix a column \\(a=s+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \n   In this column the admissible ordinates are  \n\n   \\[\n   b=1,2,\\dots ,k+1-j .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   For a given \\(b\\) set \\(i=j+b-1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \n   We claim that \\((s+j,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  \n   Indeed, the equation of \\(L_i\\) through the two points of (★) is  \n\n   \\[\n   y-1 = \\frac{i}{k-i}\\,(x-(s+i)).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=s+j\\) and \\(i=j+b-1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y-1 = \\frac{j+b-1}{k-(j+b-1)}\\,(j-i)\n        = \\frac{j+b-1}{k-j-b+1}\\,(j-(j+b-1))\n        = b-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(y=b\\), i.e. \\((s+j,b)\\in L_i\\).  \n   Because the index \\(i=j+b-1\\) is different for different values of \\(b\\) (the right‑hand side of (1) is strictly increasing), no two points of the same column are assigned to the same line.  \n   Consequently every point of \\(R_{k}\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (all‑sunny configuration)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply Lemma 2 with \\(s=0\\) (so \\(k=n\\)).  \nThe construction (★) gives the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(i,1)\\text{ and }(n,i+1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-1),\n\\]\ntogether with the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \nAll of them are sunny, they are distinct, and by Lemma 2 they cover \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(s=n-k\\).\n\n* **Case \\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and they clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* **Case \\(k=n\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma 3.\n\n* **Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n\n  1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n     Take the \\(s=n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=s .\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     These lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is at most \\(s\\).\n\n  2. **Sunny part.**  \n     Apply Lemma 2 to the right‑hand triangle \\(R_{k}\\) (the set of points with first coordinate \\(\\ge s+1\\)).  \n     Lemma 2 provides \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(R_{k}\\).\n\n  3. **Disjointness.**  \n     The lines in (2) are vertical, whereas the \\(k\\) lines from Lemma 2 are sunny, i.e. none of them is vertical; consequently all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n  4. **Coverage.**  \n     By construction the vertical lines cover the part of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a\\le s\\), and the sunny lines cover the remaining part \\(R_{k}\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nBecause we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\).  \nThe constructions above show that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎"
