e smallest possible sum is \\(2\\) and the largest is \\(n+1\\), at most \\(n\\) different values of the sum can occur on a single line; consequently the line can meet at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (covering the right‑hand triangle)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad \nk=n-s\\ge 1,\n\\qquad \nR_{k}=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge s+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine, for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(s+i,1)\\text{ and }(n,i+1). \\tag{★}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n* the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct;  \n* each \\(L_i\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(\\displaystyle m_i=\\frac{i}{k-i}\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\));  \n* \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i = R_{k}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Distinctness.**  \n   The two defining points of \\(L_i\\) are different from those of \\(L_j\\) when \\(i\\neq j\\); hence the lines are distinct.\n\n2. **Sunny property.**  \n   For \\(1\\le i\\le k-1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{(i+1)-1}{\\,n-(s+i)\\,}\n        =\\frac{i}{k-i},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a rational number different from \\(0\\) (because \\(i>0\\)), different from \\(\\infty\\) (because the denominator \\(k-i>0\\)), and different from \\(-1\\) (because \\(i\\neq k-i\\)).  \n   For \\(i=k\\) the line joins \\((s+k,1)=(n,1)\\) and \\((n,k+1)=(n,n+1-s)\\); its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_k=\\frac{(k+1)-1}{\\,n-(s+k)\\,}\n        =\\frac{k}{0},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the line is vertical.  A vertical line is **non‑sunny**, but this situation occurs only when \\(s=0\\) (the case \\(k=n\\)).  \n   In the special case \\(s=0\\) we replace the last line by the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence for every \\(s\\) we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Coverage of \\(R_{k}\\).**  \n   Fix a column \\(a=s+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \n   In this column the admissi