 |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.  \nThe three families of non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\},\n\\]\nwith integer constants \\(c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (capacity of a line)\n\n*Any line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) lie on the same line, the value of the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by at least \\(1\\) when we move from one lattice point to the next along that line.  \nSince the smallest possible sum is \\(2\\) and the largest is \\(n+1\\), at most \\(n\\) different values of the sum can occur on a single line; consequently the line can meet at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (covering a right‑hand triangle)\n\nLet integers \\(s\\ge 0\\) and \\(k\\ge 1\\) satisfy \\(s+k=n\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nR_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge s+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let \\(L_i\\) be the line through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(s+i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(n,i+1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n1. the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct;\n2. every \\(L_i\\) is sunny (if \\(s=0\\) the line \\(L_k\\) would be vertical; in that special case we replace \\(L_k\\) by the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\), which is sunny);\n3. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i = R_{k}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Distinctness.**  \n   The two defining points of \\(L_i\\) are different from those of \\(L_j\\) when \\(i\\neq j\\); hence the lines are distinct.\n\n2. **Sunny property.**  \n   For \\(1\\le i\\le k-1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{slope}(L_i)=\\frac{(i+1)-1}{\\,n-(s+i)\\,}\n                     =\\frac{i}{k-i},\n   \\]\n\n   which is never \\(0\\) (because \\(i>0\\)), never \\(\\infty\\) (because \\(k-i>0\\)), and never \\(-1\\) (because \\(i\\neq k-i\\)).  \n   When \\(s=0\\) the line \\(L_k\\) would be vertical; we replace it by the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\), whose slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  Thus every line in the final family is sunny.\n\n3. **Coverage of \\(R_{k}\\).**  \n   Fix a column \\(a=s+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \n   In this column the admissible ordinates are  \n\n   \\[\n   b=1,2,\\dots ,k+1-j .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For a given \\(b\\) set \\(i=j+b-1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \n   Substituting \\(x=s+j\\) and \\(i=j+b-1\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\),\n\n   \\[\n   y-1 = \\frac{i}{k-i}\\,\\bigl(x-(s+i)\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y-1 = \\frac{j+b-1}{k-(j+b-1)}\\,(j-(j+b-1)) = b-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((s+j,b)\\in L_i\\).  Because the index \\(i=j+b-1\\) is different for different values of \\(b\\) (the right‑hand side of (3) is strictly decreasing), no two points of the same column lie on the same line.  Consequently every point of \\(R_{k}\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (all‑sunny configuration)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply Lemma 2 with \\(s=0\\) (so \\(k=n\\)).  \nThe lines given by (2) are  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(i,1)\\text{ and }(n,i+1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-1),\n\\]\n\ntogether with the replacement line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \nAll of them are sunny, they are distinct, and Lemma 2 guarantees that their union equals \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(s=n-k\\).\n\n* **Case \\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and they clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* **Case \\(k=n\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma 3.\n\n* **Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n\n  1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n     Take the \\(s=n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=s .\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     These lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is at most \\(s\\).\n\n  2. **Sunny part.**  \n     Apply Lemma 2 to the right‑hand triangle \\(R_{k}\\) defined in (1).  \n     Lemma 2 provides \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(R_{k}\\).\n\n  3. **Disjointness and coverage.**  \n     The lines in (4) are vertical, whereas the \\(k\\) lines from Lemma 2 are sunny, i.e. none of them is vertical; consequently all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.  \n     By construction the vertical lines cover the part of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le s\\), and the sunny lines cover the remaining part \\(R_{k}\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a configuration **unless** the construction above fails because the family supplied by Lemma 2 contains a vertical line.  \nThat happens precisely when \\(s=0\\) (i.e. \\(k=n\\)), and Lemma 3 shows that the vertical line can be replaced by a sunny one, so the construction works for \\(k=n\\) as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nAssume that \\(n\\) is odd and that a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line exists; equivalently \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n*If the non‑sunny line is vertical or horizontal*, it meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma 1).  \nThe remaining \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nConsider the \\(n\\) points of the hypotenuse  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny two distinct points of \\(H_n\\) determine a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden; consequently each point of \\(H_n\\) requires a distinct sunny line.  \nThus at least \\(n\\) sunny lines are needed – a contradiction.\n\n*If the non‑sunny line is diagonal*, it must be of the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \nThe only diagonal intersecting \\(T_n\\) in more than one point is \\(c=n+1\\); it contains exactly the \\(n\\) points of \\(H_n\\).  \nRemoving this line leaves  \n\n\\[\nT'_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}2\\) points and whose own hypotenuse  \n\n\\[\nH_{n-1}=\\{(i,n-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nagain consists of \\(n-1\\) points, any two of which determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nHence the \\(n-1\\) remaining lines must all be sunny and each must contain a different point of \\(H_{n-1}\\); consequently at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are required, together with the diagonal we would have at least \\(n\\) lines, contradicting the hypothesis that we use exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\nTherefore, when \\(n\\) is odd a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line (i.e. with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines) cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even* the construction of the previous section works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); hence all such \\(k\\) are admissible.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd* the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) **except** \\(k=n-1\\); the impossibility of the latter case has been proved above.  \n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\;\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎"
