\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny** (vertical, horizontal or of the\nform \\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (capacity of a line)\n\nAny line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two points of \\(T_n\\) lie on the same line, the value of the sum\n\\(a+b\\) changes by at least \\(1\\) when we move from one lattice point to\nthe next along that line.  Since the smallest possible sum is \\(2\\) and\nthe largest is \\(n+1\\), at most \\(n\\) different values of the sum can\noccur on a single line; consequently the line can meet at most \\(n\\)\npoints of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (covering a right‑hand triangle with sunny lines)\n\nLet integers \\(s\\ge0\\) and \\(k\\ge1\\) satisfy \\(s+k=n\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nR_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge s+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let \\(L_i\\) be the line through the two\npoints  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(s+i,1),\\qquad Q_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(n,i+1), & i<k,\\\\[1mm]\n(n-1,k), & i=k .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n1. the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct;\n2. every \\(L_i\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{i}{k-i}\\) for\n   \\(i<k\\) and \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k-1}{\\,n-1-(s+k)}\\) for \\(i=k\\); none of\n   these slopes equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\));\n3. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i = R_{k}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. Distinctness follows because the two defining points of \\(L_i\\) are\n   different from those of \\(L_j\\) when \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n2. For \\(i<k\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{slope}(L_i)=\\frac{(i+1)-1}{\\,n-(s+i)}=\\frac{i}{k-i},\n   \\]\n\n   which is never \\(0\\) (since \\(i>0\\)), never \\(\\infty\\) (since\n   \\(k