0,1\\in\\mathcal K_n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  The value \\(k=2\\) is never attainable  \n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\nLet \\(s=n-2\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines.\nReplacing any non‑sunny line by a vertical one can only increase the set of\ncovered points, because a vertical line contains at least as many points of\n\\(T_n\\) as a horizontal or a diagonal non‑sunny line.  Therefore we may assume\nthat the \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(s\\) are now\ncovered.  The uncovered points form a translate of  \n\n\\[\nT_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny two distinct points of \\(T_2\\) lie on a horizontal line, a vertical line\nor a line of slope \\(-1\\); consequently **no sunny line can contain more than\none point of \\(T_2\\)**.  Thus at least three sunny lines are required to cover\nthe three points of \\(T_2\\), contradicting the assumption that only two sunny\nlines are used.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n2\\notin\\mathcal K_n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  A partial construction for \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) and put \\(s=n-k\\).\nKeep the \\(s\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s . \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nAfter translating the picture left by \\(s\\) units, the still uncovered points\nare exactly the set \\(T_k\\).  For several concrete values of \\(k\\) I was able\nto exhibit \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\); for example:\n\n* \\(k=3\\): the three lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: (1,1)\\!-\\!(2,2) \\quad(\\text{slope }1),\\\\\n  L_2 &: (1,2)\\!-\\!(3,1) \\quad(\\text{slope }-1/2),\\\\\n  L_3 &: (1,3)\\!-\\!(2,1) \\quad(\\text{slope }-2)\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  cover all points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n* \\(k=4\\): the four lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: (1,1)\\!-\\!(2,3) \\quad(\\text{slope }2),\\\\\n  L_2 &: (1,2)\\!-\\!(4,1) \\quad(\\text{slope }-1/3),\\\\\n  L_3 &: (1,3)\\!-\\!(2,1) \\quad(\\text{slope }-2),\\\\\n  L_4 &: (1,4)\\!-\\!(2,2) \\quad(\\text{slope }-2)\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  cover all points of \\(T_4\\).\n\n* \\(k=5\\): a similar explicit list of five sunny lines can be written down.\n\nTranslating these sunny lines back by the vector \\((s,0)\\) and adding the\nvertical lines (4.1) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\)\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) for which an explicit covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny\nlines can be exhibited, we obtain \\(k\\in\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the construction of §4 with \\(k=3\\) shows that \\(k=n\\) is possible.\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) the following obstruction was found for \\(k=n\\):\n\nAssume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Since the first column of \\(T_n\\) contains\n\\(n\\) points, each line must contain exactly one of them; consequently every\nline is determined by its second point in a column \\(x\\ge2\\).  Consider the\npoint \\((2,n-1)\\in T_n\\).  If it lies on the line through \\((1,i)\\) then the\nslope equals \\(n-1-i\\).  The slope may not be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore\n\\(i\\neq n-1,n\\).  Hence the point \\((2,n-1)\\) must be paired with an anchor\n\\(i\\le n-2\\).\n\nRepeating the same argument for the points \\((3,n-2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\) forces\nall anchors \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) to be used at least twice, which would make two\ndifferent lines coincide – a contradiction.  Consequently a covering with\n\\(n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nn\\notin\\mathcal K_n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge4,\n\\]\nwhile \\(n\\in\\mathcal K_3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Current status of the answer set  \n\nFrom the results proved above we have\n\n\\[\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n-1\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal K_n\\qquad 