            "content": "\n\n### RLAC Round 12 of 15 ###\n\n**Current Attack Intensity**: ADVANCED\n\n**Scoring System**:\n- Critical flaw (counterexample): -10 points\n- Major issue (logical gap): -5 points\n- Minor issue (clarity): -2 points\n- Robust solution (passes attack): +10 points\n\n**Previous Rounds**:\n  Round 8: SUSPICIOUS (0 counterexamples, 50 penalty)\n  Round 9: SUSPICIOUS (0 counterexamples, 10 penalty)\n  Round 10: SUSPICIOUS (0 counterexamples, 10 penalty)\n\n**Your Goal**: Find flaws the previous rounds missed, or confirm the solution is robust.\n\n\n\n### Attack Intensity: ADVANCED ###\n\nFocus on subtle flaws and deep rigor:\n1. Construct adversarial configurations specifically designed to break the proof\n2. Check for circular reasoning (does step N assume what it's trying to prove?)\n3. Verify that existence claims are constructive (not just asserted)\n4. Check for off-by-one errors in combinatorial arguments\n5. Look for measure zero exceptions or pathological cases\n6. Verify that all quantifiers are in the correct order\n7. Check if the proof generalizes incorrectly\n\n\n\n### MANDATORY SMALL-CASE CHECK ###\n\nBEFORE providing any verdict on this solution:\n\n1. **STOP** and pick 3-5 small concrete values\n2. **COMPUTE** what the solution predicts for each case\n3. **VERIFY** by direct calculation (not reasoning about the solution)\n4. **REPORT** any discrepancy as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nThis is MANDATORY. Do not skip small-case verification.\n\n**Example for \"Prove sum of first n integers = n(n+1)/2\"**:\n- n=1: Formula gives 1(2)/2=1, actual sum=1 ✓\n- n=2: Formula gives 2(3)/2=3, actual sum=1+2=3 ✓\n- n=3: Formula gives 3(4)/2=6, actual sum=1+2+3=6 ✓\n- n=4: Formula gives 4(5)/2=10, actual sum=1+2+3+4=10 ✓\nResult: ALL_PASS\n\n**If you find a mismatch, this is a COUNTEREXAMPLE - report it immediately.**\n\n\n### Problem ###\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal K_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Easy values.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* – the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* – the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with any sunny line through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   After turning all non‑sunny lines into vertical ones (which can only increase the set of covered points) we are left with the three points of a translate of \\(T_{2}\\); a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so at least three sunny lines are needed.\n\n4. **Uniform construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Step 1.* Keep the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (they are non‑sunny).  \n   *Step 2.* After translating the picture left by \\(n-k\\) units the uncovered points are exactly \\(T_k\\).  \n   *Step 3.* Cover \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines as follows.  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=x-(i-1)\\qquad(\\text{slope }1),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the line \\(y=x-(i-1)\\) (all these lines are sunny).  \n   These \\(k-1\\) lines contain every point of \\(T_k\\) except the single point \\((k,1)\\).  \n   Finally add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_k:\\;y-1=2\\,(x-k)\\qquad(\\text{slope }2)\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through \\((k,1)\\).  \n   The \\(k\\) lines are distinct, all are sunny, and together they cover all points of \\(T_k\\).  \n   Adding back the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   Suppose all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Because no line is vertical, each of the \\(n\\) points of the first column  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{(1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   must lie on a different line; denote by \\(L_i\\) the line containing \\((1,i)\\).  \n   The point \\((n,1)\\) cannot belong to \\(L_1\\) (that would be horizontal, slope \\(0\\)) nor to \\(L_n\\) (that would have slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Hence \\((n,1)\\) lies on some \\(L_{i_1}\\) with \\(2\\le i_1\\le n-1\\); the slope of this line is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{i_1}= \\frac{1-i_1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,-1 .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Similarly, the point \\((n-1,2)\\) cannot belong to \\(L_2\\) (horizontal) nor to \\(L_{n-1}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)); it must belong to a line \\(L_{i_2}\\) with \\(i_2\\in\\{1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{2,n-1\\}\\).  \n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{i_2}= \\frac{2-i_2}{\\,n-2\\,}\\neq0,-1 .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Continuing this argument for the whole hypotenuse  \n\n   \\[\n   H=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain a bijection \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that for every \\(a\\)\n\n   \\[\n   m_{\\sigma(a)}=\\frac{\\,n+1-a-\\sigma(a)}{a-1}\\neq0,-1 .\n   \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(a=1\\) the denominator is \\(0\\); the only way to avoid a vertical line is to have \\(\\sigma(1)\\neq1\\).  \n   For \\(a=n\\) the condition \\(m_{\\sigma(n)}\\neq-1\\) forces \\(\\sigma(n)\\neq n\\).  \n   Moreover, (5.3) shows that \\(\\sigma(a)=n+1-a\\) would give slope \\(0\\), which is forbidden; consequently \\(\\sigma\\) must be a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with **no fixed point** and **no element mapped to its complementary index** \\(n+1-a\\).\n\n   Such a permutation exists for \\(n=3\\) (the cycle \\((1\\,2\\,3)\\)), but for every \\(n\\ge4\\) it is impossible:  \n   the set of forbidden positions consists of the \\(n\\) pairs \\((a,n+1-a)\\) together with the \\(n\\) fixed points \\((a,a)\\).  \n   By Hall’s marriage theorem the bipartite graph whose left part is \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the column points) and whose right part is the same set (the hypotenuse points) with an edge \\(a\\leftrightarrow b\\) whenever \\(b\\neq a\\) and \\(b\\neq n+1-a\\) has a matching of size \\(n\\) **iff** \\(n\\le3\\).  \n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) the Hall condition fails for the subset \\(\\{1,2\\}\\): its neighbours are \\(\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\), which has size \\(n-2<2\\).  \n   Hence no such permutation exists, i.e. the required bijection cannot be formed, and a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines is impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n   For \\(n=3\\) the explicit family  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1\n   \\]\n\n   consists of three sunny lines and covers \\(T_3\\); therefore \\(k=n\\) is possible only when \\(n=3\\).\n\n6. **Collecting the results.**  \n   * \\(k=0,1\\) are always attainable.  \n   * \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\) (and also for \\(n=3\\) it is attainable, see the example above).  \n   * For every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) the construction of step 4 works, so these values belong to \\(\\mathcal K_n\\).  \n   * \\(k=n\\) is possible only for \\(n=3\\); for \\(n\\ge4\\) it is ruled out by step 5.\n\nHence the description of \\(\\mathcal K_n\\) given in the boxed formula of the verdict is exact.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\\exists\\text{ a family of }n\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_n\n\\text{ with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n  \\]\n\n  are all non‑sunny and each point of \\(T_n\\) has its first coordinate equal to one of\n  \\(1,\\dots ,n\\); therefore they cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only point of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\).  \n  Choose any sunny line through this point, for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x-(n-1) .\n  \\]\n\n  The obtained family consists of exactly one sunny line.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n0,1\\in\\mathcal K_n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The value \\(k=2\\) (impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\nLet \\(s=n-2\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines.\nReplacing a non‑sunny line by a vertical line can only **increase** the set of covered\npoints, because a vertical line contains at least as many points of \\(T_n\\) as a\nhorizontal or a diagonal non‑sunny line (both intersect the triangle in at most one\npoint, while a vertical line intersects it in at least one point).  \nHence we may suppose that the \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(s\\) are now covered.\nThe uncovered points are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(s+1,1),\\;(s+1,2),\\;(s+2,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a translate of  \n\n\\[\nT_{2}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny two distinct points of \\(T_{2}\\) lie on a horizontal line, a vertical line or a\nline of slope \\(-1\\); consequently **no sunny line can contain more than one point of\n\\(T_{2}\\)**.  Hence at least three sunny lines are required to cover the three\nuncovered points, contradicting the assumption that only two sunny lines are used.\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n2\\notin\\mathcal K_n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge4 .\n\\]\n\n(For \\(n=3\\) the set \\(T_{2}\\) does not appear; indeed \\(k=2\\) is attainable, see\nSection 6.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\ge3\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\ge3\\).  \nConsider the family of \\(k\\) lines\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y = x-(i-1) \\qquad (1\\le i\\le k-1),\\\\[2mm]\nL_k &: y-1 = 2\\,(x-k) .\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n*All lines are sunny*: the first \\(k-1\\) lines have slope \\(1\\); the last line has\nslope \\(2\\).  All slopes differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) we have \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-2\\}\\) then \\(b=a-(a-b)=x-(i-1)\\) with\n\\(i=a-b+1\\) and the point lies on \\(L_i\\) (the line of slope 1).\n\n* If \\(a-b=k-1\\) then necessarily \\((a,b)=(k,1)\\); this point lies on \\(L_k\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines in (3.1), and the\n\\(k\\) lines are distinct.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }k\\ge3\\text{ the set }T_k\\text{ can be covered by exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}.}\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nPut \\(s=n-k\\) and keep the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s . \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(s\\) lines are non‑sunny.  \nAfter translating the picture left by \\(s\\) units the still uncovered points are\nexactly the set \\(T_k\\).  \nCover \\(T_k\\) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines given by (3.1); finally translate those\n\\(k\\) lines back by the vector \\((s,0)\\).  \n\nThe resulting family consists of \\(s\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny\nlines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n-1\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal K_n\\qquad (n\\ge3). }\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\n*Existence for \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1\n\\]\n\ncover \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\); therefore \\(3\\in\\mathcal K_3\\).\n\n*Non‑existence for \\(n\\ge4\\).*  \nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nBecause no line is vertical, each point of the first column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nlies on a different line; denote by \\(L_i\\) the line containing \\((1,i)\\).\n\nConsider the hypotenuse  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(H\\) must belong to some \\(L_i\\).  \nIf \\((a,n+1-a)\\in L_i\\) then the slope of \\(L_i\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{\\,n+1-a-i\\,}{a-1}.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope is forbidden to be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nEquation (5.1) shows that\n\n* \\(m_i=0\\) precisely when \\(a=n+1-i\\);  \n* \\(m_i=-1\\) precisely when \\(i=n\\) and \\(a=n\\).\n\nConsequently a line \\(L_i\\) may contain a point of \\(H\\) only if  \n\n\\[\na\\neq n+1-i\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (i,a)\\neq(n,n).\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nThus we need a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that for every\n\\(a\\)\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(a)\\neq a,\\qquad \\sigma(a)\\neq n+1-a .\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\n(Here \\(\\sigma(a)=i\\) means that the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).)\n\nDefine a bipartite graph \\(G\\) with left part \\(L=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the indices of\nthe column points) and right part \\(R=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the possible indices of the\nhypotenuse points).  Join \\(a\\in L\\) to \\(b\\in R\\) when (5.3) holds, i.e. when\n\\(b\\neq a\\) and \\(b\\neq n+1-a\\).\n\nFor a subset \\(X\\subseteq L\\) let \\(N(X)\\) be its set of neighbours.\nIf a perfect matching existed, Hall’s condition would require \\(|N(X)|\\ge|X|\\) for\nevery \\(X\\).\n\nTake \\(X=\\{1,2\\}\\).  \nIts neighbours are all vertices of \\(R\\) except \\(1,2,n-1,n\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|N(X)|=n-4<|X|=2\\qquad\\text{whenever }n\\ge4 .\n\\]\n\nThus Hall’s condition fails, no perfect matching exists, and a permutation\nsatisfying (5.3) cannot be formed.  Hence a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny\nlines is impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Completion of the description of \\(\\mathcal K_n\\)\n\nCollecting the results obtained in Sections 1–5 we have\n\n* \\(0,1\\in\\mathcal K_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\);\n* \\(2\\notin\\mathcal K_n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\) (and \\(2\\in\\mathcal K_3\\) by the explicit\n  example \\(\\{x=1,\\;y=x,\\;y=2x-1\\}\\));\n* for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have \\(k\\in\\mathcal K_n\\) by the\n  construction of Section 4;\n* \\(k=n\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal K_n\\) only when \\(n=3\\).\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\mathcal K_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThis set is exactly the collection of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there\nexist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
