  A lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nF_n=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |F_n|=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\n* A sunny line is neither horizontal nor vertical; therefore it meets the\n  row \\(y=1\\) in at most one point and the column \\(x=1\\) in at most one\n  point, i.e. it meets \\(F_n\\) in **at most one** point.\n\n* A non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n  In each case it meets the set \\(F_n\\) in **at most two** points:\n  a vertical line meets the column once, a horizontal line meets the row\n  once, and a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) meets the row and the column once each.\n\nLet \\(h\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines and \\(s\\) the number of sunny\nlines.  Since we must use exactly \\(n\\) lines,  \n\n\\[\nh+s=n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nTo cover all points of \\(F_n\\) we need  \n\n\\[\n2h+s\\;\\ge\\;|F_n|=2n-1 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(s=n-h\\) from (1) into (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n2h+(n-h)\\ge 2n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad h\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **any admissible configuration for \\(n\\ge4\\) contains at least\n\\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines**; equivalently, at most one line can be sunny.\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(F_3\\) has only five points, and a covering by sunny\nlines is possible (see §4), so (3) does not apply.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining the bound\n\n#### 2.1  \\(n\\ge 4\\), exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-1\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  Every point of \\(S_n\\) with\nfirst coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  The only point of\n\\(S_n\\) not covered by (4) is \\((n,1)\\).\n\nThe line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-1=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n),\n\\]\n\nhence slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\).  This slope is different from \\(0\\),\n\\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore the line is sunny.  Adding this sunny line\nto the family (4) gives a set of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  Consequently \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every\n\\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n#### 2.2  \\(n\\ge 4\\), no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and obviously cover every point of \\(S_n\\).  Hence\n\\(k=0\\) is also attainable for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n#### 2.3  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe six points of \\(S_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\n*Using only sunny lines* (for instance the three lines\n\\((1,1)-(2,2),\\;(1,2)-(3,1),\\;(1,3)-(2,1)\\)) covers all six points, so the\nminimum possible number of non‑sunny lines is \\(0\\).  By replacing any\nsubset of the three horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2, y=3\\) with the sunny\nlines above we obtain configurations with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) sunny lines.\nHence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le3\\) occurs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (3) we have the universal inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\text{number of non‑sunny lines } \\ge n-1\\qquad (n\\ge4).\n\\]\n\nTherefore the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-(n-1)=1\\qquad (n\\ge4).\n\\]\n\nThe constructions of §§2.1 and 2.2 show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are\nrealizable for every \\(n\\ge4\\).  Hence the admissible set of \\(k\\) for\n\\(n\\ge4\\) is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have exhibited configurations for all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\),\nand no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because we have only three lines\nin total.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\},     & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the non‑ne