------\n### 1.  A lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nF_n=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |F_n|=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\n* A sunny line is neither horizontal nor vertical; therefore it meets the\n  row \\(y=1\\) in at most one point and the column \\(x=1\\) in at most one\n  point, i.e. it meets \\(F_n\\) in **at most one** point.\n\n* A non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n  In each case it meets the set \\(F_n\\) in **at most two** points:\n  a vertical line meets the column once, a horizontal line meets the row\n  once, and a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) meets the row and the column once each.\n\nLet \\(h\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines and \\(s\\) the number of sunny\nlines.  Since we must use exactly \\(n\\) lines,  \n\n\\[\nh+s=n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nTo cover all points of \\(F_n\\) we need  \n\n\\[\n2h+s\\;\\ge\\;|F_n|=2n-1 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(s=n-h\\) from (1) into (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n2h+(n-h)\\ge 2n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad h\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **any admissible configuration for \\(n\\ge4\\) contains at least\n\\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines**; equivalently, at most one line can be sunny.\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(F_3\\) has only five points, and a covering by sunny\nlines is possible (see §4), so (3) does not apply.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining the bound\n\n#### 2.1  \\(n\\ge 4\\), exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-1\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  Every point of \\(S_n\\) with\nfirst coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  The only point of\n\\(S_n\\) not covered by (4) is \\((n,1)\\).\n\nThe line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-1=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n),\n\\]\n\nhence slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\).  This slope is dif