(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   They are all of the form (1) and clearly cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line, \\(n\\ge4\\)).*  \n   Use the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (these are \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines).  The only uncovered point of\n   \\(S_n\\) is \\((n,1)\\).  The line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope\n   \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\); it is not of the three forbidden types, hence it\n   is sunny.  Adding it to (4) yields a configuration with exactly one\n   sunny line.\n\n   *Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) ( \\(n\\ge5\\) ).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   m=n-k\\qquad (2\\le m\\le n-2).\n   \\]\n\n   First place the \\(m\\) vertical non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed\n   \\(m\\).  The remaining points form the triangular set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n,m}= \\{(a,b)\\mid m+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which has side length \\(k\\) (i.e. it contains exactly\n   \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points).\n\n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y- i = -\\frac{i}{k}\\bigl(x-(m+i)\\bigr).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(m+i,i)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad Q_i=(m+k,\\,k-i+1),\n   \\]\n\n   both of which belong to \\(T_{n,m}\\).  Because its slope is\n   \\(-\\dfrac{i}{k}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n   Moreover the collection \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) covers every point of\n   \\(T_{n,m}\\): a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n,m}\\) satisfies \\(a-b=m\\) or\n   \\(a+b=m+k+1\\); in the first case it lies on \\(L_b\\), in the second case\n   it lies on \\(L_{k-b+1}\\).  Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together with the\n   \\(m\\) vertical lines (5) give a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   covering all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Construction for \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The six points of \\(S_3\\) can be covered entirely by three sunny lines,\n   e.g.  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1)-(2,2),\\qquad (1,2)-(3,1),\\qquad (1,3)-(2,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Replacing any of the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\,y=2,\\,y=3\\) by the\n   sunny lines above yields configurations with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n4. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n\n   * For \\(n=3\\) we have exhibited configurations for every\n     \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\); no larger \\(k\\) is possible because only three\n     lines exist.\n\n   * For \\(n=4\\) the lower‑bound argument of step 2 forces at least two\n     non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le1\\).  The constructions of step 3 give\n     both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge5\\) step 2 yields the universal bound \\(k\\le n-2\\);\n     step 3 shows that each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is\n     attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** iff it is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(and is **sunny** otherwise).  \nWe are asked for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, whose union contains every point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nAssume \\(n\\ge5\\) and that among the \\(n\\) lines only one, say \\(L\\), is\nnon‑sunny.  We treat the three possibilities for \\(L\\) separately.\n\n*If \\(L\\) is vertical*, \\(L:x=c\\).  \nConsider the points of the first row of \\(S_n\\) that are not on \\(L\\):\n\\((c+1,1),(c+2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nA line containing two of these points would be horizontal, which is\nforbidden; consequently each of them must belong to a distinct sunny line.\nSince there are at least two such points, at least two sunny lines are\nrequired in addition to \\(L\\).  Hence the total number of lines would be at\nleast \\(1+2>n\\), a contradiction.\n\n*If \\(L\\) is horizontal*, the same argument applied to the first column\n\\((1,c+1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) gives a contradiction.\n\n*If \\(L\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\)*, replace the first row by the set of\npoints \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots\\) that lie on the same diagonal; the same\nreasoning again forces at least two further lines.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge5\\) **at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be\nnon‑sunny**.  For \\(n=4\\) the same reasoning shows that at least two\nnon‑sunny lines are also necessary.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2\\qquad (n\\ge5)}\\tag{2}\n\\]\nand \\(k\\le1\\) for \\(n=4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions\n\n#### 2.1  All lines non‑sunny (\\(k=0\\))\n\nThe vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nare all of the form (1) and clearly cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1,\\;n\\ge4\\))\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand add the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its slope is\n\\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.  The family (4) together\nwith this sunny line contains \\(n\\) distinct lines and covers all points of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) (\\(n\\ge5\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad (2\\le m\\le n-2).\n\\]\n\nFirst place the \\(m\\) vertical non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\) are now\ncovered.  The uncovered points form the triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_{n,m}= \\{(a,b)\\mid m+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has side length \\(k\\) (it contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-i = -\\frac{i}{k}\\bigl(x-(m+i)\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(m+i,i),\\qquad Q_i=(m+k,\\,k-i+1),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(T_{n,m}\\).  Its slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{k}\\) is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  Moreover every point of\n\\(T_{n,m}\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_i\\): a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n,m}\\)\nsatisfies either \\(a-b=m\\) (hence it is \\(P_b\\) and lies on \\(L_b\\)) or\n\\(a+b=m+k+1\\) (hence it is \\(Q_{k-b+1}\\) and lies on \\(L_{k-b+1}\\)).  Thus the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) together with the \\(m\\) vertical\nlines (5) give a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 2.4  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe six points of \\(S_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n(1,1)-(2,2),\\qquad (1,2)-(3,1),\\qquad (1,3)-(2,1)\n\\]\n\ncover all six points.  Replacing any subset of the three horizontal lines\n\\(y=1,\\,y=2,\\,y=3\\) by the sunny lines above yields configurations with\n\\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le3\\) occurs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of all admissible \\(k\\)\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) we have exhibited configurations for every\n  \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\); larger \\(k\\) are impossible because only three lines\n  exist.\n\n* For \\(n=4\\) the lower‑bound argument of §1 forces at least two\n  non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le1\\).  Constructions of §§2.1 and 2.2 give both\n  \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge5\\) the same argument forces at least two non‑sunny\n  lines, whence \\(k\\le n-2\\) (inequality (2)).  The construction of\n  §2.3 shows that each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\},     & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem."
